/* dives-glossary.js — the layered explanations behind the mini dives (dives.js).
   Each term walks in from the shore, one step at a time, and each step goes a little
   more technical than the last, so a reader is eased toward the full (in-depth) page:
     s = SHORE       one warm, instantly relatable line: a moment the reader has lived,
                     then the gist. No jargon. (cheer.js and game-levels.js also show it
                     on its own as a “Did you know?” tip, so it must stand alone.)
     f = TOES        what it means for you and between you and others, in plain words,
                     with a tiny scenario; relationship types where useful.
     w = SHALLOWS    how it works: the proper term or parts, introduced gently, why it
                     matters, and which of the Five Pillars it belongs to.
     x = WAIST DEEP  the technical layer, 2 to 4 short paragraphs split by "\n": the
                     program’s formal framing, the research or theory behind it (named only
                     where well established, with limits, and contested ideas flagged), and
                     any formula or number the program uses (CALC-01, WP-02 bands and so on).
     u + l + lt = THE DEEP  the exact page and #anchor of the matching section of the full
                     version (u), the link’s words (l) and a one-line teaser of what’s there (lt).
   Optional: ctx = a short “Here on this page” line, tying the term to where it was tapped.
     Keys are page paths ('/relationships.html'; a simple page and its -in-depth twin share
     one key), folder prefixes ending in "/" ('/library/'), or sec: { <body data-sec>: '…' }.
   Other fields: t = title, m = phrases to spot in page text (the first one found on a
   page is marked), cs = match case exactly (used for acronyms).
   Keep it accurate: no invented statistics, no health claims. */
window.TOL_DIVES = {

  /* ---------- The book: Preface and Chapters I to V ---------- */

  unbilled: { t: 'Unbilled Debt', m: ['Unbilled Debt'],
    s: 'Ever been the only one who remembered the dentist appointment, and nobody noticed you remembered? That’s unbilled debt: the work of a shared life that nobody sees or thanks.',
    f: 'For you, it’s a tiredness you can’t quite point to. Between people, it’s the gap between what one person does and what the other even knows exists. A partner, a roommate or a sibling caring for a parent can each be carrying it without the others having any idea.',
    w: 'The Preface borrows the word from accounting. A business has “unbilled” work when the job is done but no invoice has gone out yet: real value, not on the books. At home that’s the noticing, remembering and smoothing-over. The fix is not to send a bill. It’s to get both columns onto one page, in the same units, at the same time. This is Pillar I, See the whole load.',
    x: 'Formally, the Preface treats a shared life like a set of books with a missing column. The gap isn’t between two people; it’s between work that was done and work that was ever recorded. Because it was never recorded, it can’t be thanked, shared or planned for.\nThe idea has a research family. Sociologist Arlie Hochschild wrote about “the second shift” of home work in 1989, and Allison Daminger (2019) split the thinking part of household work into four stages: anticipating a need, identifying options, deciding and monitoring. Most of those stages leave no visible trace, which is exactly why they go unbilled.\nIn the program, the missing column gets written in three places: a week of WP-01 (who did what, with a “noticed and handled” column), the Lemonade Stand totals, and the monthly Deficit Audit (WP-04). The numbers are rough on purpose. The aim is a shared picture, not an invoice for the past.',
    u: '/book/preface-in-depth.html#unbilled-debt', l: 'The Preface: unbilled debt', lt: 'The full scene, where the debt comes from, and why the book refuses to send a bill for it.',
    ctx: { '/book/preface.html': 'This is the idea the whole book starts from. Every later chapter is a way of writing this missing column down.', '/about.html': 'The founder names this as the problem fifteen years of audit work kept pointing at: work done, never recorded.', '/infographic.html': 'On this summary it sits under Pillar I: see everything that’s being carried before deciding what’s fair.' } },

  invisible: { t: 'Invisible work', m: ['invisible work', 'unseen work', 'the work nobody sees', 'invisible labor', 'emotional labor', 'mental load'],
    s: 'You know the permission slip, the low milk and the birthday on Saturday before anyone else has finished their coffee? That list in your head is invisible work.',
    f: 'For you, it’s the thinking that never ends because home never closes. Between people, one head holds the list and the other can’t see it, so “you never help” and “you never asked” are both honest. It shows up the same way with partners, roommates, co-parents and coworkers.',
    w: 'Researchers split it into parts: the mental load (noticing, remembering, planning), emotional labor (managing moods so things go smoothly) and the plain tasks nobody logs because they were “quick.” It becomes visible when you write a real week down, compare hours side by side, and thank the specific thing. This is Pillar I, See the whole load.',
    x: 'The term emotional labor comes from Arlie Hochschild’s 1983 book The Managed Heart, first about jobs like flight attendants, where managing feelings is part of the work. People later borrowed it for home life, and “mental load” became the everyday name for the planning side. Allison Daminger’s 2019 study named four stages of this cognitive labor: anticipate, identify, decide, monitor. Her finding that the anticipating and monitoring stages were the least shared matches what the Preface describes.\nA useful caution: these studies mostly looked at different-sex couples, and the words are used loosely online. The program uses them only as descriptions, never as a diagnosis of anyone.\nThe program’s handle on it is practical. WP-01 has a column for “asked for or noticed and handled,” which is where invisible work first shows up. The Lemonade Stand and CALC-01 ask you to count planning, remembering and keeping track as real hours. REPORT-01 even has a finding called “Invisible work surfacing,” for when the job list grows because hidden work finally got named.',
    u: '/book/preface-in-depth.html#kinds-of-work', l: 'The Preface: the kinds of unseen work', lt: 'Each kind of invisible work, and the different way each one hides.',
    ctx: { '/calc01-solvency.html': 'Here it’s an instruction: when you enter hours, count the planning, remembering and keeping track too, or the balance score will look fairer than it is.', '/workpapers/wp-01.html': 'This log is where invisible work first gets a row: the “noticed and handled” column.', '/library/': 'This Library theme gathers the research. The program’s own tools for it are WP-01 and the Lemonade Stand.', '/turning-toward.html': 'Here the angle is thanks: a specific thank-you is the quickest way unseen work becomes seen.', '/relationships.html': 'Between partners it’s usually heaviest. Roommates and coworkers have it too, just with different jobs on the list.', '/book/chapter-4.html': 'Chapter IV’s warning: help that stays silent turns into unseen work, so say out loud when you cover for someone.' } },

  ledger: { t: 'A shared ledger', m: ['shared ledger', 'working ledger', 'a set of books'],
    s: 'Ever argued about who did the school run more, each of you sure of a different number? A shared ledger is one page both of you can read, so the talk starts from the same facts.',
    f: 'For you, it replaces a memory that quietly rounds in your favor. Between people, each person writes only their own side, then you read both together. It works for partners, roommates splitting rent, or siblings sharing care for a parent.',
    w: 'The book borrows accounting words on purpose, because accounting can describe a system without blaming anyone. A ledger that doesn’t balance isn’t evil; it’s information. The goal isn’t a perfect 50/50, it’s a picture you both agree is true. This is Pillar I, See the whole load.',
    x: 'Double-entry bookkeeping, described in print by Luca Pacioli in 1494, rests on one idea: every entry has two sides, and a book only counts as balanced when both sides are recorded. The Preface turns that into a rule for home: each person fills in their own column, and neither writes the other’s.\nThat rule is also a defense against a well-studied bias. People tend to remember their own contributions more readily than their partner’s, simply because they were there for their own. Ross and Sicoly’s 1979 study of couples found each person’s estimated share of chores often added up to more than 100 percent. Writing things down as they happen is the cheapest fix for that.\nIn the program the ledger is a handful of pages: WP-01 (the week’s log), the Lemonade Stand (hours side by side), WP-03 (who owns each job) and REPORT-01 (where the figures sit together over time).',
    u: '/book/preface-in-depth.html#own-side', l: 'The Preface: your side, their side', lt: 'Why each person writes only their own column, and what happens when both are read together.' },

  scorekeeping: { t: 'Not keeping score', m: ['grudge with better formatting', 'scorekeeping', 'keeping score'],
    s: 'Ever kept a quiet mental list of every time you took the trash out, ready for the next argument? That’s scorekeeping. The program asks for the opposite.',
    f: 'For you, scorekeeping feels like protection. Between people, it collects evidence to win, and the other person can feel the case being built. A ledger is shared, both people add to it, and it’s read out loud to start a calmer talk, not to end one.',
    w: 'The Preface puts it plainly: a ledger only one person gets to read isn’t a ledger, it’s a grudge with better formatting. A simple test: if a number is being used to close a conversation (“well, the page says…”), it has slipped into scorekeeping. If it opens one (“what do you make of this?”), it’s doing its job.',
    x: 'Researchers have a name for this line. Margaret Clark and Judson Mills described “exchange” relationships, where people track who owes what, and “communal” ones, where people respond to needs without keeping tabs. Their work found that close relationships tend to feel worse when strict tit-for-tat accounting takes over, which is one reason intense scorekeeping is often a sign that trust is already strained.\nThat can seem to clash with writing things down, but it doesn’t. A ledger records the setup so nobody has to carry a private tally in their head. The Preface’s rules keep it communal: each person writes only their own side, both read it together, and the numbers describe the arrangement, never a person’s worth.\nThe program backs this up in its tools. CALC-01 says “the number says…” is a misuse, and REPORT-01 says it will never contain a comparison between the two of you.',
    u: '/book/preface-in-depth.html#not-scorekeeping', l: 'The Preface: writing it down is not keeping score', lt: 'The grudge-with-better-formatting line in full, and the test for telling the two apart.',
    ctx: { '/book/chapter-4.html': 'Chapter IV adds a twist: two people can both keep score honestly, in two different currencies, and then argue about the exchange rate.', '/check-ins.html': 'Here the advice is short: bring talking notes, and leave the scorekeeping at home.', '/library/': 'This Library theme has the research on exchange and communal relationships behind it.' } },

  radio: { t: 'The Radio Frequency Paradigm', m: ['Radio Frequency Paradigm', 'Radio Frequency'],
    s: 'Ever had a fight that neither of you meant to start, and afterward neither could say what it was about? The book pictures that as two radios slightly out of tune.',
    f: 'For you, it takes the shame out: you weren’t broken, you were off-station. Between people, it gives a no-blame sentence: “I think we’re on different frequencies right now.” It fits a partner, a parent, a coworker or a friend equally well.',
    w: 'Tune two radios close together but not quite matched and you don’t get silence, you get a squeal that neither radio was sending. Chapter I says what sets each person’s “frequency” is mostly pace, register (the tone under the words) and urgency, and that stress shifts all three. It’s a picture, not a measurement. This is Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive.',
    x: 'The physics is real. When two sound or radio waves are close in frequency, they drift in and out of step, and you hear a wobble or whistle at the difference between them. A 440 Hz and a 442 Hz tone together pulse twice a second. Chapter I’s point is the uncomfortable part: the closer two signals are without matching, the more noticeable the interference, which is why two people who mostly agree can still grate.\nThe book is clear that this is a metaphor. Nothing on the site records voices or measures anyone’s “frequency.” It is a way of talking about mismatch as a tuning problem rather than a character flaw.\nThe paradigm runs through the program: retuning (Chapter I), the Tone Filter (WP-09), the 90-second check-in (WP-13, named after a circuit that keeps signals in step) and the Frequency Framework, which applies the same picture to money, decisions and recovery time.',
    u: '/book/chapter-1-in-depth.html#almost', l: 'Chapter I: why nearly in step is the noisy part', lt: 'The radio picture in full, and why close-but-not-matching is where the noise lives.' },

  squeal: { t: 'The squeal', m: ['heterodyne squeal', 'heterodyne', 'squeal'],
    s: 'You know when a chat about the trash turns sharp by the third sentence, and nobody can say when it turned? That’s the squeal.',
    f: 'For you, it’s the moment you replay what you said and it sounds reasonable. Between people, one of you was tired and brief, the other anxious and quick, and the gap made a noise neither of you was making alone.',
    w: 'Engineers call it a heterodyne, or a beat: a third tone that appears when two signals are almost, but not quite, the same. Chapter I uses it for fights nobody meant to start. The words weren’t the problem; the mismatch was. So the answer isn’t deciding who’s at fault. It’s retuning.',
    x: 'In radio engineering, “heterodyning” means mixing two frequencies to produce new ones at their sum and their difference. The difference tone is what you hear as a whistle between two nearby stations. The formula is simple: the beat frequency equals the gap between the two, |f₁ − f₂|. The smaller the gap, the slower and more noticeable the wobble.\nChapter I borrows only the shape of this. In a conversation, “frequency” stands for pace, register and urgency, and the book is careful not to claim anything measurable. What carries over is the key insight: the noise belongs to the pair, not to either person.\nThat’s why the program’s responses are all about the gap. Retuning names the mismatch and slows the pace. WP-09 reshapes your next message. CALC-01 counts, in its apex score, how many rough moments you retuned before answering.',
    u: '/book/chapter-1-in-depth.html#squeal', l: 'Chapter I: the radio whistle', lt: 'The engineering behind the squeal, and why arguing about fault never clears it.' },

  static: { t: 'Static', m: ['relational static', 'static between', 'heavy static'],
    s: 'You say “in a minute,” meaning when I finish this. They hear sixty seconds. Nobody lied, and now you’re arguing about tone instead of the dishes. That’s static.',
    f: 'For you, static is a reminder that being misunderstood isn’t proof you said it wrong. Between people, it lives in the gap: one message leaves on one frequency and lands on another. It happens between friends, family and coworkers just as much as partners.',
    w: 'Nobody is static-free alone. What you can do is know your own signal well enough to say it out loud: your pace, what your silence means, what you need when you’re low. Wired Differently, the Wiring Card and the Signal Translator all help you spot static before it becomes a fight.',
    x: 'Communication researchers have described this since Shannon and Weaver’s 1949 model of communication: a sender, a channel, noise, and a receiver who has to decode the message. Noise isn’t only sound. It’s anything that makes what arrives differ from what was sent, including tiredness, history and different expectations.\nThe program adds a second layer. Even on a clear channel, receivers are built differently. The double empathy problem, named by Damian Milton in 2012, describes how misunderstandings between very differently wired people run in both directions rather than sitting inside one person. The research on it is still developing, and Wired Differently says so.\nPractically, static is handled at both ends: know your own signal (Today’s Weather, the Wiring Card), shape what you send (WP-09, the Signal Translator), and check what arrived (“What did you take from that?”).',
    u: '/how-it-works-in-depth.html#static', l: 'How it works: where the static comes from', lt: 'Why static belongs to the gap between two people, and what each of you can do about your end.',
    ctx: { '/check-ins.html': 'In a check-in, mirroring is the static check: say back what you heard before you answer it.', '/five-pillars.html': 'Static is Pillar IV’s problem: tune how you send and receive, because the noise lives between people.' } },

  channels: { t: 'Pace, register and urgency', m: ['pace, register and urgency', 'pace, register, urgency', 'pace, register or urgency', 'pace, tone and urgency'],
    s: 'One person’s “when you get a chance” means tonight; another’s means sometime this week. Same words, different window. That’s one of three channels quietly slipping.',
    f: 'For you, knowing the three helps you notice which one is off: am I going too fast, sounding sharper than I feel, or treating this as more urgent than it is? Between people, most mismatches happen on just one of them, and naming it shrinks the problem.',
    w: 'Pace is speed and timing: a revved-up person talks faster and reads a normal pause as being ignored. Register is the tone underneath: a plain update can land as an accusation. Urgency is how soon something matters. Chapter I says these three set your “frequency,” and stress shifts all of them without asking.',
    x: 'Linguists would recognize these as parts of prosody (the melody, speed and stress of speech) and pragmatics (what a sentence is used to do, beyond its literal words). Research on emotional prosody shows listeners often weight tone over content when the two disagree, which is why “fine” can mean three different things.\nThe Frequency Framework later widens urgency into timing gaps across whole areas of life, and Wired Differently shows each wiring is more sensitive on some channels: someone with auditory processing differences loses pace and tone first, an anxious listener fills vague urgency with the worst reading.\nThe Carrier Wave Decoder uses exactly these three channels to decode a moment that went sideways, and the Signal Translator flags vague timing (“later,” “whenever”) as a common urgency mismatch.',
    u: '/book/chapter-1-in-depth.html#frequency', l: 'Chapter I: what sets the frequency', lt: 'Each channel with an example, and how stress quietly turns all three up.' },

  retune: { t: 'Retuning', m: ['retuning', 'retune'],
    s: 'Ever noticed a talk turning tense and wished there were a pause button? Retuning is that button: getting back in sync before you carry on.',
    f: 'For you, it starts with one sentence without blame: “I think we’re on different frequencies right now.” Between people, you both slow down: shorter turns, longer pauses, a lower voice. Then you go back to the actual topic.',
    w: 'Chapter I gives four steps: name the mismatch out loud; check your own state (you can’t retune while revved up or shut down); slow the pace; only then return to the topic. The Tone Filter (WP-09) is the written version, and CALC-01 counts how often you do it. This is Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive.',
    x: 'In the Gottman Institute’s research these are called repair attempts: anything that stops negativity from escalating, like a joke, an apology or “let’s slow down.” Their studies found repair attempts matter a great deal, but that whether one works depends heavily on the warmth already in the relationship. That’s one reason the program pairs retuning with the everyday habits in Turning Toward.\nThe program turns retuning into a number, carefully. Retuning frequency is moments you retuned divided by rough moments this week, capped at 1.00. It feeds only the apex score, with a weight of 0.15, because it’s a self-count and easy to overstate. No rough moments means no value at all, not zero: nothing to repair isn’t a failure to repair.\nIf the apex score keeps falling while solvency holds, the setup is fine but repair isn’t happening, and the Tone Filter is the lever to reach for.',
    u: '/book/chapter-1-in-depth.html#retuning', l: 'Chapter I: retuning, step by step', lt: 'The four steps with a worked example, and how to go back in with a sentence that lands.',
    ctx: { '/book/chapter-2.html': 'Here retuning becomes the fourth input to the apex score: of this week’s rough moments, how many you retuned first.', '/calc01-solvency.html': 'On the calculator, retuning is the “rf” tally. It only feeds apex, and it’s blank, not zero, in a week with no rough moments.', '/workpapers/wp-09-tone-filter.html': 'This workpaper is retuning written down: fact, feeling and ask, before you answer.', '/wp-11.html': 'Here it’s a warning: if you retune while still revved up, the fact, feeling and ask all come out sharper than you meant.' } },

  verdict: { t: 'The Epistemic Verdict Engine', m: ['Epistemic Verdict Engine', 'Verdict Engine'],
    s: 'Ever asked yourself “is this working?” and answered with last night’s dishes? The Verdict Engine asks the same question at a calmer scale.',
    f: 'For you, it swaps a mood for a month of notes. Between people, it asks one narrow thing: judging by what you both wrote down, does the way you share the work look like it can last? It works the same for a couple, roommates or a small team.',
    w: 'It reads only three things you both filled in: hours from the Lemonade Stand or WP-01, jobs with named owners from WP-03, and each person’s own stress score from WP-02. It scores the arrangement, never a person. “Epistemic” just means “to do with what we know,” as opposed to what we remember in the heat of the moment.',
    x: 'Epistemology is the branch of philosophy about how we know what we claim to know. The About page says it’s the field the founder returns to most, because in a conflict almost nothing asserted is actual knowledge: it’s memory and inference dressed up as fact. The Verdict Engine is named for that problem.\nChapter II compares it to a small business cash-flow snapshot: it tells you about this month’s operations, not whether the owners are good people. Its output is P(Solvency), and the chapter is candid that the “P” is only a name carried over from the manuscript. It is a weighted sum, not a probability, and it predicts nothing.\nThe formula lives in CALC-01: solvency = balance × 0.40 + ownership × 0.35 + (1 − stress) × 0.25. The weights are a judgment call, not fitted to data, and the calculator lets you change them only with a warning that new reads can’t then be compared with old ones.',
    u: '/book/chapter-2-in-depth.html#names', l: 'Chapter II: what the names mean', lt: 'Epistemic, solvency and P(Solvency) in plain words, then what the check is for.' },

  solvency: { t: 'Is the setup working for everyone? (CALC-01)', m: ['P(Solvency)', 'Solvency Read', 'solvency score', 'Can the load last?', 'CALC-01'],
    s: 'Ever felt a week was “fine” while quietly running on fumes? This read asks whether your current split of work can keep going, as a number from 0 to 1.',
    f: 'For you, it turns a vague worry into three things you can look at. Between people, it gives a calm starting point: 0.70 or more is holding, 0.40 up to 0.70 is drifting, below 0.40 asks for a kind rethink of the setup, never of the person.',
    w: 'It weighs three inputs: workload balance (0.40), ownership clarity (0.35) and how much stress you’re already carrying, turned upside down (0.25). It reads the setup, not anyone’s worth, and it isn’t a compatibility score. If it’s ever used to end a talk (“the number says…”), it’s being misused. This is Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person.',
    x: 'The formula: solvency = balance × 0.40 + ownership × 0.35 + (1 − stress) × 0.25. Each input runs 0 to 1 and the weights add to 1.00, so the score does too. Economists would call this a composite index, like a cost-of-living index: useful for tracking one setup over time, but only as good as the judgment behind the weights. CALC-01 says so plainly: no weight was estimated from real-world data.\nA worked example from the calculator: three roommates, balance 0.60, 6 of 8 jobs owned (0.75), batteries averaging 0.40. That’s 0.24 + 0.2625 + 0.15 = 0.6525, shown as 0.65, drifting. The band is read from the rounded number so what you see and the band always agree.\nSome edge cases are deliberate. No hours gives no balance rather than a perfect 1.00, because an empty week isn’t an even week. If anyone’s battery is missing, there’s no read at all, since nobody’s score is ever worked out for them. The best next step is usually the biggest shortfall bar, not the total.',
    u: '/calc01-solvency.html#spec', l: 'CALC-01: the formulas, weights and bands', lt: 'Every formula, why each weight is where it is, worked examples, and the edge cases defined.',
    ctx: { '/book/chapter-2.html': 'This chapter is where the number is born. The calculator does the sums; the chapter explains why each part is there.', '/workpapers/report-01.html': 'Here the weekly solvency number is one line in your record, read next to apex so the gap between them can speak.', sec: { workpapers: 'Four of the workpapers feed this number: WP-01 hours, WP-03 owners, WP-02 batteries and, for apex, WP-09.' } } },

  inputs: { t: 'The three inputs', m: ['three ledger inputs', 'three inputs'],
    s: 'A bad night can make a whole month feel broken. The three inputs are there so the read comes from notes, not from last night.',
    f: 'For you, each input answers a question you can check: are the hours shared, does each job have an owner, how full is each battery? Between people, each one comes from a worksheet you both filled in, so nobody’s mood gets to be the data.',
    w: 'Workload balance comes from WP-01 or the Lemonade Stand. Ownership clarity comes from the RACI Treaty (WP-03). Stress, called autonomic saturation, comes from each person’s own WP-02. When a read is drifting, ownership is often the first thing to slip, but the calculator says to check rather than assume.',
    x: 'Each input is scored 0 to 1: balance = 1 − moved ÷ most (how much of the hours would have to change hands to hit the agreed split); ownership = jobs with both a Responsible and an Accountable name ÷ all jobs; stress = the average of everyone’s load score, each being their five 0–4 answers ÷ 20.\nThe weights (0.40, 0.35, 0.25) put balance first because an unshared load keeps building whether anyone notices, ownership close behind because unowned work falls to whoever notices first, and stress last because it swings most week to week. Chapter II calls these judgment calls, not findings.\nReading the three separately is the point. A week that feels awful can show fair hours, clear owners and two high batteries. That points to a hard season, not a broken arrangement, and the right lever is rest, not a treaty rewrite.',
    u: '/book/chapter-2-in-depth.html#inputs', l: 'Chapter II: the three inputs', lt: 'Where each number comes from, and what it can and can’t tell you.' },

  balance: { t: 'Workload balance', m: ['workload balance'],
    s: 'Ever been sure the week was even, until you actually added it up? Workload balance is how evenly the hours were really shared.',
    f: 'For you, it’s a check on the memory that rounds in your favor. Between people, an even split scores 1.00 and a fully one-sided week scores 0.00, whichever person carries more. Roommates and families can use an agreed split instead of an even one.',
    w: 'It comes from a week of WP-01 or the Lemonade Stand totals, and it’s the heaviest of the three inputs (0.40), because an unshared load keeps building whether or not anyone notices. Rough hours are fine: the split is what matters. An empty log isn’t a perfect week; it gives no reading.',
    x: 'For two people with an even target, balance = 1 − |pctA − pctB| ÷ 100. A 60/40 week scores 0.80. For 2 to 8 people CALC-01 uses a more general form: moved = ½ × Σ|share − target| and most = 1 − the smallest target share, then balance = 1 − moved ÷ most. Statisticians will recognize “moved” as the total variation distance between two distributions: the smallest fraction of hours that would have to change hands.\nThe calculator’s family-of-five example: 10, 8, 6, 4 and 2 hours, so 6 hours would need to move out of 30 (0.20), and most is 0.80, giving 0.75. An agreed split changes the target, not the math: if two people agree 60/40 and log 6 and 4 hours, balance is 1.00.\nThe honest limit: hours measure time, not intensity. An hour of bedtime with a wired toddler and an hour of folding laundry count the same, which is why the program pairs this number with the battery scores and a conversation.',
    u: '/book/chapter-2-in-depth.html#balance', l: 'Chapter II: workload balance', lt: 'The balance score explained step by step, with examples for two people and for more.' },

  clarity: { t: 'Ownership clarity', m: ['Ownership Clarity Score', 'ownership clarity'],
    s: 'Ever found out you both thought the other was buying the birthday present? Ownership clarity counts how many regular jobs have a clear, agreed owner.',
    f: 'For you, it’s a quick way to see where things fall through. Between people, you count the jobs with both names filled in and divide by all the jobs listed. It works for a household, a friend group planning a trip or a team at work.',
    w: 'It comes from the RACI Treaty (WP-03) and feeds CALC-01 with the second-largest weight (0.35). It measures how clear ownership is, not how fair. Don’t shorten the list to raise it: a real job nobody owns belongs on the list, because that’s the row the score is there to show. This is Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person.',
    x: 'The formula is a plain proportion: ownership = jobs with both a Responsible and an Accountable name ÷ all jobs listed. Ten jobs with seven fully named scores 0.70. A row with only one name counts as not owned.\nThe reason it weighs so much is a well-known pattern in groups. Classic studies by John Darley and Bibb Latané (1968) found people were less likely to act when responsibility was shared among many, a “diffusion of responsibility.” Later real-world work suggests groups often do help, so treat it as a tendency, but at home the tendency is familiar: work that belongs to everyone lands on whoever notices first.\nOne counterintuitive rule: REPORT-01 treats a falling clarity score alongside a growing job list as good news, called “Invisible work surfacing.” The score drops because hidden work got named, which is the score working as it should.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-03-raci-treaty-in-depth.html#clarity-score', l: 'WP-03: the ownership clarity score', lt: 'How the score is worked out, and why a shorter list is the wrong way to raise it.',
    ctx: { '/workpapers/report-01.html': 'Here it’s the OC column. Watch for “Clarity without parity”: clarity 0.85 or more while balance is 0.60 or less.', '/book/chapter-2.html': 'In this chapter it’s the second of three inputs, and the one that most often slips first when a read is drifting.' } },

  apex: { t: 'The apex score', m: ['apex score'],
    s: 'A home can split the chores perfectly and still never make up after a sharp word. The apex score is the read that notices.',
    f: 'For you, it asks a wider question than solvency: is the whole system working, including repair after friction? Between people, the gap between the two scores tells you which lever to reach for this week.',
    w: 'It adds a fourth input, retuning frequency, to the three solvency inputs. Solvency high and apex lower means the setup is fine but friction is being swallowed, not talked through: work on WP-09. Apex high and solvency lower means good talking is holding up a setup that keeps causing friction: work on the job list.',
    x: 'The formula: apex = balance × 0.35 + ownership × 0.30 + (1 − stress) × 0.20 + retuning × 0.15. If there were no rough moments that week, retuning has no value, and apex divides the other three weights by 0.85 so they still add up to 1.00. The page says when it has done this.\nThe two scores count as disagreeing when they differ by 0.08 or more. REPORT-01 has named findings for each direction: “Structure holding, repair not” and “Repair carrying the structure.” If both are below 0.40, CALC-01 suggests that another worksheet may not be what helps most, and that outside support such as a counselor or a trusted mediator is worth considering.\nThe apex score is also what the drift rule watches: a drop of more than 0.10 three weeks running is a cue for a planned review, not an emergency.',
    u: '/book/chapter-2-in-depth.html#apex', l: 'Chapter II: the apex score', lt: 'The fourth input, the new weights, and how to read the two scores together.' },

  rf: { t: 'Retuning frequency', m: ['Retuning Frequency'],
    s: 'Five moments stung this week. In two of them, somebody said “give me a second” first. That two out of five is your retuning frequency.',
    f: 'For you, it’s a record of what you did about friction, not how much there was. Between people, you count the moments something landed badly, then how many you paused and ran through the Tone Filter before answering.',
    w: 'It’s the one input about repair rather than load. No rough moments means no reading at all, not zero, because nothing to repair isn’t a failure to repair. It only feeds the apex score, with a small weight, because it’s the easiest tally to inflate.',
    x: 'The formula: retuning = moments retuned ÷ rough moments, capped at 1.00. If you somehow count more retunes than rough moments, the calculator stops at 1.00, since more repairs than problems means the tally is counting something else.\nIts weight is 0.15, and only in apex. CALC-01 explains: it’s a simple self-count, and self-counts are easy to overstate, especially when you want the number to look good. Psychologists call this social desirability bias, and the honest fix is small weights plus steady counting.\nOver time the tally is less about the number than the direction. Chapter I suggests watching whether repair is happening more often month to month, and REPORT-01 reads it next to the friction you logged in the daily check-in.',
    u: '/calc01-solvency.html#rf', l: 'CALC-01: the retuning tally', lt: 'How to count a rough moment and a retune, with the edge cases spelled out.' },

  bands: { t: 'Reading the bands', m: ['Interpretation bands'],
    s: 'A 0.69 and a 0.70 are the same week. The bands are rough zones that point to a first move, not a pass or fail.',
    f: 'For you, the band tells you what to do next. Between people, it keeps the talk on the setup: 0.70 and up, keep your rhythm; 0.40 up to 0.70, look at the biggest shortfall; below 0.40, rework the whole job list together.',
    w: 'The edges are round numbers chosen to be easy to read. Don’t let a decimal point carry a decision. Arrangements get renegotiated all the time, and a low band says something about the setup, never about anyone’s love or worth.',
    x: 'CALC-01’s three bands and their first moves: 0.70 or more, holding (the setup is carrying its own weight; don’t change it, keep the same check-in rhythm); 0.40 up to 0.70, drifting (start with the biggest shortfall, often ownership, but check rather than assume); below 0.40, time for a kind rethink (rework the whole job list, not another one-off patch).\nThe band is read from the score as shown, rounded to two decimals, so the number and the band always agree. Measurement people would say the cut-offs have no empirical calibration, and CALC-01 agrees: they’re there to make the number useful, not to claim a threshold where something changes.\nThe bands describe one week’s workload setup. They say nothing about whether a relationship is good or worth continuing.',
    u: '/calc01-solvency.html#spec', l: 'CALC-01: the bands', lt: 'Each band’s first move, and how to read solvency and apex side by side.' },

  driftrule: { t: 'The drift rule', m: ['drift rule', 'treaty review'],
    s: 'One bad week is just a week. The drift rule waits for a pattern: three drops in a row.',
    f: 'For you, it’s permission not to panic about a dip. Between people, when the rule is met the response is calm and practical: look at the job list together and write in what has changed.',
    w: 'Specifically: the apex score drops by more than 0.10 three weeks in a row. The trend log on the CALC-01 page and REPORT-01 watch for it. The answer is a planned treaty review, not a crisis talk.',
    x: 'Factories use a similar idea called statistical process control, with “run rules” such as the Western Electric rules from the 1950s: a single odd point is noise, but several points moving the same way suggest something real has changed. The drift rule is a home-sized version: three consecutive drops, each bigger than 0.10.\nIt watches apex rather than solvency because apex includes repair, so it’s the wider signal. A typical cause is quiet: a new job, a school pick-up that moved to one person and was never written down.\nThe practical catch: CALC-01 doesn’t save anything unless you switch on keeping it in this browser, so a trend needs either that, a printed log, or REPORT-01’s saved ledger file.',
    u: '/calc01-solvency.html#trend', l: 'CALC-01: the trend log', lt: 'Where the drift rule is watched, and how to keep a trend without anything being uploaded.' },

  saturation: { t: 'Autonomic Saturation', m: ['Autonomic Saturation', 'autonomic load'],
    s: 'Ever snapped at a perfectly nice question at 5:15 on a Thursday? Your tank was already full. That’s autonomic saturation.',
    f: 'For you, it separates “I’m upset about the dishes” from “I’m exhausted and the dishes are what I noticed.” Between people, a full tank explains why a small question got a big reaction, without excusing it: a kind repair is still part of the job.',
    w: '“Autonomic” refers to the part of your nervous system that runs things without you deciding: heart rate, breathing, how alert or braced you are. Saturation is how much of today’s stress was already there before this moment. WP-02 turns it into a number, and it’s one of the three CALC-01 inputs. This is Pillar III, Read your state first.',
    x: 'Researchers have a related idea called allostatic load, introduced by Bruce McEwen and Eliot Stellar in 1993: the wear that builds up when the body keeps adjusting to repeated demands. The program borrows the everyday half of it, leftover stress lowering your threshold, and makes no health claim.\nIn numbers: each person scores five factors 0 to 4 (sleep debt, workload elsewhere, recent unresolved conflict, physical state, time pressure today), adds them and divides by 20. The household’s stress input is the average of everyone’s. It enters the solvency sum turned upside down, as 1 − stress, with the smallest weight (0.25), because it swings the most week to week.\nChapter III’s rule of thumb: a high reading means anything decided in the next ten minutes should probably be revisited later. It’s a timing signal, not an excuse and not a diagnosis.',
    u: '/book/chapter-3-in-depth.html#saturation', l: 'Chapter III: autonomic saturation', lt: 'The full-tank idea, leftover stress versus this-moment stress, and what a high reading asks of you.',
    ctx: { '/book/chapter-2.html': 'Here it appears as the stress input, entered as 1 − stress with the smallest weight, 0.25.', '/workpapers/report-01.html': 'Here it’s the AS column. If it rises three weeks running while balance stays flat, the report says load isn’t the driver.', '/how-it-works.html': 'This page’s angle: stress left over from earlier lowers your threshold, so the reaction can be bigger than its cause.' } },

  baseline: { t: 'Baseline stress', m: ['baseline stress'],
    s: 'A hard day at work, a bad night’s sleep, a money worry: you carry it through the door before the household even comes up. That’s baseline stress.',
    f: 'For you, naming it stops you blaming the chores for a feeling that started at work. Between people, it protects both of you: “I’m exhausted” doesn’t have to sound like “you’re too much.” A parent caring for their own parent, a roommate in exam season, a coworker mid-move all carry it.',
    w: 'The program keeps it separate from workload so one doesn’t get blamed for the other. It enters CALC-01 through WP-02, with the lowest weight, because a single bad-sleep week shouldn’t dominate the read. How It Works names the big transitions that raise it: a new baby, a job change, a move, an illness, caring for a parent, grief.',
    x: 'Relationship researchers call this stress spillover. Studies by Lisa Neff and Benjamin Karney, among others, found that stress from outside a relationship, like work or money pressure, tends to show up inside it as more negative reactions and less generous readings of a partner. The stress didn’t start at home, but home is often where it lands.\nThat’s the logic behind several program rules. WP-02’s factors include workload elsewhere and recent conflict from any source, not just home. REPORT-01’s “Load isn’t the driver” finding fires when stress rises for three or more weeks while the chore split stays flat, pointing outside the household.\nTurning Toward’s stress-reducing conversation is the social half: a short, regular talk about outside stress where the listener is on the speaker’s side, so it has somewhere to go other than irritability.',
    u: '/book/chapter-2-in-depth.html#stress', l: 'Chapter II: the stress score', lt: 'Why stress counts at all, and why it has the smallest weight of the three.' },

  selfreport: { t: 'Self-report', m: ['self-report', 'self-rated', 'gut-check'],
    s: 'Only you know how tired you actually are. Self-report means you fill it in about yourself, and nobody fills it in for you.',
    f: 'For you, it’s a chance to say what’s true without being argued with. Between people, you can ask about someone’s answer and listen to it, but you don’t get to correct it. That holds for partners, parents and teenagers, and coworkers alike.',
    w: 'That rule keeps a reflection tool from turning into a surveillance tool. No self-report is perfectly neutral, since your mood comes along with it. What makes it trustworthy enough to act on is repetition: the same questions, week after week, with earlier answers beside today’s.',
    x: 'Researchers rely on self-report constantly and know its limits well. Mood colors memory (mood-congruent recall), people shade answers toward what looks good (social desirability), and a single answer can swing a lot. The standard fix is to ask the same questions the same way and look at the pattern, which is what psychometricians call test–retest consistency.\nThe program applies that directly. WP-02 always uses the same five factors on the same 0–4 scale, and the Calm-Down Kit deliberately reuses them, so every reading can be compared with every other. How It Works puts it simply: one entry is a mood; twelve entries are a pattern.\nThe hard rule sits in the math too: CALC-01 won’t make a read if anyone’s battery is missing, because a score is never worked out from some people’s answers standing in for everyone’s.',
    u: '/how-it-works-in-depth.html#honest-note', l: 'How it works: an honest note', lt: 'What self-report can and can’t tell you, and why repetition is what makes it useful.',
    ctx: { '/workpapers/wp-02-battery-stress-meter.html': 'Here it’s the meter’s first rule: score only yourself, for how the last day or two actually went.', '/book/chapter-3.html': 'Both of this chapter’s tools are self-report: each person fills them in about themselves, never the other.', '/wired-differently.html': 'Here it appears in a study note: some figures come only from people able to describe their own experience, which is a real limit.' } },

  surveillance: { t: 'Reflection, not surveillance', m: ['surveillance tool'],
    s: 'Ever been tempted to fill in someone else’s stress score “to save time”? That’s the moment a reflection tool turns into a surveillance tool.',
    f: 'For you, these tools are a mirror. Between people, each person fills in their own column and hands the page over with “only you know this bit.” Guessing how someone “really” feels becomes a question you ask them instead.',
    w: 'CALC-01 refuses to give a read from one person’s inputs alone. Nothing on the site takes tone of voice, recordings, message sentiment, reply times, or anything about a person they didn’t enter themselves as an input.',
    x: 'Chapter III draws the line: both of its tools are self-report, filled in by the person they describe. The design follows a principle privacy researchers call data minimization: collect only what’s needed, from the person it’s about, and keep it only as long as it’s useful.\nThe tools back this with defaults. CALC-01 keeps nothing unless you choose to keep it in this browser, and warns that anyone using the same browser could see it. REPORT-01 saves to a file you hold rather than the browser, because a household’s records shouldn’t sit on a computer two people share.\nThe list of things the program will never use is explicit: tone of voice, recordings, the mood of messages, how fast someone replies. Those are exactly the signals that make monitoring feel like care while working like control.',
    u: '/book/chapter-3-in-depth.html#together', l: 'Chapter III: using both tools together', lt: 'Why both tools are self-report only, and how to say your reading instead of making each other guess.' },

  vectors: { t: 'The 7 Ocular Vectors', m: ['7 Ocular Vectors', 'Ocular Vectors', 'seven angles', 'seven vantage points', 'vantage point'],
    s: 'One of you sees a pile of mail as a to-do list you don’t have time for; the other sees the fourth week in a row. Same pile, different angle. The book names seven such angles.',
    f: 'For you, picking your angle before a talk explains your reaction to yourself. Between people, saying it out loud (“I’m looking at this through time pressure”) stops you both guessing. It works for family, friends and coworkers too.',
    w: 'The seven: physical tiredness, time pressure, history (has this come up before?), family script, sense of fairness, communication style, and outside stress. Saturation asks how much charge is in the room; the vectors ask what each person thinks the room is about.',
    x: '“Ocular” means “to do with the eye” and a “vector” is a direction, so the name just means the angles you might be looking from. Psychologist Lee Ross described the underlying trap as naive realism: the sense that we see the world as it is, so anyone who disagrees must be uninformed, biased or unreasonable. The vectors are a checklist for catching it.\nChapter III pairs them with the old parable of the Blind Men and the Elephant, where each man describes one part honestly and they argue because each has only part of the truth. Both views can be true at once.\nThe book suggests using the vectors before a talk rather than during one. The Carrier Wave Decoder and the Signal Translator build the same idea in: each person says where they’re listening from before anyone decides what happened.',
    u: '/book/chapter-3-in-depth.html#vectors', l: 'Chapter III: the 7 Ocular Vectors', lt: 'All seven angles, each with a question to ask yourself, then how two true views fit together.',
    ctx: { '/library/thinking.html': 'On this page they’re set against a thinking trap: assuming your own view is simply reality.', '/know-yourself.html': 'Here they help sort a reaction: which angle is today’s condition, and which is older history?' } },

  familyscript: { t: 'Family script', m: ['Family script'],
    s: 'If dishes were always done straight after dinner growing up, a sink left overnight can feel wrong before you’ve even thought about it. That’s a family script.',
    f: 'For you, it explains reactions that arrive faster than reasons. Between people, two scripts meet: one home took the tree down on New Year’s Day, the other in mid-January. Neither person is being difficult. It shows up with partners, in-laws, roommates and co-parents.',
    w: 'It’s one of Chapter III’s seven angles. It isn’t right or wrong, just an angle. Naming it (“in my house, this was always done by nine”) turns a clash about character back into a clash about habits, and habits can be chosen together.',
    x: 'Family therapist John Byng-Hall used the term “family scripts” for the shared expectations about how family members behave in given situations, passed down and often re-enacted without anyone deciding to. His idea was that scripts can be noticed, then kept, edited or rewritten.\nThat fits the program’s layers in Know Your Own Wiring: wiring (lasting), shaped patterns (learned, and often changeable) and today’s conditions (passing). A family script is a shaped pattern. It feels as automatic as wiring, but it was learned.\nThe practical move is to say scripts before a job goes into the RACI Treaty. Two different pictures of “done” are a common hidden cause of a job that keeps slipping, and the Deficit Audit would otherwise log it as a structural gap month after month.',
    u: '/book/chapter-3-in-depth.html#vectors', l: 'Chapter III: the seven angles', lt: 'Family script alongside the other six, with a question for each.' },

  parity: { t: 'Deontological Parity', m: ['Deontological Parity', 'two kinds of fair'],
    s: 'The recycling is your roommate’s job, but you’ve quietly taken it out three weeks running. Did it “get done”? Parity says the question is whether the owner did it.',
    f: 'For you, it means your covering is seen, not used as proof the gap doesn’t matter. Between people, it turns “it got done, so it’s fine” into “this one keeps sliding; do we need a different owner or a different time?”',
    w: 'Once a job has a named owner in the RACI Treaty (WP-03), you judge it by one thing: did the owner do their part? Covering is kind, and it’s thanked, but it doesn’t count as the owner’s job being done. It needs a named owner first. “Deontological” means judging by duties rather than outcomes; “parity” means equal standing.',
    x: 'Philosophers split ethics into families. Consequentialism, associated with Jeremy Bentham and John Stuart Mill, judges actions by their results. Deontology, most associated with Immanuel Kant, judges by duties and promises kept. Chapter IV applies that split to chores: most chore arguments are fairness arguments “wearing a dish towel,” with each person quietly using a different kind of fair.\nThe book’s agreement is to use the duty-based kind for owned jobs. The About page adds a second meaning of parity: each person’s needs carry equal weight as a starting point, not something earned by doing more.\nIn the numbers, parity is why ownership clarity is its own input rather than folded into balance, and why WP-04 sorts repeats into a structural gap, a capacity issue or a one-off instead of simply counting who covered.',
    u: '/book/chapter-4-in-depth.html#owner', l: 'Chapter IV: the agreement, look to the owner', lt: 'The parity agreement in full, and how it protects the person who keeps filling the gaps.',
    ctx: { '/about.html': 'The founder calls this the formal version of a lifelong question: fairness based on duties and principles, not on who happened to cover.' } },

  twofair: { t: 'Outcome-based or duty-based fair', m: ['Outcome-based', 'Duty-based', 'consequentialist'],
    s: '“It all got done, who cares who did it?” versus “You said you’d handle the trash.” Both sound fair, and they’re two different kinds of fair.',
    f: 'For you, knowing which one you use explains why some weeks sting. Between people, friction shows up when each uses a different one without saying so. Ask each other: when a job gets done by the wrong person, does that feel fine, or does it bother you?',
    w: 'Outcome-based fair is flexible and suits busy, changing weeks, but it can hide a pattern where the same person always steps in. Duty-based fair is clear and protects the gap-filler, but it can feel rigid in a week when someone’s parent is in hospital. Many homes use both: duties for owned jobs, outcomes for the rest.',
    x: 'In moral philosophy these are consequentialist and deontological ethics. Social psychologists studying fairness add a third lens, procedural justice (Tom Tyler and others): people accept an outcome more readily when the way it was decided felt fair and they had a voice. The program leans on that too, which is why the treaty changes only through an agreed amendment.\nChapter IV compares choosing between them to a contract naming its governing law. Neither is wrong, but mixing them silently is where the friction comes from: one person counts hours, the other counts who noticed the job needed doing, and they argue about the exchange rate.\nThe book’s choice for owned jobs is duty-based, called deontological parity. That choice only works once each job has a named owner, which is why WP-03 comes before any fairness talk.',
    u: '/book/chapter-4-in-depth.html#outcomes', l: 'Chapter IV: judging by results, and by promises', lt: 'What each kind of fair is good at, where each goes wrong, and how to agree which you’ll use.' },

  gating: { t: 'Sensory Gating', m: ['Sensory Gating'],
    s: '“Did you move my keys?” can land like an accusation after a long day. Sensory gating is letting a comment land before you react to it.',
    f: 'For you, it’s a pause: “give me a second.” Between people, it separates “this job wasn’t done” (a fact) from “this landed as an attack” (a reaction). A minute later the keys are found and nothing needed defending.',
    w: 'Chapter IV’s three steps: notice it landed harder than its content deserved; name that out loud; decide once you’ve settled whether it needs an answer now, later or not at all. It’s about timing, not bottling things up. The room counts too: light, noise and scent change how much either person can take in.',
    x: 'The term is borrowed loosely from neuroscience, where sensory gating describes the brain turning down repeated or low-priority input so attention isn’t swamped. Labs measure it with paired clicks: the brain’s response to the second click (the P50 wave) is normally smaller than to the first. The book uses the idea as a habit, not as a clinical claim.\nThe practical link is state. When you’re revved up or shutting down, a neutral comment can arrive at full volume and seem to demand an answer right now. Gating buys the few seconds that Chapter III’s battery check and Chapter I’s retuning need.\nThe environmental side shows up across the program: Check-ins asks for a room quiet enough that a sensitive nervous system isn’t spending its budget on the lights, and the Calm-Down Kit lists “gating” as its environmental move: lower the lights, leave the noisy room, step outside.',
    u: '/book/chapter-4-in-depth.html#gating-steps', l: 'Chapter IV: notice, name, decide', lt: 'The three steps with an example, and how to keep facts and feelings as two separate questions.',
    ctx: { '/book/chapter-4.html': 'In this chapter gating is the second half of the idea: parity is about duties, gating is about timing your reaction.', '/program-overview.html': 'The overview’s angle: gating covers what gets filtered out before it reaches attention, including the room itself.', '/about.html': 'The founder’s point here: the setting of a talk isn’t neutral background. Light, noise and scent change what gets through.' } },

  deficit: { t: 'The Deficit Audit (WP-04)', m: ['Unbilled Deficit Audit', 'Deficit Audit', 'monthly look-back', 'monthly reconciliation'],
    s: '“The trash again,” for the fourth Tuesday running. A job missed once is an event; missed four weeks in a row, it’s a pattern. The Deficit Audit is the monthly look that catches it.',
    f: 'For you, it swaps “why does this keep happening to me?” for “what is it about Tuesdays?” Between people, you look at the month’s notes together and fix the setup, not the person. It works for households, shared flats and small teams.',
    w: 'Three parts: gather every job that slipped in the four weekly logs; sort each repeat into a structural gap, a capacity issue or a one-off; then close the month by fixing the structure and counting. It isn’t a performance review and it never bills backward for past hours. This is Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives.',
    x: 'The shape comes from month-end reconciliation in accounting and from root-cause analysis in quality work, where the question moves from “who made the mistake?” to “what in the system let it happen?” Toyota’s “five whys” is the best-known version of that habit.\nThe numbers are simple. A job slipped if it was noticed but not done, or only got done after being raised more than once. One tick in a month is normal life, two is worth a look, three or four is a real pattern. Each job with two or more ticks gets sorted.\nThe outputs feed REPORT-01: the count of structural gaps, and the recurrence rate (how many fixed gaps came back). A falling count is the clearest sign things are working; more than half coming back triggers the “Fixes aren’t holding” finding.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-04-deficit-audit-in-depth.html#part-a', l: 'WP-04: gather the month’s notes', lt: 'The whole audit, part by part: gather, sort, and close the month with a count.',
    ctx: { '/book/chapter-5.html': 'This chapter is the audit’s reasoning; WP-04 is the worksheet you actually fill in.', '/workpapers/report-01.html': 'Once a month its two figures go here: structural gaps found, and how many past fixes came back.' } },

  structgap: { t: 'A structural gap', m: ['structural gap'],
    s: 'The treaty says one person handles the car, but the other always books the service because they see the warning light first. That’s a structural gap.',
    f: 'For you, it’s a relief: the problem isn’t effort. Between people, either a job has no clear owner, or the named owner isn’t the one doing it. The table and real life have drifted apart, and nobody has to be at fault for that.',
    w: 'It’s one of the Deficit Audit’s three kinds of repeat problem. The fix is to write a real owner into the RACI Treaty in the same session, not to try harder. Gaps left open cost the most, because the same job gets argued over from scratch every time it comes back.',
    x: 'Systems thinkers have a saying often attributed to W. Edwards Deming: most problems come from the system, not the individual. A structural gap is that idea at home: when a job keeps slipping regardless of who’s trying, the setup is producing the result.\nWP-04’s fix is precise. Update the treaty that session: who is Responsible, who is Accountable, and whether the timing needs to change. Chapter V adds that a falling count of structural gaps month over month is the clearest sign the framework is working, and a sudden rise may mean something bigger changed.\nREPORT-01 then watches whether each fix holds. If more than half of fixed gaps come back across the recorded months, the finding “Fixes aren’t holding” says the owner, the timing or the job itself needs another look.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-04-deficit-audit-in-depth.html#part-b', l: 'WP-04: sort each gap', lt: 'The three kinds of gap side by side, with the fix for each.' },

  capacityissue: { t: 'A capacity issue', m: ['capacity issue', 'capacity conversation'],
    s: 'Cooking is yours, but a new job means you’re home late four nights a week. The job has an owner; the owner just can’t keep up. That’s a capacity issue.',
    f: 'For you, the worst fix is a silent promise to try harder. Between people, the fix is a direct, kind conversation: “I can’t keep weeknight dinners right now. Can I take weekends and the Sunday shop instead?”',
    w: 'It’s the second kind of repeat problem in the Deficit Audit. The audit only brings it to light; the talk still has to happen. Use a neutral refusal or a handover (WP-01, WP-09), and hold the talk as a check-in with a named time.',
    x: 'The About page puts the lesson bluntly: willpower collapses exactly when you need it most. Research on decision fatigue and on scarcity backs the general point. Sendhil Mullainathan and Eldar Shafir’s work on scarcity found that when people are stretched, their mental bandwidth narrows and planning gets harder. A plan that depends on the stretched person “just trying harder” is designed to fail.\nSo WP-04 treats capacity as information, not a moral issue. The neutral refusal’s three moves (acknowledge the job is real, state capacity honestly, offer something concrete) are the conversation’s shape.\nThe distinction from a structural gap matters for the numbers. A capacity issue doesn’t lower ownership clarity, because the job is owned. It shows up as a stubborn repeat in the audit and, often, as a high battery score for the owner.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-04-deficit-audit-in-depth.html#part-b', l: 'WP-04: sort each gap', lt: 'How to tell a capacity issue from a structural gap, and what each needs.' },

  oneoff: { t: 'A one-off', m: ['One-off, no action', 'one-off misses'],
    s: 'The laundry slipped three times in March while you were away for work. In April it’s back to normal. That was a one-off.',
    f: 'For you, it’s permission to note it and move on. Between people, it guards against the opposite mistake too: a temporary cause shouldn’t become a permanent change. A couple of sick weeks isn’t a reason to hand someone’s jobs away for good.',
    w: 'It’s the third kind of repeat problem in the Deficit Audit: a miss with a clear, temporary cause, like illness, travel or a busy work month. No treaty change, no big talk. If the “one-off” keeps coming back, it was probably a structural gap all along.',
    x: 'Statisticians would call this separating signal from noise, or in quality-control language, common-cause variation (the ordinary wobble of any system) from special-cause variation (a one-time event with a nameable cause). You don’t redesign a process because of a special cause, and you don’t ignore a special cause that keeps recurring.\nWP-04’s rule reflects both halves: note the cause next to the miss, so next month’s check is easy, and upgrade it if it shows up again without a new temporary cause.\nIn the numbers, one-offs don’t count toward the structural gap total that goes into REPORT-01, which keeps the monthly count honest.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-04-deficit-audit-in-depth.html#part-b', l: 'WP-04: sort each gap', lt: 'All three kinds, and the caution about letting a temporary cause make a permanent change.' },

  anchortax: { t: 'The Anchor Tax', m: ['Anchor Tax'],
    s: 'Every few weeks: who books the kids’ haircuts? Ten minutes each time, always a bit sour. That repeat cost is the anchor tax.',
    f: 'For you, it’s the drain of re-deciding the same thing. Between people, it isn’t the missed task that costs the most, it’s arguing over it from scratch every time it comes back: who noticed, who did it last, whose turn it is.',
    w: 'Chapter V says the cost grows fastest wherever a structural gap is left open. The cure is to give the job a name that sticks, in the RACI Treaty, so it stops being renegotiated. This is Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives: an unowned job quietly pulls toward whoever tolerates it least.',
    x: 'Economists call this a transaction cost: the cost of negotiating an arrangement, separate from the cost of doing the work. Ronald Coase’s work on transaction costs explained why firms settle roles in advance instead of bargaining over every task, and the same logic applies at home. A settled owner is cheaper than a weekly negotiation, even if the split isn’t perfect.\nThe program measures it indirectly. The Deficit Audit counts repeats, REPORT-01’s recurrence rate shows whether fixes hold, and the daily check-in’s friction line reveals the same small irritation coming back.\nThe cure has two parts: one owner written into the treaty, and a date to review how it’s going, so the job doesn’t silently drift back.',
    u: '/book/chapter-5-in-depth.html#deficit', l: 'Chapter V: what a gap looks like at this scale', lt: 'Why a job that keeps reopening costs more than the job itself, and where the fix goes.' },

  retrobill: { t: 'Not a retroactive bill', m: ['retroactive bill'],
    s: 'After a hard month it’s tempting to want every past hour counted and charged. The audit deliberately doesn’t.',
    f: 'For you, it doesn’t mean your past effort didn’t matter. Between people, the program honors it differently: thank the specific thing, and fix the structure so the next month is shared. “You’ve carried that for months. Let’s make it shared from now.”',
    w: 'The Deficit Audit only looks back to find the pattern, and only acts forward on the structure. A job with no owner is a gap in the treaty, not a verdict on the person who kept covering it. WP-04 is also clear that it isn’t a performance review.',
    x: 'Accountants distinguish between restating past accounts and changing a policy going forward. The audit chooses the second: it changes the policy (the treaty) and leaves the past as information.\nThere’s a relationship reason too. Margaret Clark and Judson Mills’s work on communal and exchange relationships found that strict tit-for-tat accounting tends to feel wrong between close people, so an itemized bill for the past usually deepens the sense of debt rather than clearing it. The Preface’s line about grudges with better formatting is the same warning.\nIn practice: if old hours come up during the audit, write each one as a forward change (“from now on, this job belongs to…”) and pair it with one specific thank-you.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-04-deficit-audit-in-depth.html#not-for', l: 'WP-04: what this audit is not for', lt: 'The limits of the look-back: no bills, no reviews, no verdicts on the gap-filler.' },

  rhythms: { t: 'The program’s rhythms', m: ['two rhythms'],
    s: 'Brushing your teeth daily, a weekly shop, a monthly bill run: some things need different speeds. The program has three rhythms for the same reason.',
    f: 'For you, each rhythm is small enough to keep. Between people, each catches what the others miss: daily keeps small things small, weekly closes the books, monthly spots patterns. A couple might do breakfast, Sunday tea and the first Saturday of the month.',
    w: 'Daily: the 90-second check-in (WP-13). Weekly: the closing, where you tally the Lemonade Stand and glance at the treaty. Monthly: the Deficit Audit (WP-04), which steps back to find what keeps reopening. The monthly look only works if the weekly rhythm keeps feeding it real data.',
    x: 'Engineers design control loops at different timescales for the same reason: a fast loop corrects small errors immediately, and a slow loop catches drift the fast one can’t see. A phase-locked loop (WP-13’s namesake) is the fast loop; the audit is the slow one.\nBehavioral research supports small and frequent over big and rare. The About page’s lesson, that structure survives depletion where willpower doesn’t, matches work on habits by Wendy Wood and others: repeated actions tied to a stable cue become automatic, so they keep happening on bad days.\nREPORT-01 even has a finding for it, “The habit is holding”: the daily check-in ran at least five days a week, three weeks in a row.',
    u: '/book/chapter-5-in-depth.html#monthly', l: 'Chapter V: why a monthly look, on top of the weekly ones', lt: 'What each rhythm catches, and the blind spot a monthly look covers.' },

  maintmargin: { t: 'Maintenance margin', m: ['maintenance margin'],
    s: 'Every evening planned to the hour works fine, until a child gets a fever on Tuesday. Maintenance margin is the room you leave spare so an ordinary bad week doesn’t tip everything over.',
    f: 'For you, it’s the empty evening that isn’t wasted. Between people, it’s the slack that lets one person’s hard week be absorbed without a fight. Households, shared flats and small teams all need some.',
    w: 'A household running at nearly full capacity has no reserve, and the ordinary bad week always comes. It’s part of why a high battery reading means “later,” and why stress sits in the solvency sum at all: a load that only works when everyone is rested has no margin. This is Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives.',
    x: 'The name comes from finance. In a margin account, the maintenance margin is the minimum cushion of your own money that must stay in the account; drop below it and you get a margin call, forced to act at the worst moment. The program uses it as a picture: noticing, remembering and planning draw on a real, limited account.\nQueueing theory makes the same point with math. As a system’s utilization approaches 100 percent, waiting times don’t rise gently, they rise very steeply, which is why a nearly full schedule feels fine until one small delay cascades.\nIn the numbers, margin shows up as the (1 − stress) part of solvency and as the WP-02 bands: a battery above 0.6 is a sign the margin is gone for today, and anything not urgent should wait.',
    u: '/about-in-depth.html#curriculum', l: 'About: the nine inputs', lt: 'Where the program’s economic ideas came from, alongside the other eight fields.' },

  undefinedowner: { t: 'Undefined ownership', m: ['undefined ownership', 'whoever notices first'],
    s: 'The smoke alarm chirps for a week. Both of you hear it. Each assumes the other will deal with it. That’s undefined ownership.',
    f: 'For you, it explains why you keep ending up with jobs nobody gave you. Between people, work that belongs to everyone lands on whoever notices first, and then that person gets called controlling for noticing. It happens at home, in shared flats and on teams.',
    w: 'This is the problem the RACI Treaty (WP-03) was built for: one Responsible and one Accountable name per regular job. Changes happen on purpose and in writing, never by silent default. This is Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person, with a Pillar V twist: unclaimed jobs drift quietly to one person.',
    x: 'Social psychologists call the underlying pattern diffusion of responsibility: when a task could be anyone’s, each person feels a smaller share of it. John Darley and Bibb Latané’s 1968 studies are the classic source. Later real-world research has shown groups often do step in, so treat it as a pull, not a law, but households feel the pull every week.\nThe About page says fifteen years of audit work showed most things that go wrong aren’t caused by incompetence but by undefined ownership. Internal-control practice in auditing answers it with a single named owner for every recurring task, and WP-03 borrows that almost directly.\nIn the numbers, undefined ownership is exactly what the ownership clarity score measures, and it’s the reason that score carries the second-largest weight (0.35) in CALC-01: unowned work turns a fixable imbalance into a repeating one.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-03-raci-treaty-in-depth.html#purpose', l: 'WP-03: what the two roles mean', lt: 'The purpose of the treaty and why every job gets exactly two names.',
    ctx: { '/book/chapter-5.html': 'Here it’s the reason for the monthly count: so jobs don’t drift back to whoever notices first.', '/book/preface.html': 'The Preface names it as one of the quiet pulls: jobs nobody claims drift to whoever notices first.', '/calc01-solvency.html': 'It’s why ownership carries 0.35 of the score: unowned work turns a fixable imbalance into a repeating one.', '/five-pillars.html': 'This is where Pillars II and V meet: name an owner, or the job drifts back to one person.' } },

  /* ---------- Workpapers ---------- */

  field: { t: 'The Field Audit (WP-01)', m: ['Field Audit', 'Daily Balance Sheet', 'Who did what'],
    s: 'By Sunday, “I did loads this week” and “so did I” are two stories. The Field Audit writes the week down before it turns into a story.',
    f: 'For you, it’s a record your memory can’t quietly round up. Between people, each of you logs only your own side, then you compare. Couples, roommates and co-parents use the same five columns.',
    w: 'The columns: the day, the task noticed or done, who did it, rough minutes, and whether it was asked for or just noticed and handled. That last column is where invisible work finally shows up. Totals go into the Lemonade Stand and CALC-01’s balance. This is Pillar I, See the whole load.',
    x: 'Auditors call this fieldwork: collecting evidence where the work happens, before anyone draws conclusions. Time-use researchers do something similar with time diaries, because people’s estimates of their own hours are consistently less accurate than same-day records. Rough minutes written on the day beat precise-sounding guesses made on Sunday.\nThe “asked for or noticed and handled” column is the clever part. It turns Daminger’s anticipating and monitoring stages of household work, the parts that leave no trace, into rows you can count.\nThe log feeds three numbers: the Lemonade Stand split, CALC-01’s workload balance (1 − moved ÷ most), and over a month the Deficit Audit’s count of jobs that slipped. Part B of the same workpaper, the Neutral Refusals, covers what to say when the log shows you’re already full.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-01-in-depth.html#log', l: 'WP-01: the 7-day log', lt: 'The five columns, the audit rule, and how the totals feed the rest of the program.' },

  auditrule: { t: 'The audit rule', m: ['Audit rule'],
    s: '“Did ALL the dinner again because nobody else would” versus “Cooked dinner, 45 min, noticed and handled.” Same fact. Only one can be read without bracing.',
    f: 'For you, it keeps the log from becoming a case file. Between people, it means either of you can read the page without feeling accused. “I noticed the trash three times before I took it out” is one honest row, not three rows of resentment.',
    w: 'The rule: log what happened, not what should have happened. Write the event and the minutes first; leave what it means for later. If a row contains “always,” “never” or a feeling about the other person, rewrite it as just the event.',
    x: 'Professional auditing separates evidence from conclusions: working papers record what was observed, and findings come later, with their own reasoning. Marshall Rosenberg’s Nonviolent Communication makes the same cut for conversation, “observation without evaluation,” a phrase he credited to the philosopher Krishnamurti, who called it the highest form of human intelligence.\nThe reason is practical. Once a row carries a judgment, the reader can only respond to the judgment, and the event never gets discussed. A plain row can be sorted later into a structural gap, a capacity issue or a one-off; a loaded one just gets argued with.\nThe same discipline runs through the program: WP-09’s fact line has no adjectives, the Signal Translator’s facts box is kept apart from what you’ll say, and REPORT-01’s findings describe the setup, never a person.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-01-in-depth.html#audit-rule', l: 'WP-01: the audit rule', lt: 'The honest-row rule in context, next to the log it keeps clean.' },

  refusal: { t: 'A neutral refusal', m: ['Neutral Refusals', 'neutral refusal', 'kind ways to say no', 'Capacity Check', 'Delegation Pivot', 'Time Commitment'],
    s: 'Ever said yes to bedtime solo when you had nothing left, then snapped at 8:30? A neutral refusal is a calm, kind “not like this, not right now” that keeps the door open.',
    f: 'For you, it’s a way to protect your capacity without guilt. Between people, the other person is never left with nothing: “I know bedtime is a lot solo. I’m running on empty tonight. Can we split it: I’ll do bath, you do the rest?” It works with a partner, a parent, a friend or a boss.',
    w: 'Three moves, in order: acknowledge the request is real, state your capacity honestly, offer an alternative. WP-01 sorts them into three kinds: the Capacity Check (you’re already full), the Delegation Pivot (someone else is better placed) and the Time Commitment (yes, but not on that timeline). It’s not a justification essay or a counter-list of everything you did.',
    x: 'Negotiation expert William Ury described almost the same shape in The Power of a Positive No (2007): a “Yes” to your own interest or the relationship, a clear “No,” then a “Yes?” that proposes something else. The program arrived at it separately, from the founder’s need for a way to say no that doesn’t start a fight.\nWhy the order matters: hearing your request acknowledged first lowers the listener’s guard, so the no lands as information rather than rejection. Research on how people react to refusals generally finds that a reason and an alternative make a no far easier to accept than a bare no.\nWP-01 is honest that no set of scripts can cover every situation, so it teaches the pattern and lets you adapt the words. In the Deficit Audit, a neutral refusal is the recommended way to open a capacity-issue conversation.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-01-in-depth.html#part-b', l: 'WP-01: the Neutral Refusals', lt: 'Dozens of everyday scenarios in three groups, each written out in the three-move shape.',
    ctx: { '/workpapers/wp-04-deficit-audit.html': 'Here it’s the fix for a capacity issue: an honest “I can’t keep this” with something concrete offered instead.', '/workpapers/wp-09-tone-filter.html': 'This is the fallback when the filter says you’re running high: a “not right now” before you answer.', '/wp-11.html': 'The signal line here does related work: saying “not now” without it turning into a fight.' } },

  threemoves: { t: 'Acknowledge, state capacity, offer', m: ['State Capacity', 'Offer Alternative', 'three-part shape'],
    s: 'Three sentences can turn a “no” from a door slamming into a door held open. These are the three.',
    f: 'For you, counting to three stops you over-explaining. Between people, each sentence does one job: “The car noise does need looking at. I don’t know enough about cars to handle it well. Can you take the lead, and I’ll cover something else?”',
    w: 'Acknowledge means naming the actual need, not a generic “I understand.” State capacity is honest: not an excuse, and not making it sound worse than it is. Offer an alternative: a different time, a different person, part of the job, or a plan to revisit.',
    x: 'Each move matches something studied. Acknowledgement is what listening researchers since Carl Rogers have called reflecting: showing the message arrived before responding to it. Stating capacity is a plain, “I”-centred statement, the form Thomas Gordon popularised in the 1970s as an I-message. Offering an alternative keeps the exchange cooperative rather than zero-sum, the core move in interest-based negotiation (Fisher and Ury, Getting to Yes).\nThe order is doing work. Capacity stated before acknowledgement sounds like an excuse; an alternative offered before capacity sounds like dodging.\nA quick self-check from WP-01: aim for three sentences, one of each, then stop and listen. The other person can still respond, push back or suggest something else.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-01-in-depth.html#adapt', l: 'WP-01: build your own', lt: 'The three moves spelled out for any situation not in the examples.' },

  bandwidth: { t: 'Bandwidth', m: ['emotional bandwidth', 'bandwidth', 'at capacity'],
    s: 'You have time to listen but zero patience left. Or energy, but not the know-how. Bandwidth is how much you honestly have left to give, right now.',
    f: 'For you, naming it separates “I don’t care” from “I’m empty.” Between people, it’s honest information: “I can tell you need to talk. I don’t have the bandwidth to be fully present right now. Can we talk in an hour?”',
    w: 'Saying it plainly is the middle step of a neutral refusal. WP-01 names several kinds: energy, time, skill and timing. It’s information, not an excuse. If your bandwidth is low day after day, WP-02 helps you see how much you’re already carrying. This is Pillar III, Read your state first.',
    x: 'Economist Sendhil Mullainathan and psychologist Eldar Shafir used “bandwidth” in their 2013 book Scarcity for the mental capacity that gets used up when people are stretched. In their studies, people under scarcity (of time or money) did worse on attention and self-control tasks, not because of character but because part of their mind was busy with the shortfall.\nThat’s the program’s angle too: low bandwidth is a condition, not a verdict. Know Your Own Wiring separates lasting wiring, learned patterns and passing conditions, and bandwidth usually belongs to the last one.\nIn numbers, bandwidth shows up indirectly in the WP-02 battery (above 0.6, put off what can wait) and in the Calm-Down Kit’s re-entry bands (under 0.50 go back, 0.50 to 0.60 another round, 0.60 or above after two rounds, postpone).',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-01-in-depth.html#capacity-check', l: 'WP-01: the Capacity Check', lt: 'Scripts for the moments a request arrives when you’re already full.' },

  boundary: { t: 'A calm boundary', m: ['calm boundary', 'boundary statement'],
    s: 'Not a wall, not a speech: one clear, kind sentence about what you can and can’t do right now.',
    f: 'For you, it’s short on purpose so you can say it without an edge. Between people, it leaves room to answer: “I get why your mom’s request feels urgent. I don’t have room for it this week. Can you let her know it’ll wait, or take this one?”',
    w: 'WP-01 describes a neutral refusal as exactly this: a single, calm boundary statement that leaves room to renegotiate. It isn’t a justification essay, a counter-list of everything you did, or a refusal to ever revisit the request. Each of those shuts a door; a calm boundary holds one open.',
    x: 'Therapists often define a boundary as a statement about what you will do, not a rule for what the other person must do. “I don’t have room this week” is yours to say; “you can’t ask me that” tries to control someone else. That difference is why the program’s boundary sentences start from your own capacity.\nTiming matters. A boundary said while revved up tends to come out sharper than meant. If you can’t say it calmly yet, the Calm-Down Kit’s signal line buys time first: name your state, how long you need, and when you’ll be back.\nA boundary is also not an exit. WP-02 and WP-11 are both clear that a boundary or a high score used to make a conversation never happen has become a different problem.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-01-in-depth.html#adapt', l: 'WP-01: the three moves for any situation', lt: 'How a single boundary sentence is built, and what it deliberately leaves out.' },

  battery: { t: 'Your load score (WP-02)', m: ['Battery & Stress Meter', 'Battery &amp; Stress Meter', 'Autonomic Saturation Index', 'battery score', 'load score', 'How full is your battery?'],
    s: 'Like a phone at 8 percent, some days you’re running on almost nothing before anything has even happened. Your load score (WP-02) is a one-minute check of how much you’re already carrying. A heavier load means a lower battery.',
    f: 'For you, it answers “am I upset about this, or just full?” Between people, it’s a timing tool: “My load’s high today. Can we do the budget talk tomorrow evening?” You fill it in only about yourself, never about the other person.',
    w: 'Score five things from 0 to 4 for the last day or two, add them up and divide by 20. Under 0.3: probably about the thing itself. 0.3 to 0.6: say “heads up, I’m carrying more than usual.” 0.6 or more: put off what doesn’t need deciding in the next hour. A high score means “later,” never “never.” This is Pillar III, Read your state first.',
    x: 'Load score = (sleep debt + workload elsewhere + recent unresolved conflict + physical state + time pressure) ÷ 20, each scored 0 (not at all) to 4 (very true). Ten points is 0.50. The bands in the code are low below 0.30, medium from 0.30 to under 0.60, high from 0.60, read from the rounded score.\nIt’s a self-rated checklist, not a validated scale. Psychometricians would say it has face validity (the items obviously relate to load) but no tested reliability, and WP-02 says so: it’s a structured gut-check, not a clinical test. What makes it useful is using the same five items every time.\nThe same number travels. CALC-01 averages everyone’s load score into its stress input (entered as 1 − stress, weight 0.25). The Calm-Down Kit takes it before and after settling, and uses 0.50 and 0.60 as its re-entry thresholds. REPORT-01 watches it for three-week rises.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-02-battery-stress-meter-in-depth.html#reading', l: 'WP-02: what each score means', lt: 'The formula, the three bands and what each one asks of you.',
    ctx: { '/book/chapter-3.html': 'In this chapter the load score is the number behind “a full tank overflows”: it separates leftover stress from this-moment stress.', '/relationships.html': 'In any relationship the use is the same: say your number before a hard talk, never guess theirs.', '/wp-11.html': 'The Kit reuses this exact score before and after you settle, so the two readings can be compared.', '/book/chapter-2.html': 'Here the load score becomes the stress input: everyone’s score averaged, entered as 1 − stress.', sec: { workpapers: 'Among the workpapers, this is the one to run first when something feels on fire: WP-02, then WP-09.' } } },

  fivefactors: { t: 'The five battery factors', m: ['5-Factor', 'five factors', 'Sleep debt', 'Workload elsewhere'],
    s: 'You feel “fine” but bit someone’s head off over a small question. Running through five ordinary drains often shows why.',
    f: 'For you, scoring them one by one points to the easiest fix. Sleep 3, work 2, conflict 0, physical 3 (skipped lunch), time 2: that’s 10 out of 20, or 0.50, and eating something is the quickest improvement.',
    w: 'The five, each scored 0 to 4 for the last 24 to 48 hours: sleep debt, workload elsewhere (job, school, caring), recent unresolved conflict (from any source), physical state (hungry, sick, in pain), and time pressure today. The Calm-Down Kit uses the same five on purpose, so every reading can be compared.',
    x: 'Each factor maps to something researchers study. Sleep loss is the most striking: in a 2007 study by Seung-Schik Yoo and colleagues, sleep-deprived volunteers showed stronger amygdala responses to upsetting images than rested ones. Today’s Weather reflects the same idea by giving last night’s sleep a lot of weight.\nThe others echo work on stress spillover (workload elsewhere), unresolved conflict carrying over, the everyday “HALT” reminder (hungry, angry, lonely, tired), and time pressure narrowing attention.\nEqual weights are a design choice, not a finding. Five items at 0 to 4 give 21 possible totals, which is plenty of resolution for a timing decision. Changing the items or weights would break comparisons with past readings, which is why WP-02, WP-11 and CALC-01 all share them.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-02-battery-stress-meter-in-depth.html#checklist-h', l: 'WP-02: the checklist', lt: 'The five rows exactly as you score them, with what 0 and 4 mean.' },

  notclinical: { t: 'Not a clinical tool', m: ['not a clinical instrument', 'not a clinical or diagnostic instrument', 'not a clinical tool', 'not a validated clinical'],
    s: 'A bathroom scale can’t tell you why you feel off, and neither can these tools. They help you reflect; they don’t diagnose or treat anyone.',
    f: 'For you, think of them as a structured gut-check, not a test with a right answer. Between people, a number here describes a situation, never a person, so it can’t prove who is right.',
    w: 'The numbers weren’t built from research data, and they predict nothing. That’s not a weakness to hide; it’s what keeps them gentle. The Calm-Down Kit says it directly: panic attacks, trauma responses and ongoing anxiety are not what these tools address, and a doctor or therapist is the better call.',
    x: 'In measurement science, a clinical instrument has to show reliability (it gives consistent results) and validity (it measures what it claims), usually across large, tested samples, and it’s interpreted by someone trained to use it. None of the program’s tools has been through that, and none claims to.\nWhat they are is closer to a structured journal. CALC-01 calls itself a rule-of-thumb worksheet; REPORT-01 says every finding is a written rule you can check, not a model or a prediction.\nThat honesty has a practical upside. Because nothing claims to know you better than you do, the numbers can start a conversation without anyone having to defend themselves against them.',
    u: '/how-it-works-in-depth.html#honest-note', l: 'How it works: an honest note', lt: 'What the tools are, what they aren’t, and why they’re still worth using.' },

  heuristic: { t: 'A heuristic', m: ['heuristic self-reflection tool', 'practical heuristic', 'heuristic'],
    s: 'A quick sketch of a room is great for deciding where the sofa goes, even though it’s not a surveyor’s measurement. That’s a heuristic: a practical rule of thumb.',
    f: 'For you, it’s a helpful starting point, not a verdict. Between people, it gives two people the same simple frame to talk from. What makes it worth acting on is using it the same way, week after week.',
    w: 'The site calls its tools heuristics on purpose. CALC-01’s weights are judgment calls; the “P” in P(Solvency) is only a label; the Lemonade Stand is a “Heuristic” in its full name. One entry is a mood. Twelve entries, asked the same way, start to show a pattern memory alone can’t.',
    x: 'Herbert Simon introduced the idea of “satisficing” in the 1950s: in real life, people use good-enough rules because perfect information isn’t available. Later, Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky showed how mental shortcuts can mislead (biases), while Gerd Gigerenzer showed that simple, well-chosen rules often perform surprisingly well (“fast and frugal” heuristics). Both are true, which is why a heuristic should be simple, stated openly and checked over time.\nThe program follows those three rules. Every formula is printed, every weight has a reason, and every rule in REPORT-01 is listed so it can be checked rather than trusted.\nThe limit is stated just as openly: a heuristic can’t predict anything or say whether a relationship is worth continuing. It can give you a calmer place to start.',
    u: '/how-it-works-in-depth.html#honest-note', l: 'How it works: an honest note', lt: 'Why the program is built from rules of thumb, and how repetition makes them trustworthy.',
    ctx: { '/library/': 'This Library theme uses “heuristic” in the research sense: a shortcut that’s useful but can mislead.', '/start-here.html': 'Here it’s a promise: the framework is a rule of thumb, not a clinically tested tool.', '/terms-of-service.html': 'In the terms it’s a legal note: the tools are rule-of-thumb reflection aids, not validated instruments.' } },

  raci: { t: 'RACI: one owner per job (WP-03)', m: ['Domestic RACI Treaty', 'RACI Treaty', 'RACI', 'One owner per job'], cs: true,
    s: 'You both thought the other was buying the nieces’ birthday presents. One row with two names would have caught it. That’s the RACI Treaty.',
    f: 'For you, it ends the weekly re-deciding of who owns what. Between people, each regular job gets two names: who does it, and who notices if it didn’t happen. It fits a household, a shared apartment, co-parents across two homes, or a team.',
    w: 'Responsible does the job; Accountable notices if it didn’t happen and follows up (it can be the same person). The starter list covers groceries, cooking, dishes, laundry, bills, cleaning, pet care, car, the family calendar and emotional check-ins. It changes only through an amendment, in writing. This is Pillar II, Fix the setup, not the person.',
    x: 'RACI comes from project management, where it stands for Responsible, Accountable, Consulted and Informed. The home version keeps only R and A, because most household jobs don’t need a consulted and informed list, and fewer columns means the table actually gets filled in. The About page says it was borrowed almost directly from internal-control practice in auditing: one named owner per recurring task.\nIt feeds CALC-01 directly. Ownership clarity = jobs with both names ÷ all jobs listed, weight 0.35. A row with one name counts as unowned.\nThe treaty has built-in change control. Either person can call a review at the weekly closing, changes go into the table, and everyone agrees to them. REPORT-01’s “Treaty gone quiet” finding fires after six weeks with no changes, because a living agreement that never changes probably isn’t being used.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-03-raci-treaty-in-depth.html#treaty', l: 'WP-03: the treaty table', lt: 'The full starter table, then how it’s changed and agreed.' },

  ra: { t: 'Responsible and Accountable', m: ['Responsible (R)', 'Accountable (A)', 'one Responsible'],
    s: 'One person writes the shopping list and goes. The other checks on Friday that it’s planned. Doer and noticer: that’s Responsible and Accountable.',
    f: 'For you, it means a shared job doesn’t have to be a vague job. Between people, one person can hold both roles, or you can split them. Accountable isn’t a boss; it’s the quiet safety net so a job doesn’t slide into nobody’s column.',
    w: 'Responsible does the task. Accountable notices if it didn’t happen and follows up. A row only counts toward ownership clarity when both names are written in, and an empty Accountable box is often where a structural gap is hiding.',
    x: 'Organization designers talk about a single point of accountability: whatever the size of the team, one person is answerable for each outcome. RACI charts in project management usually insist on exactly one A per task for that reason. The home treaty keeps the rule and drops the jargon.\nSplitting the roles is useful when skills or schedules differ. One person may be home when the job needs doing; the other may be better at remembering it exists. The split lets the noticing, often the invisible part, be named and owned.\nIn the numbers, a row with R and no A is not owned, so it lowers ownership clarity. That’s deliberate: a job someone does but nobody watches tends to slip the first time the doer has a hard week.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-03-raci-treaty-in-depth.html#purpose', l: 'WP-03: what the two roles mean', lt: 'The two roles defined, and why every job gets exactly one of each.' },

  amendment: { t: 'The amendment process', m: ['amendment process'],
    s: 'A new job with early starts, a new baby, a health issue: life changes. The amendment process is how the job list changes with it, openly.',
    f: 'For you, it means asking for a change is normal, not a failure. Between people, it protects both: without it, ownership slowly shifts to whoever cracks first, and the other may not even know it happened.',
    w: 'Three steps: either person can call a review at the weekly closing; changes are written into the table, not just agreed out loud, and both people agree to the new version. Nobody has to earn a change or apologize for asking.',
    x: 'Constitutions and contracts both include a way to amend them for the same reason: an agreement that can’t change gets ignored instead. Change control in engineering and project management follows the same pattern: propose, record, approve, so everyone is working from the same version.\nREPORT-01 watches for the opposite problem. Its “Treaty gone quiet” rule fires after six or more recorded weeks with no treaty changes, reading from WP-03 and WP-13, because a living agreement that never changes probably isn’t being used.\nThe Deficit Audit is the other trigger. Every structural gap it finds should end in an amendment that same session, so the fix is written down rather than remembered.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-03-raci-treaty-in-depth.html#amendment', l: 'WP-03: changing the treaty', lt: 'The three steps for changing the table on purpose, not by drift.' },

  ratification: { t: 'Agreeing on the list', m: ['Agreeing on It', 'Agreeing on it', 'clear, not perfect'],
    s: 'One box ticked at the bottom of the page, “We’re agreed on this,” and a date to look at it again. It turns a draft into an agreement.',
    f: 'For you, agreeing means “clear,” not “perfect.” Between people, you’re agreeing that ownership is written down correctly, not that every job feels fair. One row can still carry a note: “revisit in a month.”',
    w: 'It’s the last part of WP-03. Clear first, fair next: once ownership is written down, fairness can be talked about calmly, with the Lemonade Stand and the Deficit Audit. Splitting those two conversations is what makes both of them go better.',
    x: 'Separating “is it clear?” from “is it fair?” is a deliberate design choice. Negotiation research suggests that agreeing on the facts first makes later disagreement about values easier to handle, because nobody is arguing about what the arrangement even is.\nIt also connects to the numbers. Ownership clarity (jobs with both names ÷ all jobs) measures only the first question. REPORT-01’s “Clarity without parity” finding exists for the second: clarity 0.85 or higher while balance is 0.60 or lower, meaning the treaty is clear but the split is not.\nAn agreed table gives the Deficit Audit a baseline. Without one, a job that slipped can’t be sorted into structural gap, capacity issue or one-off.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-03-raci-treaty-in-depth.html#agreeing', l: 'WP-03: agreeing on it', lt: 'What agreeing does and doesn’t mean.' },

  tone: { t: 'The Tone Filter (WP-09)', m: ['Tone Transducer', 'fact, feeling and ask', 'fact, feeling and a clear ask', 'Say it so it lands', 'transducer step'],
    s: 'You’ve typed “You never think about anyone but yourself” and your thumb is over send. The Tone Filter is the 30-second check you do first.',
    f: 'For you, it turns a raw reaction into three lines: the fact (no adjectives), the feeling underneath, and the ask. Between people, the other person can answer an ask; they can only defend against a verdict.',
    w: 'WP-09’s example: raw reaction “You never think about anyone but yourself.” Fact: the trash wasn’t taken out before pickup. Feeling: frustrated, a little unseen. Ask: a fixed day and time for trash. If the sentence is a verdict on the person (“you always,” “you’re careless”), stop and swap it for one fact and one ask. This is Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive.',
    x: 'A transducer turns one kind of signal into another, like a microphone turning sound into electricity. WP-09’s transducer step turns a reaction into something the other person can receive.\nIts three lines closely match Marshall Rosenberg’s Nonviolent Communication, which uses four: observation, feeling, need and request. It also echoes the Gottman Institute’s “softened start-up”: complain about an event rather than criticize a person. Gottman’s lab reported that how a conflict conversation starts strongly predicts how it ends, which is why the program puts this step before speaking.\nIn the numbers, running a rough moment through WP-09 before answering is what counts as a retune. Retunes ÷ rough moments gives retuning frequency, the fourth input to CALC-01’s apex score (weight 0.15).',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-09-tone-filter-in-depth.html#transducer', l: 'WP-09: the transducer step', lt: 'The three questions, the verdict test, and a full worked example.',
    ctx: { '/workpapers/wp-09-tone-filter.html': 'On this page you can fill the three lines in straight away, then test the ask in the Signal Translator.', '/check-ins.html': 'In a check-in, these three lines become your opening: one fact, one impact, one ask.' } },

  filter: { t: 'The filter step', m: ['filter step', 'Emotional Translation Filter'],
    s: '“Are you going to be on your phone all night?” lands like an attack. Before answering, the filter asks: is there a kinder reading that fits too?',
    f: 'For you, it’s four quick questions in your head. Between people, it often turns a defensive reply into a warm one: “Sorry, I’ll put it down. Want to watch something?”',
    w: 'The four checks: is this about a specific task or event? Would I read it the same way if my battery weren’t already running high? Is there a neutral reading that also fits? Am I responding to their words, or to a pattern from an old conversation? If you’re running high or a neutral reading fits, use a “not right now” script first.',
    x: 'Psychologists call what the filter does cognitive reappraisal: changing how you interpret a situation before the emotion runs its full course. James Gross’s research on emotion regulation found reappraisal generally works better for people and relationships than suppression, which tends to cost more and leak out anyway.\nThe “neutral reading” question targets a known pattern called hostile attribution bias: reading ambiguous behavior as intentionally hostile. It’s more likely when you’re tired, stressed or primed by past conflict, which is why the second question points at your WP-02 battery.\nThe fourth question is about history. Chapter III’s “historical pattern” angle and the Stories page’s Second Arrow both describe the same trap: answering the last five times instead of this one.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-09-tone-filter-in-depth.html#filter', l: 'WP-09: the filter step', lt: 'The four checks as a yes/no table, and what to do when one comes up.' },

  factsbox: { t: 'The facts box', m: ['facts box'],
    s: '“The dishes were still in the sink” is a fact. “You never help” is a criticism of the person. The facts box keeps them apart.',
    f: 'For you, writing the plain event first shows whether it’s worth raising at all. Between people, the listener hears a request instead of a charge: the box says “dishes in the sink at 7am,” the sentence says “could we agree a quick kitchen close by 9?”',
    w: 'In the Signal Translator the facts box holds the event and isn’t scored as the message. When a fact and a judgment are mixed, the listener can only answer the judgment, so they defend their character and the event never gets discussed.',
    x: 'Separating observation from evaluation is the first step in Rosenberg’s Nonviolent Communication and a core habit in auditing, where evidence and conclusions sit in different places. The facts box builds that separation into the tool.\nIt also improves what the translator can do. The Signal Translator looks for patterns in wording, such as labels, vague timing and hints, that often cause static for particular wirings. If a frustrated fact sits inside the sentence, the tool ends up scoring the frustration. Moved to the facts box, it scores only what you plan to say.\nThe Check-ins page links this to WP-09: the fact line goes in the box, and the ask becomes the phrase. That pairing is the simplest version of the whole program’s method: facts first, then the request.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#translator', l: 'Check-ins: where the Signal Translator sits', lt: 'When to use the translator, what to put in each box, and what it protects.' },

  pll: { t: 'The 90-second check-in (WP-13)', m: ['Phase-Locked Loop', '90-second daily check-in', '90-second check-in', 'daily loop'],
    s: 'Brushing your teeth takes two minutes and prevents a lot of dentist visits. The 90-second check-in does the same for small frictions.',
    f: 'For you, it’s four one-sentence lines at a steady moment, like after dinner. Between people, one speaks and the other just listens: no debating, no solving. Couples, parents and teens, and roommates can all run it.',
    w: 'Load, one appreciation, one small friction (optional), one ask for tomorrow (optional). Anything bigger waits for the weekly catch-up. The one thing to watch: using it to deliver criticism, which makes people start avoiding it, which causes exactly the drift it’s meant to prevent. This is Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive.',
    x: 'A phase-locked loop is an electronic circuit that keeps an oscillator in step with a reference signal. It compares the two continuously and makes tiny corrections, so the error never builds up. PLLs are inside radios, phones and computers for exactly this reason: small constant corrections are cheaper than big occasional ones.\nWP-13 claims the same at home: a 90-second daily check-in heads off most of what would otherwise become a 45-minute weekend argument. That’s the program’s own rule of thumb rather than a measured figure, but it matches habit research: small, frequent, cue-linked actions survive bad days better than big resolutions.\nIn the numbers, loops run each week go into REPORT-01. “The habit is holding” fires at five or more days a week for three weeks; “The loop isn’t feeding forward” fires when friction is logged for three or more weeks but nothing moves on to WP-03 or WP-09.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-13-pll-protocol-in-depth.html#daily-loop', l: 'WP-13: the daily loop', lt: 'The four lines word for word, and the one rule that keeps it light.' },

  fourparts: { t: 'Load, appreciation, friction, ask', m: ['load, appreciation, friction, ask', 'load, appreciation, friction'],
    s: '“Medium today. I appreciated the note on the counter. No friction. Could you grab bread tomorrow?” Four lines, twenty seconds.',
    f: 'For you, it’s a quick status report, not a conversation. Between people, starting with the appreciation sets the tone for the rest, and the friction line keeps small things small before they pile up.',
    w: 'Load: “Today I was at about low, medium or high capacity.” Appreciation: one specific thing, with the moment. Friction: one small thing that didn’t feel great (optional). Ask: one thing that would help tomorrow (optional). Specific beats general.',
    x: 'Each line has research behind it. The load line is a one-word state report, the everyday version of Pillar III. The appreciation line matters because specific gratitude is what psychologist Sara Algoe calls “find, remind and bind”: it helps people notice and stay close to those who are good for them. The friction line keeps complaints small and early, the opposite of what Gottman calls “kitchen-sinking,” where a backlog pours out at once.\nThe ask line borrows from implementation-intention research (Peter Gollwitzer): a concrete “tomorrow, by four” is far more likely to happen than a vague wish.\nThe friction line is also data. REPORT-01 watches whether frictions logged over three or more weeks ever move on to a fix; if not, “The loop isn’t feeding forward.”',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-13-pll-protocol-in-depth.html#daily-loop', l: 'WP-13: the daily loop', lt: 'Each of the four lines, with examples and the no-debate rule.' },

  drift: { t: 'Frequency drift', m: ['frequency drift'],
    s: 'A missed thank-you, an irritation left unsaid, and after a busy month a small comment about the car sets off a 45-minute argument about everything. That slow slide is drift.',
    f: 'For you, nobody decides to drift, so there’s no one to blame. Between people, each small thing was minor; together they weren’t. It happens with friends who stop calling as much as with partners.',
    w: 'The daily check-in exists to stop drift building up: tiny corrections every day instead of big ones every so often. Its main risk is being skipped, and the easiest way to get it skipped is to use it for criticism. Keeping it light keeps it going.',
    x: 'In electronics, drift is a slow change in a signal’s frequency or phase caused by temperature, ageing or noise. Left alone, even a tiny drift accumulates: a clock that gains one second a day is off by six minutes a year. A phase-locked loop exists to catch drift before it adds up.\nIn relationships, researchers describe something similar with “turning away” from small bids: in Gottman and Levenson’s newlywed study, couples who later split had turned toward far fewer of each other’s small bids. No single miss mattered; the pattern did.\nThe program’s answer is layered rhythms: the daily loop corrects small errors, the weekly catch-up moves repeats to the right worksheet, and the monthly audit catches what the weekly scale can’t see.',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-13-pll-protocol-in-depth.html#why', l: 'WP-13: why small and frequent beats big and rare', lt: 'How the circuit works, and why tiny daily corrections head off the weekend argument.' },

  resync: { t: 'The weekly resync', m: ['weekly resync'],
    s: '“No heads-up about dinner” came up three times this week. Once a week, the resync notices, and moves it somewhere it can actually be fixed.',
    f: 'For you, it means the daily check-in can stay light, because repeats have a place to go. Between people, a repeated friction becomes a small change instead of a growing irritation.',
    w: 'Skim the week’s daily notes for anything that came up more than twice. If it’s about who owns a job, it goes to the RACI Treaty (WP-03). If it’s about how things are said, it goes to the Tone Filter (WP-09). If it needs a real talk, that’s a check-in, not a longer 90 seconds.',
    x: 'Operations teams call this triage: sort incoming issues by type and send each to the process built to handle it. The resync is a five-minute triage of the week’s frictions.\nThe routing rule is simple and it matters. Ownership problems are structural, so they belong in the treaty. Delivery problems are about tone and timing, so they belong in WP-09. Mixing them up is how people end up having a tone argument about what was really an unowned job.\nREPORT-01 checks whether the resync is doing its job. If friction is logged for three or more weeks with nothing moved on to another worksheet, it reports “The loop isn’t feeding forward.”',
    u: '/workpapers/wp-13-pll-protocol-in-depth.html#weekly-resync', l: 'WP-13: the weekly catch-up', lt: 'What to look for in the week’s notes, and where each repeat goes.' },

  closing: { t: 'The weekly closing', m: ['weekly closing', 'closing the books', 'close the books'],
    s: 'Sunday evening, a cup of tea, ten minutes: add up the week, glance at who owns what, and call the week done. That’s the weekly closing.',
    f: 'For you, it gives the week a clear end so it doesn’t leak into the next. Between people, it’s the one regular moment where either of you can ask to change the job list, without it being a big deal.',
    w: 'Tally the Lemonade Stand hours, glance at the RACI Treaty, run the weekly resync on the daily notes, and note anything that needs a proper talk with a named time. It’s the middle rhythm, between the daily check-in and the monthly Deficit Audit, and it feeds both.',
    x: 'Accountants “close the books” at the end of each period: they reconcile what was recorded, fix errors while they’re fresh, and lock the period so the next one starts clean. The weekly closing borrows that habit, including the key rule that a month-end review is only as good as the weekly records under it. WP-04 says the same about the Deficit Audit.\nIt’s also where the numbers come together. This is the moment to run CALC-01, and to add the week to REPORT-01: WP-01 minutes, WP-02 battery sums, WP-03 owned and listed jobs, WP-09 friction and retunes, WP-13 loops, and any treaty changes.\nChapter II says the solvency number’s only job is to give people a calm starting point for exactly this conversation.',
    u: '/book/chapter-5-in-depth.html#monthly', l: 'Chapter V: the weekly and monthly rhythms', lt: 'Where the weekly closing sits among the rhythms, and why the monthly look depends on it.',
    ctx: { '/workpapers/report-01.html': 'This is when you add the week here: a minute of copying in the figures from each worksheet you ran.', '/workpapers/wp-03-raci-treaty.html': 'This is the moment either of you can ask to change the treaty, in writing, with both initials.', '/workpapers/wp-13-pll-protocol.html': 'Anything the daily check-in can’t hold waits for this: the weekly closing.', '/book/chapter-2.html': 'The chapter says the solvency number exists to start exactly this conversation.' } },

  /* ---------- Settling the body ---------- */

  kit: { t: 'The Calm-Down Kit (WP-11)', m: ['Calm-Down Kit'],
    s: 'In the middle of a heated moment, nobody thinks “let me weigh my options.” The Calm-Down Kit is a plan you make while calm, so later you just do what you already chose.',
    f: 'For you, it’s your own settling plan: what sets you off, your body’s first signal, two go-to moves and one sentence to say. Between people, that sentence means a pause is understood as a pause, not walking out. It’s for you, never something to hand the other person.',
    w: 'Three parts: before you need it (triggers, first signal, two defaults, a signal line); in the moment (read your battery, say the line, run your default); coming back (read your battery again and go back when the number says so). It isn’t treatment. This is Pillar III, Read your state first.',
    x: 'The design rests on two research findings. Psychologist George Loewenstein described the hot–cold empathy gap: when calm, we badly underestimate how differently we’ll think when upset, so plans made in the heat of the moment are poor. And Peter Gollwitzer’s work on implementation intentions found that simple “if this, then I’ll do that” plans, made in advance, make follow-through much more likely.\nThe Kit is both at once: decide in the cold state, act on a pre-made if-then in the hot one.\nIt measures with the same scale as everything else, on purpose: WP-02’s five questions, 0 to 4, divided by 20, taken before and after. Re-entry thresholds are 0.50 and 0.60. It deliberately leaves out anything that works by cold, pain or shock, and makes no claims about frequencies or brainwaves. For panic, trauma responses or ongoing anxiety, it points to a doctor or therapist instead.',
    u: '/wp-11-in-depth.html#purpose', l: 'WP-11: what the kit is for', lt: 'The whole kit: purpose, defaults, the six kinds of settling move, re-entry and what it is not for.',
    ctx: { '/library/calm.html': 'This Library theme has the research on settling; the Kit is where you turn it into your own two-move plan.', sec: { workpapers: 'Among the workpapers, the Kit comes before the treaty talk and the Tone Filter: settle first, then talk.' } } },

  firstsignal: { t: 'The body’s first signal', m: ['first signal', 'somatic signals'],
    s: 'A tight jaw. Gripping your mug. Shoulders creeping up. Your body usually knows you’re getting wound up before your thoughts do.',
    f: 'For you, knowing your first signal gives you a head start. Between people, it lets you pause before any sharp words: put the mug down, breathe out slowly, say you need a moment.',
    w: 'Common first signals: a tight jaw or shoulders, shallow breathing, sharper hearing, cold hands, heat, a tight chest, an urge to leave or to win. The Calm-Down Kit asks you to name yours on an ordinary day, so the first sign becomes the cue instead of waiting for the fifth.',
    x: 'Researchers call the sense of your body’s inner state interoception. People vary a lot in how early and accurately they notice these signals, and studies suggest interoceptive awareness can improve with practice, much like any other kind of noticing.\nThe program’s rule, from Check-ins, is that the explosion comes late; the useful signs are early and ordinary. That lines up with emotion-regulation research (James Gross’s process model): it’s easier to change the course of an emotion near its start than once it’s in full swing.\nSome wirings notice body signals later or less clearly. The Library notes this is more common with autism, ADHD and alexithymia, and suggests outside cues can help, like regular meals and a set time to check in with yourself.',
    u: '/wp-11-in-depth.html#defaults', l: 'WP-11: before you need it', lt: 'Where you name your triggers and first signal, and choose your two defaults.' },

  defaults: { t: 'Your two defaults', m: ['two defaults'],
    s: 'When you’re at 9 out of 10, you won’t browse a menu. Your two defaults are the settling moves you picked in advance, so you just do the first one.',
    f: 'For you, a first choice and a backup: “walk it out” at home, “naming the room” when you can’t leave, like in the car. Between people, a known default means the other person isn’t left guessing what you’re doing.',
    w: 'The Kit lists six kinds of move to choose from: physiological (a longer out-breath), orienting (naming the room), kinesthetic (weight and pressure), environmental (lower the input), movement (walk it out) and auditory (low, steady sound, if it suits you). Everything works by slowing the body down, not startling it.',
    x: 'Choosing in advance is the whole trick. Decision research shows that choices get harder and worse under stress and fatigue, and Gollwitzer’s implementation-intention studies show that a pre-set “if I notice X, I’ll do Y” gets carried out far more reliably than a general intention to calm down.\nThe six categories cover different routes in. Some people settle through the breath, some through the senses, some through movement. The Kit suggests testing both defaults once on a calm day, so your body already knows them.\nIt is honest about limits: these are ordinary settling techniques, not treatment, and nothing in the list has been tested as one. The auditory row is marked as personal preference, not a proven method.',
    u: '/wp-11-in-depth.html#moment', l: 'WP-11: in the moment', lt: 'The six kinds of move, each with exactly how to run it.' },

  asymbreath: { t: 'Asymmetric breathing', m: ['Asymmetric breathing', 'long exhale'],
    s: 'Ever noticed a big sigh on the way out seems to let something go? Asymmetric breathing does that on purpose: breathe out for longer than you breathe in.',
    f: 'For you, it needs no equipment, no privacy and no explanation: four seconds in, six out, for at least two minutes, at a table or in the car. Between people, you can do it quietly while the other person is still talking.',
    w: 'The longer out-breath is the part that helps; counting just keeps you doing it long enough, which is harder than it sounds when you’re upset. It’s one of the Calm-Down Kit’s defaults, and the Carrier Wave Decoder’s one-minute retune uses the same in-for-4, out-for-6.',
    x: 'The physiology behind it is well described. Heart rate naturally rises a little as you breathe in and falls a little as you breathe out, a pattern called respiratory sinus arrhythmia. The out-breath is linked with the parasympathetic side of the nervous system (the “rest” branch, carried largely by the vagus nerve), so lengthening it tilts each breath toward the slowing phase.\nFour in and six out is ten seconds per breath, or six breaths a minute, a pace researchers often use when studying slow breathing.\nThe program keeps claims modest: it’s an ordinary settling technique, not a treatment, and if counting makes you more tense rather than less, another default is a better choice.',
    u: '/wp-11-in-depth.html#moment', l: 'WP-11: the settling moves', lt: 'The breathing row, alongside the five other kinds of move you can pick from.' },

  namingroom: { t: 'Naming the room', m: ['Naming the room', 'Five things you can see'],
    s: 'The argument in your head is loud, but the room is right here: a lamp, a blue mug, the fridge hum. Naming the room brings your attention back to where you actually are.',
    f: 'For you, it’s five things you can see, four you can hear, three you can touch, said quietly or just thought. Between people, it’s invisible and needs no explanation, so it works mid-conversation.',
    w: 'It’s the Kit’s orienting move, and a good pick if you tend to go flat, blank or far away under stress, not only if you get revved up. Check-ins notes that going blank is a kind of dysregulation too; it isn’t the same as calm.',
    x: 'Therapists often call this a grounding exercise, and the 5-4-3-2-1 version (five sights, four sounds, three touches, two smells, one taste) is widely used. The Kit uses the first three steps.\nThe idea behind it is attention. Worry and rumination use the same limited mental room as noticing the world; filling that room with plain, present detail gives the loop less space. It also re-engages the senses, which is why it suits people who drift away rather than rev up.\nOrienting has a long history in the study of attention: animals, including us, reflexively look around to check the surroundings when something changes. Doing it on purpose borrows that reflex.',
    u: '/wp-11-in-depth.html#moment', l: 'WP-11: the settling moves', lt: 'The orienting move and the other five, each with exactly how to run it.' },

  arousal: { t: 'Autonomic arousal', m: ['autonomic arousal', 'acute arousal'],
    s: 'You finish a stressful call and walk straight into a question about the weekend. The answer comes out clipped. Your body was still running from the call. That’s arousal.',
    f: 'For you, arousal just means how switched on your body is. It helps you run for a bus; it doesn’t help you hear a careful sentence. Between people, high arousal makes pauses feel like being ignored and neutral words land at full volume.',
    w: 'The Calm-Down Kit’s whole purpose is to bring it down far enough that an ordinary conversation, the treaty session (WP-03) or the Tone Filter (WP-09) is possible again. A fact, feeling and ask written while revved up all come out sharper than you meant.',
    x: 'Psychologists have linked arousal and performance since Robert Yerkes and John Dodson’s 1908 study, often summarized as an inverted U: some arousal sharpens you, too much makes complex tasks harder. The original study was about mice and simple learning, and the neat curve is an oversimplification, but the everyday point holds: listening and careful wording are exactly the complex tasks that suffer first.\nOn the program’s ladder, high arousal is the “revved up” or Gusty rung. Know Your Own Wiring adds Dan Siegel’s window of tolerance: the zone where you can feel strong feelings and still think. Above it you’re revved up, below it shut down.\nThe Kit measures it indirectly with the WP-02 battery, and uses the before-and-after readings to decide when to go back in.',
    u: '/wp-11-in-depth.html#purpose', l: 'WP-11: what the kit is for', lt: 'Why the kit exists, and why a revved-up body makes every other tool harder.' },

  signalline: { t: 'Your signal line', m: ['signal line', 'return line', 'pause script'],
    s: 'Walking out of a room mid-argument without a word can look like walking out on the person, however you meant it. A signal line is one sentence that turns it into a pause.',
    f: 'For you, it’s agreed in advance so you don’t have to invent it while flooded. Between people: “I’m at capacity. I need ten minutes. I’ll be back at quarter past and we can pick this up.”',
    w: 'Its shape: say how you are, how long you need, and when you’ll be back. The return time is the part that matters; without it the sentence is a way out, not a pause. Both people agree ahead of time that hearing the line isn’t an insult or an escape.',
    x: 'Gottman’s research describes stonewalling, going silent or leaving without a word, as one of the patterns most damaging to relationships, and notes it often comes from being overwhelmed rather than uncaring. The signal line lets you take the break the overwhelmed body needs without sending the stonewalling message.\nIt also addresses a pattern couples researchers call demand–withdraw (studied by Andrew Christensen and others): one person pushes to talk, the other pulls away, and each move makes the other’s stronger. A clear return time gives the pursuing person something to hold, so they can let the other go.\nThe program ties it to numbers: the Kit re-reads your WP-02 battery before you return, and if you’re still at 0.60 or more after two rounds, you postpone to a named time rather than going back in.',
    u: '/wp-11-in-depth.html#defaults', l: 'WP-11: your signal line', lt: 'The shape of the line, and the agreement you both make about hearing it.' },

  returntime: { t: 'A return time', m: ['return time'],
    s: '“Tomorrow after dinner” is a return time. “Later” isn’t. One is a promise; the other leaves someone waiting.',
    f: 'For you, naming a clock time makes the pause easier to take. Between people, the other person doesn’t have to fill the silence with worry: “We’re okay. This is a pause. I’ll come back at 8:40.”',
    w: 'A flooded body often needs at least twenty minutes before it can hear again, and the return shouldn’t slip past the next day. When you come back, come back to the same topic, and don’t add a new one as interest on the delay. If your body isn’t back yet, say so and set a new time.',
    x: 'The twenty-minute figure comes from the Gottman Institute, whose lab work on physiological flooding found people generally needed at least that long for their bodies to settle before they could engage well again. Check-ins cites it and adds the next-day limit, so a pause never turns into avoidance.\nThere’s also an anxiety angle. Uncertainty is harder on many people than bad news, and research on intolerance of uncertainty suggests an open-ended wait can feel worse than a defined one. A clock time turns an open loop into a closed one.\nIn the program, the return is checked with a number: rescore WP-02 before going back. Under 0.50, go at the time you said; 0.50 to 0.60, one more round; still 0.60 or more after two rounds, name a new time.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#regroup', l: 'Check-ins: how to regroup', lt: 'The return line word for word, and what does and doesn’t belong in a regroup.' },

  reentry: { t: 'The re-entry threshold', m: ['re-entry threshold'],
    s: '“I feel better” can be a guess. The re-entry threshold uses your battery number to decide when you’re ready to go back into a talk.',
    f: 'For you, it protects you from going back in too soon and blowing up round two. Between people, it makes the return reliable: the other person knows you’ll come back when you actually can.',
    w: 'Rescore WP-02 before going back. Under 0.50: go back at the time you named. 0.50 to 0.60: do a second round of your default. Still 0.60 or above after two rounds: postpone and name a specific time. Then do one small, single-focus task first, like putting the dishes away, to check your calm holds.',
    x: 'This is a simple decision rule with a before-and-after measurement, the same structure clinicians and coaches use when they check a baseline, try something, and re-measure. Using the identical WP-02 scale for both readings is what makes the comparison meaningful; a different scale would be comparing apples with pears.\nThe thresholds (0.50 and 0.60) are practical choices, not research cut-offs. They sit just below and at the WP-02 “high” band, so the rule reads: don’t go back into a hard talk while your battery is in the red.\nThe small-task step is a test run. It’s low-stakes and single-focus, so if you snap at the dishes, you’ve learned something without anyone getting hurt.',
    u: '/wp-11-in-depth.html#reentry', l: 'WP-11: coming back', lt: 'The before-and-after table, the three bands and the small first task.' },

  postpone: { t: 'Postponing, not avoiding', m: ['postponement', 'avoidance'],
    s: 'A talk about the in-laws keeps getting pushed to “sometime.” The difference between postponing and avoiding is one thing: a named time.',
    f: 'For you, postponing with a time is care, not escape. Between people, naming it takes the dread out for both: “Can we set Thursday at seven for this? I don’t want it hanging over us.”',
    w: 'The program gives good reasons to postpone: a high battery score, a room that isn’t ready, a re-entry number that hasn’t come down. But WP-02 says a high score is a postponement tool, not an exit, and WP-11 says a kit used to make hard conversations never happen has become the problem it was meant to solve.',
    x: 'Psychologists distinguish strategic delay from avoidance. Avoidance brings short-term relief and tends to make the feared thing loom larger over time, a pattern well described in anxiety research. A dated postponement gets the relief without the growing dread, because the topic has a slot.\nThe Library’s motivation page adds that procrastination is often short-term mood repair: we put off what makes us feel bad now. A calendar time is the simplest counter.\nWP-13 exists partly to catch the difference: a friction that keeps appearing in the daily check-in but never gets a check-in of its own is avoidance showing up in the data, and REPORT-01 flags it as “The loop isn’t feeding forward.”',
    u: '/wp-11-in-depth.html#reentry', l: 'WP-11: coming back', lt: 'Putting a talk off honestly, with a specific time, when the numbers say not yet.',
    ctx: { '/check-ins.html': 'Here it’s a rule for the room: if one condition is missing, postpone with a return time. That isn’t avoidance.', '/workpapers/wp-13-pll-protocol.html': 'Here the warning runs the other way: turn the daily loop into criticism and people start avoiding it.', '/library/motivation.html': 'This theme explains procrastination as short-term mood repair, which is exactly why a named time helps.' } },

  box: { t: 'Box breathing', m: ['box breathing', 'Box breathing'],
    s: 'Picture tracing a square: breathe in for four, hold for four, out for four, hold for four. That’s box breathing.',
    f: 'For you, the steady count gives a busy mind one simple job. Between people, it’s a quiet way to settle before a planned talk, or to wind down afterward, without anyone needing to know.',
    w: 'It slows you to a few breaths a minute. In the Night Garden a glowing light keeps the count and a flower blooms each round. It differs from the Calm-Down Kit’s asymmetric breathing, where the out-breath is longer; use whichever feels easier. It’s a calm practice, not a treatment.',
    x: 'Box breathing is widely taught to people who need to stay steady under pressure, including military personnel and first responders, which is where many people first hear of it. Four counts per side makes about sixteen seconds per breath, so roughly four breaths a minute if the count is a steady second.\nThe holds are what make it different. They lengthen each cycle and give attention something precise to track, which is part of why some people find it calming and others find the holds uncomfortable. If holding feels tense, a long exhale without holds is a good alternative.\nThe Night Garden also explains the wider idea: slow, steady breathing is one of the most reliable ways to settle the body, and pairing it with a gentle visual makes it easier to keep going.',
    u: '/night-garden.html', l: 'Breathe in the Night Garden', lt: 'A glowing light keeps the count for you, and a flower blooms every round.' },

  regulatefirst: { t: 'Regulate first, negotiate second', m: ['regulate first, negotiate second', 'regulate first', 'autonomic regulation'],
    s: 'Ever tried to sort out a problem the minute you walked in the door, and it went badly? The same talk an hour later, after food and a sit-down, takes half the time. Settle first, then talk.',
    f: 'For you, it means your body gets a say in timing. Between people, a calm body can hear a hard sentence; a revved-up one hears an attack even in a careful one. It holds for partners, parents and kids, and coworkers.',
    w: 'It runs through the whole system: check-ins start with a state check, the Calm-Down Kit comes before the treaty session and the Tone Filter, and CALC-01 asks you to check your state before reading any number. This is Pillar III, Read your state first.',
    x: 'The About page says this rule came from experience: thinking tools worked until the body was past its limit, and then did nothing. It matches what emotion researchers find: strong physiological arousal narrows attention and makes flexible thinking harder, which is exactly what negotiation needs.\nParenting and teaching use the same order, sometimes summarized as “regulate, relate, reason” (associated with Bruce Perry’s work): settle the body, reconnect, and only then work on the problem.\nIn the program’s numbers, “regulated” has a practical meaning: on the ladder, the calm and connected rung; on WP-02, a battery under 0.50 before going back into a hard talk.',
    u: '/five-pillars-in-depth.html#read-your-state', l: 'The Five Pillars: read your state first', lt: 'Why state comes before words, inside you and between you.' },

  /* ---------- The nervous system ---------- */

  nervous: { t: 'Your nervous system', m: ['nervous system'],
    s: '“Are you free Saturday?” sounds like an invitation on a rested morning and one more demand after a bad night. The words didn’t change. Your nervous system did.',
    f: 'For you, knowing this ends a lot of self-blame: shutting down isn’t a choice, gearing up isn’t aggression. Between people, it explains why the same sentence can land three different ways depending only on the body hearing it that day.',
    w: 'It’s the part of you that speeds up for danger and slows down for rest, mostly without asking. The program treats its states as information, not character. The Nervous System Ladder and Today’s Weather give you a quick way to name where yours is before a hard moment. This is Pillar III, Read your state first.',
    x: 'The autonomic nervous system runs things you don’t decide: heart rate, breathing, digestion, alertness. It has two main branches. The sympathetic branch mobilizes you (faster heart, sharper focus, ready to act), and the parasympathetic branch, carried largely by the vagus nerve, slows things for rest and repair. They work more like a balance than an on-off switch.\nThe About page calls this the field that explained the founder to himself: learning how it controls state ended years of treating his own responses as character defects.\nThe program’s three-state ladder is a simplified, everyday map of this system. The site is careful that it’s a set of labels, not a measurement, and that the more detailed theory it borrows from (polyvagal theory) is debated.',
    u: '/book/chapter-3-in-depth.html#ladder', l: 'Chapter III: the three everyday states', lt: 'The three states in plain words, where the labels come from, and what each one is good for.',
    ctx: { '/relationships.html': 'In any relationship the first step is the same: a quick check can show a 4pm snap was leftover stress, not about them.', '/check-ins.html': 'Here it’s why the bond line matters: left unsaid, a nervous system fills the silence with a worse story.', '/library/stress.html': 'This theme has the stress-response details; the program uses them only as everyday labels.' } },

  ladder: { t: 'The Nervous System Ladder', m: ['Nervous System Ladder', 'the ladder'],
    s: 'Before a talk about the holidays, one of you says “I’m gusty,” the other “I’m clear.” You take a short walk first. That thirty-second check is the ladder.',
    f: 'For you, it tells you what this moment is good for, without grading you. Between people, each names their own rung, never the other’s. If either is revved up or shut down, it isn’t the moment for a hard talk.',
    w: 'Three rungs: calm and connected at the top, revved up in the middle, running on empty at the bottom. Today’s Weather uses the same three as Clear, Gusty and Fogged in, so you never need the technical words. Check-ins, CALC-01 and the Carrier Wave Decoder all start with it.',
    x: 'The three rungs line up with names from polyvagal theory, proposed by Stephen Porges in the 1990s: ventral vagal, sympathetic and dorsal vagal. Therapist Deb Dana popularised the ladder image. Parts of the theory, especially its claims about how the vagus nerve evolved and works, are debated by scientists, so the book uses the three states only as everyday labels, not as medical facts.\nA less contested cousin is Dan Siegel’s window of tolerance: a zone where you can feel strong feelings and still think, with revved up above it and shut down below. Know Your Own Wiring uses that picture too, and says it’s useful rather than measured.\nIn the program, the ladder is the fast check and WP-02 the fuller one. A calm rung and a battery under 0.50 is the practical meaning of “ready to talk.”',
    u: '/book/chapter-3-in-depth.html#ladder', l: 'Chapter III: the three everyday states', lt: 'The three rungs, where the names come from, and why the theory behind them is debated.' },

  ventral: { t: 'Ventral: safe and social', m: ['ventral vagal', 'Safe and social'],
    s: 'A slow Sunday morning, both rested: “Can we talk about summer plans?” “Sure, now’s good.” That easy state is the top rung, calm and connected.',
    f: 'For you, breath is easy, shoulders aren’t braced, and you can hear something hard without defending yourself. Between people, it’s the best window for the conversations that matter, so it’s worth using rather than waiting for a crisis.',
    w: 'It’s the top rung of the ladder, Clear in Today’s Weather. Nobody lives here all the time, and that’s fine. The point isn’t to stay on the top rung; it’s to notice when you’re there. You never need the technical term.',
    x: 'In polyvagal theory this is called the ventral vagal state, linked to what Porges called the social engagement system: the face, voice and listening working together when a person feels safe. That specific mapping is one of the debated parts of the theory, so the program keeps it as a label.\nWhat’s well supported more generally is that feeling safe and relaxed goes with more flexible attention, more generous interpretation of others and better listening. Barbara Fredrickson’s broaden-and-build research, for example, found positive states widen the range of thoughts and actions people consider.\nIn practice, the program reads this state as: Clear on Today’s Weather, a talk window that’s open, and a WP-02 battery comfortably under 0.50.',
    u: '/book/chapter-3-in-depth.html#ladder', l: 'Chapter III: the three everyday states', lt: 'The calm rung alongside the other two, with what each one is good for.' },

  sympathetic: { t: 'Sympathetic: revved up', m: ['sympathetic activation', 'sympathetic', 'mobilized'],
    s: 'Running late, someone asks where the car keys went, and your answer comes out sharp. You’re not angry at the question; your body is already sprinting. That’s the revved-up state.',
    f: 'For you: talking faster, interrupting sooner, small things suddenly urgent or personal. Between people, a calm “no rush, I’ll look too” helps more than pointing out the tone. Logic tends to bounce off right now.',
    w: 'It’s the middle rung of the ladder, Gusty in Today’s Weather, and “wound up” in Mood Arbitrage. It isn’t aggression; it’s a body doing its job. A long out-breath, slowing down or a pause helps before any real talk.',
    x: 'The sympathetic nervous system is the “fight or flight” branch, a phrase from physiologist Walter Cannon in the early 1900s. It releases adrenaline within seconds, raises heart rate and sharpens attention toward threats. That’s helpful for a deadline or a near-miss in traffic and much less helpful for hearing nuance.\nChapter I notes what it does to conversation: pace speeds up, and a neutral pause can be read as stonewalling. The Library adds the slower stress system (the HPA axis and cortisol), which is why a stressful morning can still be coloring the evening.\nIn the program’s numbers, this state often shows up as a WP-02 battery in the medium or high band. The Kit’s re-entry rule (under 0.50) is a practical test that it has passed.',
    u: '/book/chapter-3-in-depth.html#ladder', l: 'Chapter III: the three everyday states', lt: 'The revved-up rung in context, and what helps before any real talk.' },

  dorsal: { t: 'Dorsal: shut down', m: ['dorsal vagal', 'shut down', 'shutdown'],
    s: '“I don’t know. Whatever.” A blank face, few words, a body that feels heavy. That flat, far-away state is the bottom rung: shut down.',
    f: 'For you, it’s not calm and it’s not a choice; it’s a body that has run out of steam for now. Between people, fixing things won’t help yet. Steady company will: “The dishes aren’t going anywhere. I’m not upset. I’ll make us some tea.”',
    w: 'It’s the bottom rung of the ladder, Fogged in in Today’s Weather. During a shutdown, stop asking questions, keep your voice low, and offer a later time. Check-ins is clear that going blank is dysregulation too, not the same as calm.',
    x: 'Polyvagal theory calls this the dorsal vagal state and describes it as an older “immobilisation” response. This is the most debated part of the theory, since critics dispute the anatomy it rests on, so the program treats it as a label for an experience many people recognize rather than as settled science.\nThe experience itself is well documented under other names: shutdown, freeze, or being below Dan Siegel’s window of tolerance. Wired Differently notes autistic shutdown can look similar, and that guidance there is to avoid questions and instructions until the person has recovered.\nIn Mood Arbitrage, the matching move is “steady company”: no demands, no fixing, just letting someone know they’re not in trouble and not alone.',
    u: '/book/chapter-3-in-depth.html#ladder', l: 'Chapter III: the three everyday states', lt: 'The shut-down rung, where the name comes from, and why the theory is debated.',
    ctx: { '/five-pillars.html': 'In the Five Pillars this is one of the three states Pillar III asks you to notice before you judge a moment.', '/library/': 'Across the Library themes, this is Pillar III: know if you’re calm and connected, revved up, or running on empty before you judge a moment.', '/signal-translator.html': 'Here it’s a stop sign: if the listener shuts down or stops replying, pause and name a time to come back.', '/check-ins.html': 'Here it’s a rule: if either of you is fogged in, now isn’t the time for the topic.' } },

  vagalbrake: { t: 'The vagal brake', m: ['vagal brake'],
    s: 'Ever noticed your own shoulders drop when someone sits beside you and just breathes slowly? Moods are catching, and calm is too.',
    f: 'For you, it means you can only lend calm you actually have, so settle yourself first. Between people, a lower voice, a slower pace and steady company give the other person room to settle as well.',
    w: 'Mood Arbitrage builds on this: settle yourself, read their state, offer one small move that matches, then stay with it for a few minutes without rushing back to the topic. The real point still gets said later, at a set time, when you’re both steadier.',
    x: '“Vagal brake” is Stephen Porges’s term for the vagus nerve’s steady slowing effect on the heart, which can ease off to let heart rate rise and re-engage to bring it down. It’s part of polyvagal theory, which is debated, so treat the name as a picture rather than a mechanism the program depends on.\nThe better-supported idea underneath is emotional contagion, studied by Elaine Hatfield, John Cacioppo and Richard Rapson: people tend to pick up each other’s expressions, voices and moods, often without noticing. Developmental research on co-regulation between babies and caregivers points the same way.\nThat’s why Mood Arbitrage’s first step is about you, not them: your own steadiness is the thing that can spread.',
    u: '/tools/mood-arbitrage-free.html', l: 'Try Mood Arbitrage', lt: 'The four steps for helping a tense moment soften, with a full worked example.' },

  dysregulation: { t: 'Dysregulation', m: ['emotional dysregulation', 'dysregulation'],
    s: 'Your voice getting faster than the topic deserves, “always” and “never” arriving, or going blank and wanting to disappear. That’s dysregulation: your feelings or body getting ahead of you.',
    f: 'For you, the skill isn’t never getting there; it’s noticing early. Between people, name your own signs, not theirs: “I’m leaving the conversation. My voice is up. Back in twenty.” Diagnosing it in someone else as a way to win only adds heat.',
    w: 'Check-ins lists early signs: a voice faster or louder than the topic; the same sentence said again; a label where a fact was; a second topic brought in to win the first; jaw, heat, a tight chest; going blank or short; reaching for old messages as evidence. Shutdown counts too.',
    x: 'Emotion regulation is a large research field. James Gross’s process model describes regulation as happening at different points: choosing the situation, changing it, shifting attention, reinterpreting it, and changing the response. The earlier you step in, the less effort it takes, which is why the program focuses on early signs.\nDysregulation isn’t a diagnosis here. Everyone gets there sometimes, and some wirings get there faster, including ADHD (where emotion can run hot) and trauma-related hypervigilance. The program describes, never diagnoses.\nIn practice, the response is always the same shape: pause with a return time, settle using your Kit defaults, rescore WP-02, and come back to the same topic.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#signs', l: 'Check-ins: notice it early', lt: 'The full list of early signs, and what regulating before the next sentence looks like.' },

  flooded: { t: 'Flooded', m: ['emotional flooding', 'flooded'],
    s: 'Ever been so overwhelmed mid-argument that you couldn’t follow what was being said? That’s flooding.',
    f: 'For you, a single deep breath isn’t a plan when you’re flooded; a pause is. Between people, say one line and then actually come back: “We’re okay. This is a pause. I’ll be back at 8:40.” The other person doesn’t follow or send a stack of texts.',
    w: 'A flooded body often needs at least twenty minutes before it can hear again, and the return shouldn’t slip past the next day. Use the pause to come back to yourself, not to prepare a better case. The timeout is for connection, not for lawyering up.',
    x: 'The term comes from John Gottman’s research, where flooding (formally, diffuse physiological arousal) was measured in the lab with heart rate and other signals during couples’ conflict discussions. When partners were flooded, their ability to listen and problem-solve dropped sharply. Gottman’s lab found it takes about twenty minutes for the body to settle, which is where the program’s figure comes from.\nFlooding is also why the program separates timing from content. A good sentence delivered to a flooded listener still tends to go badly, because they can’t take it in.\nIn the numbers, flooding usually means a WP-02 battery well into the high band. The Kit’s rule, still 0.60 or more after two rounds means postpone, exists for exactly this.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#regulate', l: 'Check-ins: regulating before the next sentence', lt: 'What to do the moment you notice you’re flooded, and what not to do with the pause.',
    ctx: { '/signal-translator.html': 'Here it’s a timing warning: a flooded listener can’t take in even a well-translated sentence, so pause first.', '/library/': 'This Library theme covers the research; the program’s own pause rules are in Check-ins.' } },

  coregulate: { t: 'Co-regulating', m: ['co-regulate'],
    s: 'After a loud bang sets someone on edge, you sit nearby and breathe slowly without saying much. A few minutes later, they can talk. That’s co-regulating.',
    f: 'For you, it’s a way to help without fixing. Between people, calm is catching: a steady voice, a slow breath and quiet company can help another body feel safe enough to ease off. It’s always offered, never forced; some people want closeness, others space first.',
    w: 'Wired Differently suggests it first when someone is activated, especially for trauma-wired people, whose nervous system may stay on watch. Slow breathing together, or the Calm-Down Kit, comes before talking. It doesn’t replace each person settling themselves; it helps alongside.',
    x: 'Co-regulation is a core idea in developmental psychology: babies can’t yet settle themselves, so caregivers do it with voice, touch and rhythm, and children gradually build their own self-regulation from that. Research on adults suggests the effect doesn’t disappear; close partners’ physiological states can move together, especially in calm, safe moments.\nEdward Tronick’s “still face” experiments showed how much infants rely on a responsive adult: when a parent’s face went blank, babies quickly became distressed, and recovered when the parent re-engaged. The adult version is subtler, but steadiness still spreads.\nThe program adds two guardrails: ask first (“stay close, or give you space?”), and never make co-regulation someone else’s job. Each person still has their own Kit.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#trauma', l: 'Wired Differently: trauma and hypervigilance', lt: 'How a nervous system that stays on watch receives words, and what helps first.' },

  /* ---------- Hard conversations: Check-ins ---------- */

  checkins: { t: 'Check-ins', m: ['Check-ins'],
    s: '“While you’re walking out the door” is an ambush. “Can we use twenty minutes at 8, after dinner, for the kitchen thing?” is a check-in.',
    f: 'For you, it’s a way to raise the topic that’s been waiting without it becoming a fight. Between people, it’s short, agreed and about one thing: twenty to forty minutes, settled bodies, a named time to stop and come back. Families, roommates and coworkers can use the same order.',
    w: 'The order: name the container (time, place, one topic, the stop rule, the return); check states; the speaker gives one fact and one impact; the listener mirrors before any rebuttal; switch; a solution that works for both, with an owner and a review; close with one specific appreciation. The 90-second daily check-in is the small version.',
    x: 'Check-ins puts three published methods into one order: Speaker-Listener from the PREP program (Howard Markman, Scott Stanley and Susan Blumberg), Imago dialogue (Harville Hendrix: mirror, validate, empathise) and the Gottman Institute’s conflict skills. Each has its own research base, and the evidence is strongest for structured communication training like PREP; Imago has less independent testing. Check-ins borrows the steps, not any claim that one method is proven best.\nThe design choices follow from research on conflict. Gottman’s lab found that harsh starts predict harsh endings, that flooding stops listening, and that repair matters. So the check-in softens the start, builds in pauses, and ends on a concrete plan.\nIt can’t replace a clinician or a mediator, and it scores no one. What it adds to the program is a container: the place where a repeated friction from WP-13 or a capacity issue from WP-04 gets its proper conversation.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#ground', l: 'Check-ins: the ground rules', lt: 'The full safe-room method, from ground rules and the room to acknowledgement, rebuttal and a plan for both.' },

  groundrules: { t: 'The ground rules', m: ['check-in ground rules', 'ground rules'],
    s: 'A talk about a missed pickup starts drifting into last year’s vacation. The ground rules are what pull it back: “That’s real. It isn’t this meeting.”',
    f: 'For you, they’re short enough to say out loud before you begin. Between people, a hard topic is allowed; overload, blame and criticism of the person are not. The speaker says, once: “I am not against you. I don’t think you meant harm.”',
    w: 'The rules: one topic (a second gets parked with a date); no blame and no criticism of the person; no assumed bad intent on either side; prepare so the room isn’t doing the sorting; notice dysregulation early and pause with a return time. If it heats up, stop and come back to the same topic.',
    x: 'Facilitators and mediators set ground rules for the same reason: people behave better when the rules were agreed before emotions ran high. The Library notes that ground rules are a form of meta-communication, talk about how you’ll talk, and that making them explicit helps when safety feels fragile.\nEach rule targets a pattern from research. One topic prevents what Gottman called kitchen-sinking. No criticism of the person addresses the first of his “Four Horsemen.” No assumed bad intent counters hostile attribution bias. Pausing early respects the twenty-minute flooding finding.\nThe rules are the same across the program: WP-13’s daily loop, WP-09’s tone check and the Signal Translator all point back to them, so the habits carry from small talks to big ones.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#ground', l: 'Check-ins: ground rules', lt: 'Each rule with the exact words to say, for speaker and listener.',
    ctx: { '/library/': 'The Library looks at why agreed rules help; Check-ins gives the program’s actual rules to use.', '/know-yourself.html': 'Here the rules are the bridge from knowing your own wiring to talking with someone else about theirs.' } },

  onetopic: { t: 'One topic', m: ['One topic'],
    s: 'If you have five things to raise, you have a list, not a meeting. One topic is the simplest ground rule and the hardest to keep.',
    f: 'For you, it means picking the one that, if settled, would make the others smaller. Between people, it keeps both bodies from overloading, because an overloaded body hears criticism even in a careful sentence.',
    w: 'Before you sit down, sort everything into three piles: today (one item), a system (a job with no owner, which goes to WP-03 or the weekly catch-up) and can wait (with a named date). The urgency you feel is often fear of leaving something unfinished, not proof that every item is equal.',
    x: 'Gottman’s term for the opposite is kitchen-sinking: throwing every grievance into one argument. His lab found it makes conflicts much harder to resolve, because nothing can be finished and each new item raises the heat.\nCognitive research explains part of why. Working memory holds only a few items at once, and stress shrinks it further, so a many-topic conversation outruns what either person can track, especially someone with ADHD or someone flooded.\nThe program turns sorting into a system. Structural items go to the treaty, repeated frictions get triaged in the weekly catch-up, and the Signal Translator’s load note warns when your talking notes grow past one or two sentences.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#ground', l: 'Check-ins: ground rules', lt: 'How to sort today, a system and can-wait before you sit down.',
    ctx: { '/relationships.html': 'Here the rule is tailored per relationship: roommates, for example, start with the numbers rather than with who is late.', '/wired-differently.html': 'For a highly sensitive listener this rule matters extra: a soft start, a quiet room and one topic.', '/workpapers/wp-13-pll-protocol.html': 'Even in 90 seconds it applies: if a second topic shows up, set it aside for the weekly catch-up.', '/wp-11.html': 'After settling, this is the rule that stops your new calm being used to deliver the criticism you paused.' } },

  notagainst: { t: '“I am not against you”', m: ['not against you', 'not against them'],
    s: 'Before raising the phone bill: “I’m not against you, and I don’t think you meant anything by it.” The other person’s shoulders drop, and the talk stays about the bill.',
    f: 'For you, it’s a sentence that says what you actually mean before the hard part. Between people, leaving it unsaid lets the other person’s nervous system write a worse story than you meant.',
    w: 'The full version: “I am not against you. I am not saying you are the problem. I do not think you meant harm. I want this one thing sorted.” The listener can say the other half: “I’m not taking this as an attack until I’ve mirrored it.” Once is enough; repeating it after every sentence starts to sound like a script.',
    x: 'This is what researchers call a safety cue. Studies of threat perception show that ambiguous social situations are more likely to be read as hostile when people are stressed, and that explicit reassurance can reduce that bias. Saying your intent up front removes the ambiguity before it can be filled in.\nIt also sets up the rest of the check-in. Gottman’s lab found that the first few minutes of a conflict conversation strongly predict how it ends; a line that lowers threat at the start makes a soft start-up easier to hear.\nThe line appears across the program for that reason: in Check-ins, WP-09’s tone check, WP-13’s ground rules and the Signal Translator’s suggested openers.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#ground', l: 'Check-ins: ground rules', lt: 'The speaker’s line and the listener’s line, word for word.',
    ctx: { '/relationships.html': 'In every relationship on this page the same line helps: say once, before the topic, that you’re not against them.', '/signal-translator.html': 'The translator’s suggested openers include this line, with a time to come back if either of you pauses.', '/workpapers/wp-09-tone-filter.html': 'After your three lines, this is what to add out loud: you want one thing fixed, and you’re not against them.' } },

  parkit: { t: 'Parking a topic', m: ['Park it', 'park the rest', 'parked'],
    s: '“And you never plan anything for weekends.” Halfway through a talk about the dishes. Parking it sounds like: “That’s real. It isn’t this meeting. I’m writing it down for Sunday.”',
    f: 'For you, it lets a second topic be taken seriously without derailing the first. Between people, the person who raised it isn’t wrong, just early. On Sunday, the weekend topic gets its own twenty minutes.',
    w: 'A parked item needs a date: one with no date is a dismissal wearing good manners. And don’t explain why their point matters less; just say it isn’t this meeting. Keep a small “parked” list and look at it at your weekly closing.',
    x: 'Meeting facilitators call this a “parking lot”: a visible list for good points that don’t belong to the current agenda. It works because it acknowledges the point (so the person doesn’t have to keep pushing it) while protecting focus.\nThe research reason is the same as for one topic: stacking topics raises load and heat. There’s also a memory effect sometimes called the Zeigarnik effect, the tendency for unfinished tasks to stay on our minds. Writing a parked item down, with a date, is one way to let it rest.\nIn the program, parked items often turn out to be structural. When the same one is parked twice, it probably belongs in the RACI Treaty or the Deficit Audit rather than in another talk.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#ground', l: 'Check-ins: ground rules', lt: 'How to park a second topic without dismissing the person who raised it.' },

  complaint: { t: 'Criticism or complaint', m: ['criticism of the person', 'complain, don\'t blame', 'complain, don’t blame'],
    s: '“You never help. You’re careless.” versus “The sink was full at 7. I need it cleared by 9.” One is about a person, the other about an event. That’s the difference between criticism and a complaint.',
    f: 'For you, a complaint is something you can actually get fixed tonight. Between people, a verdict on someone’s character can only be defended against; an event can be owned and changed.',
    w: 'Gottman’s softened start is the line: complain, don’t blame. Describe what happened, don’t judge the person, and start from what you need. Tone doesn’t turn one into the other: “I’m not angry, but you always…” is criticism in a calm voice. If a sentence needs a label, it isn’t ready: take it through WP-09.',
    x: 'John Gottman named criticism as the first of his “Four Horsemen,” four patterns (criticism, contempt, defensiveness and stonewalling) that his longitudinal studies of couples linked with relationships ending. His distinction is precise: a complaint targets a specific behavior; criticism adds a global statement about the person’s character, often with “always” or “never.”\nThe Horsemen research comes mostly from couples, and the prediction figures from Gottman’s lab have been debated by other researchers, but the basic pattern, that attacks on character escalate conflict, is widely supported.\nThe program builds the distinction into its tools. WP-09’s fact line has no adjectives, the Signal Translator flags labels, and REPORT-01 promises it will never contain a judgment about either person.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#ground', l: 'Check-ins: ground rules', lt: 'The criticism rule with examples, and how to turn a verdict into a fact and an ask.',
    ctx: { '/signal-translator.html': 'In the translator, the fact goes in the facts box and the complaint becomes the phrase, so a label never gets scored as the message.', '/workpapers/wp-13-pll-protocol.html': 'In the daily loop, the friction line is a complaint, never a criticism: one small event, no verdict.' } },

  softstart: { t: 'A softened start', m: ['softened start', 'Soften the start', 'soft start'],
    s: '“Why is there never any milk?” versus “I noticed we ran out of milk again. Could we add a spare to the list?” The second is a softened start.',
    f: 'For you, it means writing your first sentence before you say it. Between people, a soft start lets the other person stay open; a harsh one puts them on guard from the first word. For a highly sensitive listener it matters even more.',
    w: 'Three habits: complain without blaming, describe without judging, speak from “I” rather than “you always.” Start from what you need, not from what they are. It’s the first of Gottman’s six skills that Check-ins draws on.',
    x: 'Gottman’s lab reported that the way a conflict discussion began, especially in the first three minutes, predicted how it would end in a large share of cases. That finding comes from one lab’s studies of couples, but it’s one of the more consistent results in the field, and it explains why the program works so hard on the first sentence.\nThe “I” part goes back to psychologist Thomas Gordon’s “I-messages” in the 1970s, and Rosenberg’s Nonviolent Communication makes a similar move by leading with an observation and a need.\nThe program gives you tools to prepare the start: WP-09’s fact, feeling and ask, the Signal Translator for testing the wording against the listener’s wiring, and talking notes so the sentence is written before anyone is waiting.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#order', l: 'Check-ins: the order, and why', lt: 'Where the soft start sits in the order, and the three methods it comes from.' },

  intentimpact: { t: 'Intent and impact', m: ['Intent does not cancel impact', 'impact from intent'],
    s: 'A joke about someone’s cooking in front of friends: meant lightly, and it stung. What you meant and how it landed can both be true.',
    f: 'For you, it takes the pressure off proving you’re a good person. Between people: “I didn’t mean harm. I can see it landed as harm. I’m not asking you to cancel that.” The hurt is allowed to be real.',
    w: 'Say the no-harm line before the fact, not after they flinch, and save the defense of your intent until they’ve been heard. Otherwise it’s the rebuttal arriving early. Most static between differently wired people is a missed signal, not a plot.',
    x: 'Communication models since Shannon and Weaver separate what was sent from what was received, and psychologists add that people judge themselves by intentions and others by actions, part of a broader pattern called the actor–observer asymmetry. Each person is, honestly, reading a different thing.\nThat’s why “I didn’t mean it that way” so often backfires: it answers the sender’s question when the receiver asked a different one.\nThe program’s double-empathy angle adds to this. When two people are wired differently, both intent and impact can be accurate at once, and the fix is comparing notes, not deciding who’s right. The Signal Translator helps you predict impact before speaking; Check-ins helps you acknowledge it after.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#ground', l: 'Check-ins: ground rules', lt: 'The three-part intent-and-impact line, and when to say it.' },

  saferoom: { t: 'A room that can hold it', m: ['A safe time', 'A safe environment', 'A stable connection', 'The room has to be safe'],
    s: '10:50pm, both nearly empty, phones buzzing, in the cluttered kitchen that is also the topic. The words were fine; the room was wrong.',
    f: 'For you, fixing the room is often easier than fixing the words. Between people, the same talk moved to the sofa after dinner, phones in another room, can feel completely different.',
    w: 'Four conditions: a safe time (named ahead, not in or just after a spike, not when someone is leaving); a safe environment (private, phones face down, no children listening); outside factors handled (hunger, pain, alcohol, a deadline); and a stable connection (a short signal the bond is okay). If one is missing, postpone with a return time.',
    x: 'Environmental psychology has long shown that surroundings shape behavior: noise, crowding, heat and poor light raise stress and irritability. For some wirings, like highly sensitive or autistic people, the room can use up much of their capacity before a word is said.\nThe outside-factors list reflects the everyday “HALT” reminder (hungry, angry, lonely, tired) plus substances. Check-ins is firm: if either person has been drinking or is otherwise altered, the check-in waits.\nThe program connects this to its numbers. Today’s Weather gives a talk window (open, wait or closed) that mostly follows the sky reading, and a WP-02 battery over 0.60 is itself a reason the room isn’t ready.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#room', l: 'Check-ins: the setting comes first', lt: 'The four conditions in detail, including what to do when one is missing.' },

  bondsignal: { t: 'A bond signal', m: ['bond signal', 'bond line', 'the bond is ok'],
    s: '“We are okay. This is one topic, not a verdict on us. I’m not leaving this.” Some people can’t hear a hard fact until they’ve heard that first.',
    f: 'For you, it might be exactly what you need before a hard talk, or not at all. Between people, one may need the bond confirmed while the other needs a little space first. Both can be true in one pair.',
    w: 'It goes before the topic, if welcome. A hug or a hand is optional, never owed, and off if anyone doesn’t want it or you’re not in private. For an anxious listener, say it once and mean it; repeating it after every sentence starts to sound like a script.',
    x: 'Attachment theory, begun by John Bowlby and Mary Ainsworth, describes how people look for signs that a close person is available and responsive, especially under stress. Adult researchers often describe two dimensions, attachment anxiety (worry about being left) and avoidance (discomfort with closeness), and people high in anxiety tend to find silence during conflict especially hard.\nSue Johnson’s Emotionally Focused Therapy frames many couple fights as an underlying question, “are you there for me?” A bond signal answers that question directly, before the practical topic.\nThe program pairs it with the opposite need. Wired Differently’s two conflict needs note that some people need space before closeness, so the signal is offered, briefly, and followed by a pause and a return time.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#room', l: 'Check-ins: a stable connection', lt: 'How to give a bond signal that’s welcome, and when to leave it out.' },

  speakerlistener: { t: 'Speaker-Listener', m: ['Speaker-Listener'],
    s: '“I felt rushed because the lunches weren’t made.” “What I heard: the morning felt rushed because lunches weren’t ready. Did I get that?” That back-and-forth is Speaker-Listener.',
    f: 'For you, it slows a hard talk down so you’re heard before anyone answers. Between people, the listener isn’t preparing a rebuttal while the other is still talking. Practice it on dinner plans before using it on something loaded.',
    w: 'The speaker talks in short pieces, about fifteen to thirty seconds. The listener paraphrases, asks if they got it, and only then do roles switch. It’s one of three published methods Check-ins puts in one order, with Imago dialogue and Gottman’s six skills.',
    x: 'The technique comes from PREP (the Prevention and Relationship Education Program), developed by Howard Markman, Scott Stanley and colleagues at the University of Denver, and described in Fighting for Your Marriage. Check-ins cites the Family Institute at Northwestern for the method. PREP has been tested in many studies, with generally positive but modest effects that vary by group, which is about what you’d expect from any communication training.\nThe short pieces are the key design feature. Short turns keep the listener’s working memory from overflowing, and stop either person from stockpiling a case.\nIt pairs with active listening, the practice Carl Rogers and Richard Farson described in the 1950s: reflect the content and feeling back before responding.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#order', l: 'Check-ins: the order, and why', lt: 'All three methods in one order, and why acknowledgement comes before any rebuttal.' },

  gottman: { t: 'Gottman’s six skills', m: ['Gottman\'s six skills', 'Gottman’s six skills'],
    s: 'A vacation-plans talk heats up. One of you says, “Sorry, that came out sharp. Let me try again.” That repair is one of six research-based conflict habits.',
    f: 'For you, pick one to practice at a time. Between people: soften the start, accept influence, repair if it spikes, soothe yourself, soothe the other if welcome, and compromise.',
    w: 'They come from the Gottman Institute and are one of the three methods Check-ins puts in order. Turning Toward draws on the same research for everyday closeness: bids, love maps, and fondness and admiration. The site notes the studies mostly involve couples and applies the ideas more widely.',
    x: 'John Gottman and Robert Levenson began observing couples in a laboratory (nicknamed the “Love Lab”) in the 1970s and 1980s, measuring conversation, facial expression and physiology, then following couples for years. From that work came the Four Horsemen, the flooding finding and the emphasis on repair.\nThe research is influential and also debated. Critics have questioned how well some headline prediction figures hold up in new samples, and most participants were couples. The program uses the skills as practical habits, not as proof of any prediction.\nEach skill has a place in the program: soft start in WP-09, repair in retuning and the apex score, self-soothing in the Calm-Down Kit, accepting influence and compromise in Check-ins’ solution for both.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#order', l: 'Check-ins: the order, and why', lt: 'The six skills alongside Speaker-Listener and Imago, in one working order.' },

  acknowledgement: { t: 'Acknowledgement', m: ['acknowledgement before any rebuttal', 'proof of receipt', 'acknowledgement', 'Mirror first', 'mirrored'],
    s: '“What I heard is: the dishes were still there this morning, and it felt like the evening was left on you. Did I get that?” That’s acknowledgement: proof the message arrived.',
    f: 'For you, it’s not agreement, so you don’t give anything up by doing it. Between people, it changes the whole talk: “I hear you, but” cancels everything before it; use “and.” Stay until they say you have it; two or three short turns is normal.',
    w: 'If they correct you, mirror the correction without defending the first version. It works differently across wirings: for an autistic person, a literal repeat is respect, not coldness; for ADHD, a one-line written mirror can help; for a trauma-wired person, slow down and ask “was that close?”',
    x: 'Carl Rogers called this reflective or active listening: restating what someone said, and sometimes how they seem to feel, before responding. Imago dialogue’s first step, mirroring, is the same move with a stricter script.\nIt works partly because feeling understood reduces defensiveness. Research on “perceived responsiveness” (Harry Reis and colleagues) links feeling understood, valued and cared for with closer, more satisfying relationships. The mirror is the fastest way to show the first of those.\nThe Library adds a finding from apology research: acknowledging responsibility was rated the most important part of an apology, above offers of repair. Mirroring is a small, everyday version of that acknowledgement.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#acknowledge', l: 'Check-ins: acknowledgement', lt: 'How to mirror, how to take a correction, and how it changes for different wirings.',
    ctx: { '/signal-translator.html': 'Here the aim is the same: acknowledgement isn’t agreement, and hearing how it landed doesn’t cancel your fact.', '/library/': 'This Library theme covers the research; Check-ins shows the exact mirroring steps.' } },

  validation: { t: 'Validation', m: ['validation', 'validating before arguing'],
    s: '“If I came down to a job I thought was done, I’d be frustrated too.” You can say that and still see it differently. That’s validation.',
    f: 'For you, it’s one honest way their reaction makes sense. Between people, it comes after the mirror, not instead of it. “You’re too sensitive” and “that isn’t what happened” do the opposite: they shut listening down.',
    w: '“That can make sense” means their reading is possible from the facts. It doesn’t mean you intended harm, and it doesn’t mean their reading is the only one. Don’t hand someone a feeling they didn’t name: “You said it landed as being left with it” is safer than “you seem hurt.”',
    x: 'Psychologist Marsha Linehan, who developed Dialectical Behavior Therapy, described six levels of validation, from simply paying attention up to treating someone as a capable equal. The middle levels, reflecting accurately and saying how a reaction makes sense given someone’s history or situation, are what Check-ins asks for.\nResearch on emotional invalidation, being told your feelings are wrong or overblown, links it with more distress and more escalation in the moment. That’s why the program lists “you’re too sensitive” as something to avoid.\nFor someone with alexithymia, who finds it hard to name their own feelings, being handed a feeling-word can feel like a correction. Validation that stays with their own words is the safer version.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#acknowledge', l: 'Check-ins: acknowledgement and validation', lt: 'Mirror, then validate, then empathy if you can do it without inventing a feeling.' },

  rebuttal: { t: 'A proper rebuttal', m: ['proper rebuttal', 'a rebuttal'],
    s: '“I got the part about last night’s dishes. My part: I said I’d do them and didn’t. One fact to add: I did the lunch boxes.” That’s a rebuttal that doesn’t erase what you just heard.',
    f: 'For you, accuracy matters, and you’re allowed to add your side. Between people, the other person hears their point survived. What isn’t allowed is a rebuttal that pretends the acknowledgement never happened.',
    w: 'Four moves, in order: restate their fact in one line; own your part without a discount; add one fact they may not have (one event, a time, no “always”); separate impact from intent, then stop. If it needs a label like “lazy” or “too much,” it’s a verdict, not a rebuttal.',
    x: 'The shape borrows from structured debate and from negotiation. Restating the other side first, sometimes called steelmanning or, in Rapoport’s rules for criticism (popularised by philosopher Daniel Dennett), re-expressing their position so clearly they wish they’d put it that way, shows you’re arguing with what they said, not a caricature.\nOwning your part without a discount is the opposite of defensiveness, Gottman’s third Horseman. Adding one event with a time keeps the new information checkable, the same rule as WP-01’s audit rule.\nAfter a rebuttal, be quiet. Their turn is another acknowledgement, not an instant verdict, so the conversation keeps its order instead of collapsing into point-scoring.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#rebuttal', l: 'Check-ins: a proper rebuttal', lt: 'The four moves with a before-and-after example.' },

  acceptinfluence: { t: 'Accepting influence', m: ['Accept influence', 'accepting influence'],
    s: 'You want to repaint the living room this weekend. They say weekends are their only rest. You ask what rest looks like, and paint one wall on Saturday morning. That’s accepting influence.',
    f: 'For you, it means letting their view actually change your plan, not just listening politely. Between people, a solution that meets only the faster or louder person’s need gets called peace and felt as a loss.',
    w: 'It’s one of Gottman’s skills and the heart of a solution that works for both: write both needs down first, then find one move that meets the minimum of each, not the maximum of the louder one.',
    x: 'In Gottman’s research, accepting influence (being open to a partner’s opinions and letting them shape decisions) was linked with more stable relationships. One widely cited 1998 study found this especially for husbands in different-sex couples. As with much of this research, it comes from couples and a particular era, so the program applies the principle rather than the statistic.\nNegotiation research reaches the same place from another direction. Fisher and Ury’s Getting to Yes distinguishes positions (what someone says they want) from interests (why they want it), and finds that agreements built on both sides’ interests hold better.\nIn Check-ins this becomes a rule of order: both needs are written and mirrored before anyone proposes a solution.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#both', l: 'Check-ins: a solution that works for both', lt: 'How to write both needs down, and the five parts every agreement needs.' },

  bothsides: { t: 'A solution for both', m: ['both-sides solution', 'A solution that works for both'],
    s: '“You need the kitchen not to be a surprise. I need the evening close not to be a test. The move: a note on the fridge. Owner: me. We look again Sunday.” That’s a solution for both.',
    f: 'For you, it’s the difference between “I feel heard” and “something will actually change.” Between people, it meets the minimum of each need, not the maximum of one. Some topics take more than one sitting, and a seven-day experiment with a date isn’t failure.',
    w: 'Five parts, or it’s just a wish: need A in their words, mirrored; need B in their words, mirrored; one action covering the minimum of each; a named owner and a clock time; a review, like the next weekly closing. If it’s really about ownership, move it to WP-03.',
    x: 'Negotiation theory calls this integrative bargaining: looking for options that create value for both sides instead of splitting a fixed pie. Fisher and Ury’s Getting to Yes (1981) made it famous with four principles: separate the people from the problem, focus on interests, invent options for mutual gain, and use fair criteria.\nThe five-part check adds what project managers insist on: an owner, a date and a review. Without those, even a good agreement tends to evaporate.\nThe review is where the program’s numbers come back in. A change that holds shows up as fewer frictions in the daily loop and, over a month, fewer repeats in the Deficit Audit.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#both', l: 'Check-ins: a solution that works for both', lt: 'The five parts, with examples, and why an experiment with a date counts.' },

  negpos: { t: 'Turning a negative into a positive', m: ['negative into a positive', 'Negatives to positives'],
    s: 'The sink with no owner after 9 becomes a ten-minute close together, or a note on the fridge. That’s turning a miss into one small habit that works.',
    f: 'For you, it means a bad evening wasn’t wasted. Between people, you keep the cost visible and name one thing the other person already does well. “Thank you for saying it as a fact instead of as a verdict on me.”',
    w: 'Keep the cost in plain words; ask what the miss was pointing at (a need, a missing owner, a tired time of day, a hint that should have been an ask); turn it into one repeatable action; name one thing that already works; put the action where the program can see it, like the treaty or the weekly catch-up.',
    x: 'Teams in the military, aviation and healthcare use the after-action review: what was supposed to happen, what actually happened, why, and what we’ll do differently. The format works because it treats a miss as information about the system, not a charge against a person, the same stance as Pillar II.\nThe “one thing that already works” step draws on research into positive and negative interactions. Gottman’s lab reported that stable couples kept many more positive than negative moments even during conflict (the often-quoted ratio is about five to one), though the exact ratio is debated.\nThe positive has to be an action, not a mood. “A written close of the kitchen” is checkable in next week’s notes; “try to be more considerate” isn’t.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#leverage', l: 'Check-ins: turning a negative into a positive', lt: 'The steps for converting a miss into one repeatable action, with example words.' },

  toxicpos: { t: 'Not toxic positivity', m: ['toxic positivity'],
    s: '“It’s fine, don’t worry about it!” said through gritted teeth. A pile of dishes doesn’t become a gift because you rename it.',
    f: 'For you, it means you don’t have to pretend. Between people, the problem stays visible; what changes is the mood around it, so the real point can land. “That was hard, and I’m glad we’re talking calmly. What would help next time?”',
    w: 'Mood Arbitrage and Check-ins both say it plainly: settle the room, keep the fact. The opposite warning applies too: don’t sand the fact down into “a little miscommunication.” If you leave with only a compliment, the cost was hidden and it will come back.',
    x: 'Emotion researcher James Gross distinguishes reappraisal (changing how you see a situation) from suppression (hiding what you feel). His studies found suppression tends to cost more: it takes effort, doesn’t reduce the inner experience much, and can make the other person in a conversation feel less connected. Toxic positivity is suppression in a cheerful voice.\nResearch on “emotional acceptance” (for example by Brett Ford and colleagues) suggests people who accept rather than judge their negative feelings tend to do better over time, which supports keeping the cost in plain words.\nThe program’s version: name the problem in one plain sentence, then add something warm. The Signal Translator can check that the warm part hasn’t swallowed the fact.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#leverage', l: 'Check-ins: keeping the cost visible', lt: 'How to shift the mood without hiding the problem.' },

  regroup: { t: 'Regrouping', m: ['regroup'],
    s: '“I’m back. The topic is still the sink. I’m not against you. My state is clear now.” That’s how a pause ends: a regroup.',
    f: 'For you, it’s a way back in that doesn’t start a second fight. Between people, you pick up exactly where you left off, in a calmer body, starting again from the fact.',
    w: 'A regroup is not a rematch. Don’t open with what they did during the pause, a new topic you thought of while away, or a better-prepared case. If your body isn’t back yet, say so and set a new time; the clock arriving isn’t the same as being ready.',
    x: 'Researchers studying conflict describe repair as a normal, expected part of close relationships, not a sign of failure. Developmental psychologist Edward Tronick’s work on parents and babies is a striking example: interactions were frequently out of sync and then repaired, and the repairing itself seemed to matter for the relationship.\nThe regroup is the adult, planned version. Check-ins’ return line restates the topic (so drift can’t sneak in), restates the stance (not against you) and reports your state, the same three things the program checks at the start of any hard talk.\nThe “not ready yet” option connects to the Calm-Down Kit’s re-entry rule: rescore WP-02, and if you’re still 0.60 or more after two rounds, set a new time.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#regroup', l: 'Check-ins: how to regroup', lt: 'The return line, what doesn’t belong in a regroup, and what to do if you’re not ready.' },

  repair: { t: 'Repair', m: ['repair'],
    s: 'The morning after a sharp word: “Last night I said you never listen. What I meant was I felt rushed. Next time I’ll ask for five minutes.” That’s repair.',
    f: 'For you, it’s how you come back after getting it wrong, which everyone does. Between people, specific repair works better than a general promise: the sentence you wish you’d used, and one change for next time.',
    w: 'Repair is one of Gottman’s six skills and a situation you can pick in the Signal Translator. CALC-01’s apex score counts it, because a home can share work evenly and still not be repairing. After a talk squeals anyway, the Carrier Wave Decoder helps with the moment it slipped. This is Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive.',
    x: 'In the Gottman Institute’s research, repair attempts (anything that stops a conversation escalating: a joke, an apology, “let’s slow down”) were a key difference between couples who did well and those who didn’t. Their work also found that whether a repair attempt lands depends a lot on the goodwill built up beforehand.\nEdward Tronick’s studies of parents and infants suggested that everyday interaction is often out of sync, and that the repeated experience of mismatch followed by repair helps build trust. Relationships don’t need to be free of ruptures; they need reliable repair.\nIn numbers: retuning frequency (retunes ÷ rough moments, capped at 1.00) feeds the apex score with weight 0.15. When solvency is 0.08 or more above apex, REPORT-01 says “Structure holding, repair not.”',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#translator', l: 'Check-ins: where the Signal Translator sits', lt: 'How the translator and the Decoder fit around a check-in, including repair afterward.',
    ctx: { '/calc01-solvency.html': 'Here repair is the fourth input to apex: of this week’s rough moments, how many you retuned first.', '/book/chapter-2.html': 'The chapter’s point: solvency asks if the load can last; apex asks if repair is happening too.', '/workpapers/report-01.html': 'Watch the gap between solvency and apex here: 0.08 or more either way is a named finding.', '/library/': 'This Library theme looks at repair from research; the program’s own repair tools are WP-09 and the Carrier Wave Decoder.', '/conversation-reader.html': 'The Reader looks for repair attempts in a thread, like apologies and softening lines, and shows where they happened.' } },

  talkingnotes: { t: 'Talking notes', m: ['talking notes'],
    s: 'Ever gone into a hard talk with ten things in your head and said the worst one first? Talking notes are the few sentences you write down before anyone is waiting.',
    f: 'For you, they turn a swirl into one or two clear lines. Between people, you start with one sentence, stop, and let them say what they heard. You don’t owe the whole list.',
    w: 'You build them in the Signal Translator and save one page to take into the room. The load note keeps it kind: one sentence is light; two can fit if both are small; four is a list, not a conversation. Never read a score out loud.',
    x: 'Preparing what you’ll say lowers the load on working memory during a stressful conversation, the same reason speakers use notes and pilots use checklists. Under stress, retrieval gets harder and the most emotionally loaded item tends to come out first.\nThe build follows the program’s pipeline: write everything you’re tempted to raise; sort it into today, a system and can wait; set up both people and the room in the Signal Translator; translate each sentence; read the load note; save the PDF.\nWhat the notes contain is also filtered. Structural items go to the treaty, not the talk. Scores stay at home, because a number read out mid-talk becomes a verdict.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#notes', l: 'Check-ins: how to build the talking notes', lt: 'Step by step from a messy list to one page you can take into the room.' },

  stonewalling: { t: 'Stonewalling', m: ['stonewalling'],
    s: 'Going silent, turning to your phone, walking out without a word. The other person is left talking to a wall. That’s stonewalling, and it’s often overwhelm, not punishment.',
    f: 'For you, it may be the only way your body knows to stop. Between people, it reads as walking out however it’s meant, and for someone who fears being left, silence can feel like the start of it.',
    w: 'The fix isn’t forcing yourself to keep talking. It’s leaving with a sentence: name your state, how long you need, and when you’ll be back. That turns a wall into a door. The other side matters too: a revved-up listener can read an ordinary pause as stonewalling when it isn’t.',
    x: 'Stonewalling is the fourth of Gottman’s Four Horsemen. His lab found it often followed flooding: the person who withdrew frequently had a high heart rate, and pulling away was an attempt to self-soothe. The antidote he describes is physiological self-soothing with a break, then returning.\nCouples researchers also describe the demand–withdraw cycle (Andrew Christensen and others): one person pursues, the other withdraws, and each makes the other’s move more likely. Naming the pause and a return time interrupts the cycle from both ends.\nIn the program, the signal line is the tool, the Calm-Down Kit is where you prepare it, and the re-entry rule decides when you’re ready to come back.',
    u: '/wp-11-in-depth.html#defaults', l: 'WP-11: your signal line', lt: 'How to agree a line in advance so a pause is never mistaken for a wall.' },

  defensiveness: { t: 'Defensiveness', m: ['defensiveness'],
    s: '“I know, I know, it was a terrible day and I just couldn’t…” said before anyone has asked anything. That’s defensiveness: protecting yourself before you’ve heard what’s being said.',
    f: 'For you, it usually comes from a body on guard, not from a bad character. Between people, you can ask for help with it: “If I start defending, stop me.” Or say it yourself: “Give me a second, I want to hear this properly.”',
    w: 'The Check-ins order takes the pressure off: mirror first, rebut later. If your answer is already crowding your listening, say so and take a minute. Mood Arbitrage shows how to soften the room so there’s less to defend against.',
    x: 'Defensiveness is the third of Gottman’s Four Horsemen, usually appearing as counter-attack, excuses or innocent victimhood. His suggested antidote is taking responsibility for even a small part of the problem, which is the “own your part” step in Check-ins’ rebuttal.\nPsychologists link defensiveness to threat: when a comment feels like an attack on who you are, the self-protective response kicks in. The Library notes research on guilt and shame: guilt (“I did a bad thing”) tends to lead toward repair, while shame (“I am bad”) tends to lead toward hiding, defensiveness or blame. That’s why the program keeps talk about events, not character.\nSelf-affirmation research (Claude Steele and others) suggests people are less defensive when they feel secure in their broader worth, one more reason the program pairs hard talks with fondness and specific thanks.',
    u: '/check-ins-in-depth.html#acknowledge', l: 'Check-ins: acknowledgement', lt: 'How mirroring first gives defensiveness less to push against.',
    ctx: { '/library/': 'The Library has the research on defensiveness; the program’s own antidote is mirroring first, in Check-ins.' } },

  contempt: { t: 'Contempt', m: ['contempt'],
    s: 'An eye roll, a sneer, a mocking tone, a sigh that says “here we go again.” Contempt sends one message: I’m above you.',
    f: 'For you, it often shows up when you’re exhausted and have stopped seeing the good. Between people, it lands harder than anger. The guard against it is remembering, on purpose, what you like and respect about someone.',
    w: 'Turning Toward notes Gottman’s research singles it out as the most damaging of the conflict patterns he studied. One caution from Wired Differently: an eye roll or grunt can be a tic, not contempt, especially with Tourette syndrome. Respond to the words, and if someone tells you it was a tic, believe them.',
    x: 'Contempt is the second of Gottman’s Four Horsemen, and in his longitudinal studies it was the single strongest predictor of couples later separating. It includes sarcasm, mockery, name-calling and hostile humor. As with the other Horsemen findings, the numbers come mostly from couples in one research program, but the pattern is widely recognized.\nThe antidote Gottman describes is a culture of fondness and admiration: deliberately noticing and saying what you value, so respect is the default. The Library adds that humor which mocks or belittles tends to slide into contempt, while shared laughter can be a strong repair.\nIn the program, the guard is structural as well as personal. Numbers never describe a person, REPORT-01 never compares the two of you, and the Stories page’s I and Thou warns that the moment the numbers become the person, the ledger has failed.',
    u: '/turning-toward-in-depth.html#fondness', l: 'Turning Toward: remember what you like about them', lt: 'Fondness and admiration as the everyday guard against contempt.',
    ctx: { '/library/': 'This Library theme covers the research on contempt; Turning Toward is where the program builds the antidote.' } },

  /* ---------- Tools: signals, frequencies and moods ---------- */

  carrier: { t: 'The carrier wave', m: ['Carrier Wave Decoder', 'carrier wave', 'carrier from the payload'],
    s: '“You never help” can be carrying “I’m exhausted and invisible.” The words are the payload; the tone carrying them is the carrier wave.',
    f: 'For you, answering the carrier instead of the words often changes the next line completely. Between people, most household friction isn’t about content; it’s a carrier the other person can’t receive. The Decoder works for any two people after a talk goes sideways.',
    w: 'The Carrier Wave Decoder walks through one moment that squealed: check your state, a one-minute retune (in for 4, out for 6), decode what was out of sync across pace, register and urgency, a tone check on your draft, and, if something keeps coming back, a finding with a plan. This is Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive.',
    x: 'In radio, a carrier wave is a steady signal at the station’s frequency, and the program (voice or music) is added to it by modulation: changing its strength (AM, amplitude modulation) or its frequency (FM, frequency modulation). Tune to the wrong carrier and the program never arrives, however good it is.\nChapter I uses that as a picture: every message rides on a tone, a pace and an urgency, and if those don’t reach the listener, the content doesn’t either. Linguists would call the carrier paralanguage, the how rather than the what.\nThe Decoder is a reflection aid, not a clinical tool, and measures nothing about either person. It keeps notes in your browser so you can come back, which means anyone using that browser could see them.',
    u: '/carrier-wave-decoder.html', l: 'Open the Carrier Wave Decoder', lt: 'One guided session for a moment that squealed: state, retune, decode, tone check, practice and findings.',
    ctx: { '/relationships.html': 'Between family members it works the same way: decode what was out of sync before arguing about whether something was.', '/privacy-policy.html': 'In this policy it’s named because it keeps your notes in your own browser, so you can come back to them.' } },

  findings: { t: 'Findings and action plans', m: ['corrective action plans', 'corrective action plan', 'Material weakness', 'Significant deficiency'],
    s: 'The same problem keeps coming back, and talking about it hasn’t fixed it. A finding is a calm, written way to name it and give it a plan.',
    f: 'For you, it turns a recurring sore spot into something with steps. Between people, a finding describes the arrangement, never a person, and the other person responds first in their own words: agree, partly agree or disagree.',
    w: 'Four levels, borrowed from audit: an observation (a one-off, just noted); a deficiency (could recur, quick fix with an owner); a significant deficiency (recurring or clearly felt, a full plan with a date and a test, reviewed weekly); a material weakness (your usual repairs aren’t catching it, reviewed monthly with WP-04, and a counselor is worth considering).',
    x: 'The levels come straight from internal-control auditing. In US public-company audits, a “significant deficiency” and a “material weakness” are defined terms for control problems of increasing seriousness, and auditors report them to boards. The Decoder borrows the ladder, not the legal definitions.\nThe four-part write-up, condition (what happened), criteria (what was expected), cause (what in the arrangement allowed it) and effect (what it cost), is the standard structure for audit findings in government auditing guidance. It forces fact and cause apart, and neither is allowed to be a character trait.\nA plan closes only after it has held through one monthly review, and closed findings become strengths. That’s the program’s version of verifying a fix rather than assuming it worked.',
    u: '/carrier-wave-decoder.html', l: 'Open the Carrier Wave Decoder', lt: 'The findings tab: four levels, the four-part write-up, and how a plan is closed.' },

  translator: { t: 'The Signal Translator', m: ['Signal Translator'],
    s: '“We need to talk later.” To an anxious listener, that can mean hours of bracing. The Signal Translator shows how a sentence may land before you say it.',
    f: 'For you, it’s a pre-flight check on your own next line. Between people, you set each person’s wiring, the relationship and the room, and see a version you’re both more likely to hear the same way. It’s never a score to read out at someone.',
    w: 'It looks for patterns in wording, like hints, sarcasm, vague timing and labels, that often cause static for particular wirings. Every reading says “may,” because people vary. It can’t hear tone, see faces or know your history, and it can’t diagnose anyone. Nothing you type is saved.',
    x: 'Under the hood it’s a rule-based checker: a set of written patterns, each linked to a wiring or situation where research or lived experience suggests that pattern tends to cause static. That’s closer to a grammar checker than to an AI that understands you, which is why it says “may” and scores the wording, never the person.\nThe patterns draw on the same sources as Wired Differently: literal processing and hint-reading in autism, working memory and time in ADHD, reading load in dyslexia, threat interpretation in anxiety, and the double empathy problem across all of them.\nIt fits the program’s order: WP-09 gives you fact, feeling and ask; the fact goes in the facts box, the ask becomes the sentence; the load note stops you packing four topics into one talk.',
    u: '/signal-translator.html', l: 'Try the Signal Translator', lt: 'Set both wirings, the relationship and the room, then test the sentence you plan to say.' },

  reader: { t: 'The Conversation Reader', m: ['Conversation Reader'],
    s: 'A text thread left you uneasy, but you can’t say where it went wrong. The Conversation Reader shows where it turned.',
    f: 'For you, it slows you down before replying. Between people, it shows what each of you may be hearing, and a calmer way to answer. It watches for things like good news met with a flat reply.',
    w: 'Paste a thread (a text chat, a chat export or an email exchange), or type a remembered talk as “Me:” and “Them:” lines. It marks where things warmed up, flags patterns like “always,” sarcasm, dismissals and repair attempts, and can check a reply as you draft it. It runs in your browser and nothing you paste is saved.',
    x: 'It uses pattern-matching on words, the same broad family as sentiment analysis, but with hand-written rules rather than a trained model. Each rule targets something relationship researchers have studied: absolutes and character statements (Gottman’s criticism), dismissals, apologies and other repair attempts, unanswered questions, and flat replies to good news (Shelly Gable’s work on responding to good news).\nIts limits are real and stated. It can’t hear tone, doesn’t know your in-jokes, and the same words can be affectionate between two people and cutting between two others.\nSo the program treats its output as a prompt, never evidence. Use it to slow down, never to prove who was right.',
    u: '/conversation-reader.html', l: 'Open the Conversation Reader', lt: 'Paste a thread and see where it warmed up, what each person may be hearing, and a calmer reply.' },

  freq: { t: 'Frequency Calibration', m: ['Frequency Calibration'],
    s: 'You like to check the budget weekly; they’d rather look monthly. Neither is wrong. Frequency Calibration maps how often each of you naturally wants to check in on five parts of life.',
    f: 'For you, it names your own natural pace for money, recovering from stress, deciding, talking and revisiting values. Between people, the gaps show up side by side, and each gap gets a simple agreed rhythm instead of a repeated argument.',
    w: 'Mismatched rhythms are information, not failure, and it isn’t a compatibility test or a prediction. For each gap it suggests a coherence mechanism, like a set money check or a named return time. Big gaps are normal in every kind of relationship. Nothing you type is saved.',
    x: 'Each area is a timescale. You write rhythms in plain terms (“every two days,” “weekly”), and the tool compares them. A 2-day and a 14-day rhythm are seven times apart: one person is ready to check in again long before the other is, and that waiting is where the friction lives.\nThe underlying idea, that people differ in preferred tempo, has support in several research areas: chronotype research on morning and evening types, work on decision speed and need for closure (Arie Kruglanski), and on how often people want contact in close relationships. The program combines these loosely into one practical map, and says it’s a rule-of-thumb worksheet, not a tested assessment.\nIt suggests starting with stress-recovery gaps, since those spill into every other area, and re-running it each season because jobs, health and life stages change people’s rhythms.',
    u: '/tools/frequency-calibration.html', l: 'Open Frequency Calibration', lt: 'Map both of your rhythms in five areas, then pick one agreement for each gap.' },

  framework: { t: 'The Frequency Framework', m: ['Frequency Framework'],
    s: 'Saturday morning: you want to decide about the vacation now; they want a few days. Neither is stalling or rushing. The Frequency Framework explains that clash.',
    f: 'For you, it reframes “we’re incompatible” as “we run at different speeds.” Between people, you don’t need to change your natural pace; you need an agreed time to meet, like deciding on Wednesday evening.',
    w: 'It treats a relationship as rhythms running at five speeds: financial, autonomic (recovery after stress), decisional, communicational and philosophical (revisiting values). The friction lives in the mismatch, called the beat frequency, and the fix is coherence mechanisms: agreed rhythms. This is Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive.',
    x: 'It extends Chapter I’s radio picture from single conversations to everyday life. The physics analogy is beat frequency (the wobble two close tones make) and coherence (waves keeping a steady relationship). The page is honest that these are pictures, not claims about physics or physiology.\nThere’s a lovely historical parallel. In 1665 Christiaan Huygens noticed two pendulum clocks on the same beam gradually swinging in step. Physicists call this entrainment: coupled rhythms can synchronize without either being forced to change its nature. Agreed check-ins work like the shared beam.\nThe Framework connects to the program’s tools: Frequency Calibration measures your five rhythms, the 90-second loop is the daily coupling, and the monthly audit catches drift the daily scale can’t see.',
    u: '/frequency-framework-in-depth.html#paradigm', l: 'The Frequency Framework: the radio picture', lt: 'The paradigm, a live visual of two rhythms drifting, then all five frequencies.' },

  fivefreq: { t: 'The five frequencies', m: ['five frequencies', 'Financial Frequency', 'Autonomic Frequency', 'Decisional Frequency', 'Communicational Frequency', 'Philosophical Frequency'],
    s: 'After a work crisis, one of you wants to talk straight away and the other needs an hour of quiet. One feels abandoned; the other feels pressured. That’s two recovery rhythms clashing.',
    f: 'For you, seeing five separate rhythms helps you spot where the friction really lives. Between people, a gap in one area says nothing about the others: a couple can match perfectly on money and clash on decisions.',
    w: 'Financial: how often bills and spending are checked. Autonomic: how long your body takes to reset after stress. Decisional: how long you need before committing. Communicational: how often you need to check in to feel secure. Philosophical: how often you want to revisit whether your values still line up.',
    x: 'Each rhythm has a research cousin. Autonomic recovery connects to stress-physiology work on how quickly heart rate and mood return to baseline, which varies a lot between people. Decisional pace connects to research on need for cognitive closure. Communicational pace connects to attachment research, where people higher in attachment anxiety tend to want more frequent reassurance.\nThe Framework doesn’t claim a single study behind the five; it’s a practical grouping of areas where timing clashes are common. Its value is diagnostic: “communicate better” is vague, while “our reset times differ by an hour” points to a specific agreement.\nThe calibration tool suggests tackling autonomic gaps first, then communication, then decisions, money and values, because an unresolved recovery clash colors all the rest.',
    u: '/frequency-framework-in-depth.html#five', l: 'The Frequency Framework: the five frequencies', lt: 'Each frequency with an everyday example of what a mismatch feels like.' },

  beat: { t: 'Beat frequency', m: ['beat frequency', 'beat frequencies'],
    s: 'Your 90-minute reset collides with their need to check in every ten minutes, and you get the “why won’t you talk to me?” fight. That clash is a beat frequency.',
    f: 'For you, it removes the shame: you’re not incompatible, your rhythms are out of step. Between people, it makes solutions obvious: a mismatch in reset times needs a reset agreement, not “communicate better.”',
    w: 'The Framework’s point: the real friction isn’t in either rhythm, it’s in the gap between them. Knowing that removes the shame, makes the fix clearer, and lets you predict friction before it happens, like moving weekend planning from Friday night to Thursday lunch.',
    x: 'In acoustics, two tones at frequencies f₁ and f₂ produce a beat at |f₁ − f₂|. Musicians use this to tune: as two strings approach the same pitch, the beats slow down and vanish when they match. Piano tuners listen for exactly this.\nThe Framework borrows the insight that the beat belongs to the pair. It isn’t a property of either tone, and it can’t be fixed by making either tone “better,” only by bringing them into a shared relationship.\nIn the program, that shared relationship is a coherence mechanism: a spoken agreement about when, how and how often you sync up. Frequency Calibration suggests one for each gap it finds.',
    u: '/frequency-framework-in-depth.html#beat', l: 'The Frequency Framework: where the squeal comes from', lt: 'Beat frequency with everyday examples, and why knowing your rhythms matters.' },

  coherence: { t: 'Coherence mechanisms', m: ['coherence mechanisms', 'coherence mechanism'],
    s: 'A text at lunch, twenty quiet minutes after getting home, then ten minutes of catch-up before dinner. Both needs have a slot. That’s a coherence mechanism.',
    f: 'For you, it’s permission to keep your own pace. Between people, it’s a simple spoken agreement about timing, so two different speeds stop producing friction. Unspoken rhythms drift; scheduled ones hold.',
    w: 'Examples from around the site: a weekly money check, a set day to decide, a named return time after a pause, the daily 90-second check-in. The trick is to say it out loud, agree on it, try it for a couple of weeks, then review.',
    x: 'In physics, waves are coherent when they keep a constant phase relationship, which is what makes lasers and interference patterns possible. Engineers get separate systems into coherence by giving them a shared reference, like a clock signal.\nThe everyday version is a shared schedule. Behavioral research supports why: agreed, cue-linked routines (Wendy Wood’s habit work, Gollwitzer’s if-then plans) are followed far more reliably than good intentions, and they don’t depend on anyone’s mood on the day.\nThe program’s own coherence mechanisms nest by timescale: the daily loop, the weekly closing and the monthly Deficit Audit. Frequency Calibration adds custom ones for your particular gaps.',
    u: '/frequency-framework-in-depth.html#beat', l: 'The Frequency Framework: agreeing when you’ll meet', lt: 'Why you don’t need to change your speed, only agree when and how you’ll sync up.' },

  mood: { t: 'Mood Arbitrage', m: ['Mood Arbitrage'],
    s: 'You come home to a kitchen full of dishes and a roommate who’s had a terrible day. Mood Arbitrage is the gentle path between a lecture and a cold shoulder.',
    f: 'For you, it starts with settling yourself, because you can only lend calm you actually have. Between people, you read their state, offer one small move that matches it, and save the real topic for later. It works with a partner, family, a co-parent, a roommate, a coworker or a friend.',
    w: 'Four steps: settle yourself first; read their state (wound up, shut down or scattered); choose one move that matches; stay with it for a few minutes without rushing back to the topic. It isn’t forced cheerfulness, and it isn’t brushing their feelings aside. The real point still gets said, later, in a check-in.',
    x: 'In finance, arbitrage means taking advantage of a gap between two prices. The name here is playful: noticing the gap between the mood in the room and the mood the conversation needs, and closing it before talking.\nThe research underneath is emotional contagion (Elaine Hatfield and colleagues): moods spread between people through faces, voices and posture, often without anyone noticing. That’s why your own steadiness is step one.\nThe state names are loosely borrowed from polyvagal theory, which the page flags as popular but debated. Here they’re plain labels for what you notice, not measurements. The free page teaches the principle and one full example; a fuller version with a plan builder is in level 2.',
    u: '/tools/mood-arbitrage-free.html', l: 'Try Mood Arbitrage', lt: 'The core idea, three states to look for, the four steps and a complete messy-kitchen example.' },

  threemoods: { t: 'Three states to look for', m: ['Three Mood Frequencies', 'Helicopter Brain', 'Structural Containment', 'Priority Anchor', 'One thread at a time', 'Steady company'],
    s: 'Tight jaw and sharp words; a blank “whatever”; five worries spilling out in one breath. Three different states, and each needs a different kind of help.',
    f: 'For you, it’s a guess to make before you respond, and you can be wrong: if you can’t tell, ask. Between people, the wrong help makes things worse: a joke can feel like teasing to someone who’s shut down.',
    w: 'Wound up (on guard): help them feel you’re on the same side, and lower the temperature. Shut down (flat, far away): show they’re not alone, with no demands, through steady company. Scattered (too many threads): find the one next step, one thread at a time. Not sure: let them choose.',
    x: '“Wound up” and “shut down” are loosely borrowed from polyvagal theory’s sympathetic and dorsal states, which Mood Arbitrage flags as popular but debated. “Scattered” is just an everyday description. None are measurements or diagnoses.\nResearch on social support backs the idea of matching help to need. Carolyn Cutrona’s optimal-matching work and later studies find that help people didn’t want, like advice when they wanted comfort, can feel worse than no help. The three states are a quick way to guess which kind is wanted.\nThey line up with the program’s ladder (revved up, shut down) and with WP-13’s one-topic rule for the scattered state, so the same language carries from reading yourself to reading someone else.',
    u: '/tools/mood-arbitrage-free.html', l: 'Try Mood Arbitrage', lt: 'The three states with signs to look for, and a quick move finder.' },

  fourstep: { t: 'The four steps', m: ['Four-Step Sequence'],
    s: 'Settle yourself, read them, pick one move, stay with it. Four small steps for the moment someone close to you is tense.',
    f: 'For you, the first step is always you: a few slow breaths, or “I’ll come back in a few minutes.” Between people, the last step is the one most people skip: staying steady instead of rushing back to the point as soon as things ease.',
    w: 'Step 1: settle yourself first. Step 2: read their state without judging it; asking is a perfectly good move. Step 3: choose one move that matches (lower the temperature, steady company, one thread at a time, or ask first). Step 4: stay with it for a few minutes, and come back to the topic later at a set time.',
    x: 'The order mirrors the program’s rule, regulate first and negotiate second, applied to someone else. Caregiving research on co-regulation points the same way: a calm adult helps a child settle mainly through their own steadiness, not through explanation.\nStep 3’s idea, matching support to need, has research support: help that fits what someone wants tends to land well, and mismatched help can backfire.\nStep 4 protects the gain. Rushing back to the topic as soon as tension eases tends to bring it straight back. The page’s advice is to come back later, at a set time, in a check-in with one topic and no blame.',
    u: '/tools/mood-arbitrage-free.html', l: 'Try Mood Arbitrage', lt: 'The four steps with a table matching each state to a goal and a move.' },

  absurdity: { t: 'Lowering the temperature', m: ['Absurdity Pivot', 'Lower the temperature'],
    s: '“Rough day, huh? I think these mugs have staged a rebellion. Want company while we deal with them, or ten minutes first?” That’s lowering the temperature.',
    f: 'For you, it’s a way to show you’re on their side, not on the dishes’ side. Between people, it’s for someone wound up and on guard, never for someone shut down, where humor can feel like teasing.',
    w: 'Slow down, stand beside them, take the heat out. If light humor is normal between you, one warm line about the situation (never about them) can help. If it doesn’t land, drop the joke at once: “Sorry, not the moment. What would help?” Later, at a calm time, agree who owns the dishes on busy days (WP-03).',
    x: 'Researchers studying couples have found that shared, affectionate humor can work as a repair attempt and ease physiological tension, while hostile or mocking humor does the opposite and can slide into contempt. The difference is who the joke is about: the situation, or the person.\nThere’s also a benign-violation idea in humor research (Peter McGraw and Caleb Warren): things feel funny when something is a bit wrong but safe. A rebellion of mugs is safely absurd; a joke about someone’s bad day isn’t safe.\nThe move ends in a question for a reason. Offering a choice (“company, or ten minutes first?”) hands back control, which helps someone on guard stop defending and say what they actually need.',
    u: '/tools/mood-arbitrage-free.html', l: 'Try Mood Arbitrage: the messy-kitchen example', lt: 'The whole scene, what to do if they’re wound up, and what changes if they’re shut down instead.' },

  lemonade: { t: 'The Lemonade Stand', m: ['Lemonade Stand Heuristic', 'Lemonade Stand'],
    s: 'One person makes the lemonade, the other sells it. Both matter. But if only one keeps the books, the other can never know if it’s fair.',
    f: 'For you, it’s a weekly tally of your own hours, including the quiet jobs. Between people, two to eight of you each enter your own hours, and the split appears as a plain fact, not a verdict. Roommates can add the money side too: rent, bills, shared shopping.',
    w: 'List what it took to keep your place running this week, one line per job, with each person’s hours. It works best fed by a week of WP-01, so hours come from notes rather than memory. Its totals feed workload balance in CALC-01. Nothing leaves your device. This is Pillar I, See the whole load, with a nod to Pillar V.',
    x: 'The heuristic’s point is about information, not effort. Economists call this information asymmetry: when one party can see the full books and the other can’t, even honest people end up disagreeing about what’s fair. A shared tally removes the asymmetry.\nIt also counters a known bias. In Ross and Sicoly’s classic study, partners’ estimates of their own share of household tasks added up to more than 100 percent, because each remembered their own work more easily. Hours written down during the week are the fix.\nThe numbers flow on: the split becomes CALC-01’s balance (for two people with an even target, 1 − |pctA − pctB| ÷ 100), and over weeks REPORT-01 compares it with ownership clarity to spot “Clarity without parity.”',
    u: '/lemonade-stand.html', l: 'Open the Lemonade Stand', lt: 'Two to eight people, hours and optional money, with the split shown as plain bars.' },

  weather: { t: 'Today’s Weather', m: ['Today’s Weather', 'Today\'s Weather'],
    s: 'Some mornings are clear, some are gusty, some are fogged in, and it changes without asking your permission. Today’s Weather is a one-minute reading of your own inner weather.',
    f: 'For you, it tells you what today is good for and what to leave until the front passes. Between people, it words its advice for who you’ll mostly be around: a partner, family, a co-parent, roommates, coworkers, someone you care for or friends.',
    w: 'Only step one is required: pick the sky (Clear, Gusty or Fogged in). Optional steps add pressure, ground conditions (hunger, pain, noise, caffeine, screens), last night’s sleep, people load, what’s blowing in and what would help. The forecast gives good hours, a talk window, sixty seconds that might help, and a sentence you can copy. This is Pillar III, Read your state first.',
    x: 'It’s a rough tally, not a measurement. The sky sets the starting point, and heavy pressure, a short night, a lot of people time, body needs and open worries each add a little. A skipped step counts as a small, middle amount. Sleep counts for a lot, because a short night makes almost everything feel heavier.\nThe three skies are loosely borrowed from polyvagal theory’s three states, which the page flags as popular and still debated. The weather metaphor is deliberate: weather isn’t a grade or a verdict, and it passes.\nPsychologists would recognize the sleep emphasis from research linking sleep loss to stronger emotional reactions, and the “ground conditions” list from the HALT idea (hungry, angry, lonely, tired). An optional 7-day log, kept only in your browser, turns single readings into a pattern.',
    u: '/quick-checks.html#today', l: 'Check today’s weather', lt: 'The eight steps, the forecast, and how the reading is worked out.' },

  talkwindow: { t: 'The talk window', m: ['talk window'],
    s: 'Short night, heavy pressure, and you’re about to raise a relative’s visit. The talk window says: not today. Tomorrow after dinner, it’s open.',
    f: 'For you, a closed window doesn’t mean never; it means pick a better time. Between people, send a short message instead: “Can we talk about the visit tomorrow after dinner?”',
    w: 'Part of your Today’s Weather forecast. It mostly follows the sky: clear skies are good for talking, gusty skies say wait a little, and fog says leave it for another day. It pairs with the Check-ins idea that the room has to be safe before the topic is.',
    x: 'Timing research in close relationships is consistent on one point: the same conversation goes differently depending on the state people bring to it. Gottman’s flooding work and research on stress spillover both show that tired, stressed or already-activated people interpret and respond less generously.\nThe talk window turns that into a simple three-way rule rather than a score, which makes it easy to act on. It’s a heuristic: the page says it’s a rough tally of what you told it.\nIt connects to the rest of the program’s timing tools: the WP-02 battery bands (above 0.6, put off what can wait), the Calm-Down Kit’s re-entry rule, and Check-ins’ four conditions for a room that can hold a hard topic.',
    u: '/quick-checks.html#forecast', l: 'Today’s Weather: your forecast', lt: 'The forecast in full: good hours, what to leave, the talk window and a sentence to copy.' },

  peopleload: { t: 'People load', m: ['people load', 'peopling'],
    s: 'A teacher comes home after a day of nonstop talking and gives a one-word answer to a simple question. Not rudeness: the people tank is empty.',
    f: 'For you, even good company takes energy, and some days ask for a lot. Between people, telling the people at home your people load in one word when you walk in stops quiet being mistaken for distance.',
    w: 'Today’s Weather asks it as one step, from “barely any” to “nonstop, no gaps.” It sits alongside sleep and pressure because it quietly changes how much you have left for home. A heavy reading is information about a day, not a failing in a person.',
    x: 'Personality research has studied this under introversion and extraversion. Studies suggest that after sustained socializing, many people, not only introverts, report more fatigue a few hours later, and that the effect is stronger for some temperaments.\nWired Differently adds that some wirings spend more on each interaction: highly sensitive people take in more detail, and autistic people who mask spend extra effort monitoring eye contact, expressions and turn-taking. For them, people load can be the main reason an evening feels hard.\nIn the program’s numbers it’s one of the inputs that nudges the Today’s Weather reading up, and it often explains a WP-02 battery that looks higher than the day’s events alone would suggest.',
    u: '/quick-checks.html#h-people', l: 'Today’s Weather: people load', lt: 'The people-load step and how it feeds your forecast.' },

  almanac: { t: 'Your 7-day log', m: ['almanac'],
    s: 'One day of weather tells you about today. Seven days start to show your climate. That’s what the 7-day log is for.',
    f: 'For you, it can show which fronts keep coming back, like Monday money worries, and how often the heavy days follow a short night. It’s private: kept only in your browser, and you can erase it any time.',
    w: 'Save a day’s reading and the log shows a week of skies and pressure bars. One entry is a mood; several are a pattern memory alone can’t show you. Nothing is uploaded, and no one else can see it.',
    x: 'Researchers use a similar method called experience sampling or daily diaries: short, repeated check-ins that capture patterns people can’t reliably recall later. Memory tends to be dominated by the most intense and the most recent moments (what Daniel Kahneman called the peak-end rule), so a daily record gives a fairer picture.\nThe privacy design is deliberate. The log uses your browser’s local storage, never a server, and the page warns that on a shared computer anyone using that browser could see it.\nIn the wider program, the same logic runs through REPORT-01: numbers recorded week by week so the picture builds up instead of resetting.',
    u: '/quick-checks.html#almanac', l: 'Today’s Weather: your 7-day log', lt: 'The log, the pattern it shows, and how to erase it.' },

  wired: { t: 'Wired differently', m: ['Wired Differently', 'neurotypes', 'neurotype'],
    s: 'You text “Fine.” and mean it literally. They read the full stop as a door slamming. Same word, two different receivers.',
    f: 'For you, it’s a way to understand how your own brain takes words in. Between people, a mix-up usually runs both ways, so you compare notes instead of looking for blame. It applies to partners, family, friends and coworkers alike.',
    w: 'The guide covers wirings that are neurodevelopmental (autism, ADHD, dyslexia, dyspraxia, auditory processing differences, developmental language disorder, Tourette), traits across groups (alexithymia, high sensitivity), conditions that change how words land (anxiety, trauma, OCD), plus neurotypical wiring. It describes tendencies, not any one person, and can’t diagnose anyone. This is Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive.',
    x: 'It adds a second layer to Chapter I’s radio picture: even on the same station, receivers are built differently. The organizing idea is the double empathy problem (Damian Milton, 2012): misunderstandings between very differently wired people are mutual, not a deficit inside one of them.\nThe page is careful with evidence, naming sources and flagging where research is early or mixed. For example, it reports both an early study finding that mixed autistic and non-autistic chains lost story details faster and a larger repeat that didn’t find that extra loss, but did find the people got on less easily.\nThe word neurodiversity is usually credited to sociologist Judy Singer in the late 1990s. The program’s use is practical: wiring is information to understand and explain, not a flaw to hide, and the best source on how someone receives words is that person.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#double-empathy', l: 'Wired Differently: the problem runs both ways', lt: 'The double empathy problem, then fourteen wirings, each with how it receives words and how to speak to it.' },

  card: { t: 'The Wiring Card', m: ['Wiring Card'],
    s: '“If I go quiet, it means I’m thinking, not upset. Please write down anything with more than two steps.” A few lines like that, swapped on a calm day, can save weeks of guessing.',
    f: 'For you, it’s a short card about how you take in words: how you receive things best, what your silence means, what to avoid, how a request lands. Between people, it tells someone how to send so it arrives. It works at home, with friends and at work.',
    w: 'It isn’t a diagnosis, a label or a contract, and it changes with your state: when you’re depleted, every line gets stricter. Make it before a hard conversation, not during one. It doesn’t oblige anyone to manage you. Nothing you type is saved.',
    x: 'Some workplaces use a similar idea called a “user manual” or “working with me” document, where each person writes how they prefer to receive information and feedback. The Wiring Card is the personal, gentler version.\nIts design follows the double empathy idea: instead of one person guessing how the other’s mind works, each describes their own. That swaps inference, which is error-prone across different wirings, for direct information.\nThe “when I’m depleted, tighten this” line connects wiring (lasting) to conditions (passing), the distinction at the heart of Know Your Own Wiring. The card pairs naturally with the Signal Translator, where you can set the same wiring details before testing a sentence.',
    u: '/wiring-card.html', l: 'Make your Wiring Card', lt: 'Fill in a short card about how you take in words, then print or copy it to share.' },

  report: { t: 'REPORT-01: The Full Read', m: ['REPORT-01', 'The Full Read', 'compilation layer'], cs: true,
    s: 'Each worksheet sees one thing well and is blind to the rest. REPORT-01 is where they sit side by side, week after week.',
    f: 'For you, a minute a week builds a picture memory can’t. Between people, it shows where the figures agree, where they contradict, and which difference matters, without ever judging either of you.',
    w: 'Each week you type in what the worksheets produced: WP-01 minutes, WP-02 battery sums, WP-03 owned and listed jobs, WP-09 friction and retunes, WP-13 loops, Kit use and treaty changes. Each month you add the Deficit Audit figures. Your ledger is a file you save and reload; nothing is stored in the browser.',
    x: 'In accounting terms it’s a consolidation: separate ledgers combined into one statement so contradictions between them become visible. WP-01 can’t see whether the load is owned; WP-03 can’t see whether it’s shared; together they can.\nEvery finding is a written rule, listed on the page, for example: clarity without parity (ownership 0.85 or more while balance is 0.60 or less), the drift rule (apex down more than 0.10 three weeks running), treaty gone quiet (six or more weeks with no changes), and the habit is holding (the daily loop on five or more days a week for three weeks).\nIt promises never to contain a judgment about either person, a comparison between you, or advice about the relationship’s future. It isn’t a tested clinical or statistical tool, and if a finding is used to end a conversation, it’s being misused.',
    u: '/workpapers/report-01-in-depth.html#report', l: 'REPORT-01: the Full Read', lt: 'How weeks and months are added, the report itself, and every rule it uses.',
    ctx: { '/workpapers/report-01.html': 'On this page you add a week in about a minute, then save the ledger file somewhere private.' } },

  reportfindings: { t: 'The Full Read’s findings', m: ['Clarity without parity', 'Fixes aren’t holding', 'Fixes aren\'t holding', 'Treaty gone quiet', 'Invisible work surfacing'],
    s: 'The treaty is crystal clear, but one person still does most of the hours. Two worksheets, two different stories. The Full Read’s findings notice exactly that.',
    f: 'For you, each finding points to one lever to try next. Between people, it gives you a neutral sentence to start from: not “you don’t do enough,” but “our clarity is high and our balance is low.”',
    w: 'Examples: clarity without parity; structure holding, repair not; repair carrying the structure; load isn’t the driver; the drift rule; fixes aren’t holding; treaty gone quiet; the loop isn’t feeding forward; kit doing its job; invisible work surfacing; the habit is holding.',
    x: 'Each rule is a threshold on two or more figures: clarity ≥ 0.85 with balance ≤ 0.60; solvency more than 0.08 above apex (or the reverse); stress rising three or more weeks while balance stays flat; apex down more than 0.10 three weeks running; more than half of fixed gaps coming back; six or more weeks with no treaty change; friction logged three or more weeks with nothing moved on; the Kit used in a week stress fell; clarity falling while the task list grows; the loop on five or more days a week, three weeks running.\nSoftware engineers would call this a rule engine, and auditors use the same idea when they test figures against fixed, published expectations: every result can be checked by hand.\nThe most surprising one is “Invisible work surfacing.” A falling clarity score usually looks bad, but when the task list grew at the same time, it means hidden work finally got named, which is the score working as it should.',
    u: '/workpapers/report-01-in-depth.html#method', l: 'REPORT-01: how this report is written', lt: 'Every finding, the exact rule behind it, and which worksheets it reads from.' },

  recurrence: { t: 'Recurrence rate', m: ['recurrence rate'],
    s: 'The recycling got a new owner in March, April and May, and it keeps slipping. Re-fixing isn’t the same as fixing. Recurrence rate measures the difference.',
    f: 'For you, it stops a falling gap count from looking like progress when it isn’t. Between people, it moves the question from effort to design: maybe collection day falls on the owner’s longest workday.',
    w: 'REPORT-01 records it from the monthly Deficit Audit: how many of last month’s fixed gaps came back. Above 50 percent across the recorded months means the same gaps are being patched instead of closed, so the structure needs another look: the owner, the timing, or whether the job needs splitting.',
    x: 'Recurrence rate = gaps that came back ÷ gaps fixed. Service teams track the same idea as a reopen rate, and quality engineers watch it as a sign that a fix treated a symptom rather than a root cause.\nThe threshold, more than half across the recorded months, is a practical line, not a research finding. It triggers the “Fixes aren’t holding” finding, which reads from WP-04.\nIt works together with the structural gap count: a falling count with low recurrence is real progress; a falling count with high recurrence suggests problems are being moved around. Toyota’s “five whys” is a good way to dig into a recurring gap before reassigning it again.',
    u: '/workpapers/report-01-in-depth.html#monthly', l: 'REPORT-01: add a month', lt: 'What gets recorded each month, and how recurrence is worked out.' },

  prog: { t: 'PROG-01: the 6-week program', m: ['PROG-01', '6-Week Program', 'six gentle weeks'],
    s: 'Not sure where to start, and a bit overwhelmed by all the pages? PROG-01 walks you through it, one week at a time, for six weeks.',
    f: 'For you, you don’t need week one to be perfect before starting week two. Between people, the other person joining is optional but helpful; many start alone with a week of logging, and the other joins for the treaty.',
    w: 'Week 1, foundation and a self-audit; week 2, ownership; week 3, the numbers and your batteries; week 4, communication and tone; week 5, getting in sync with the daily loop; week 6, making it last. It ends with a second read you can compare with the first.',
    x: 'Pacing matters. The Suite Index warns that using every workpaper at once turns them into paperwork, the most common reason a household gives up in week two. Behaviour-change research agrees: small steps, added one at a time and tied to existing routines, stick better than big launches.\nThe six weeks follow the program’s logic: see the load (Pillar I), fix the setup (II), read your state (III), tune your signals (IV), then keep an eye on the quiet pulls that let things slide back (V).\nThe before-and-after reads use the same formulas each time, so they can be compared. It suits any two people who share time, space, work or care.',
    u: '/prog-01-in-depth.html#how-to-use', l: 'PROG-01: how to use the six weeks', lt: 'How the program is paced, then each week’s reading, worksheet and expected output.' },

  extraction: { t: 'The extraction cycle', m: ['extraction cycle'],
    s: 'For months you quietly plan every family birthday. Then one year a forgotten thank-you stings far more than it should. That sting is the extraction cycle showing itself.',
    f: 'For you, it explains resentment that seems out of proportion. Between people, nobody has to be unkind for it to run: the giving just stopped being seen, so it stopped feeling like a gift.',
    w: 'PROG-01’s week 3 looks for it in your own records: a few weeks of WP-01 logs and battery readings show who is carrying more and when stress spikes. The remedy isn’t a bigger argument; it’s making the work visible and owned, and thanking the specific thing.',
    x: 'Equity theory, developed by J. Stacy Adams in the 1960s and applied to relationships by Elaine Hatfield (then Walster) and colleagues, holds that people track the ratio of what they give to what they get, and feel distress when it seems out of balance. The person over-giving tends to feel resentment; the one under-giving may feel uneasy or guilty.\nThe extraction cycle adds invisibility: when the giving is unseen, the other person can’t even notice the imbalance, so it can’t be corrected. That’s the Preface’s unbilled debt turning sour over time.\nIn the numbers it often shows up as a low workload balance plus a high battery for one person, and REPORT-01 may flag it as “Clarity without parity” once the treaty is clear but the split isn’t.',
    u: '/prog-01-in-depth.html#week-3', l: 'PROG-01: week 3, the numbers and your batteries', lt: 'Where the cycle gets spotted in your own logs and battery readings.' },

  externalstatic: { t: 'External static', m: ['external static', 'environmental statics'],
    s: 'A month of night shifts, and one of you is short with everyone. The other starts to wonder if something’s wrong between you. It might be external static.',
    f: 'For you, naming outside pressure stops you blaming home for it. Between people, it turns a worry into a plan for a hard season: fewer asks, simpler meals, a check-in when the shifts end.',
    w: 'External static is strain from outside the relationship (money, work, illness, broken sleep) that shows up inside it. The planned Static Dampening Log (WP-14) is designed to separate the two, but isn’t written yet. Until then, WP-02 and Turning Toward’s stress-reducing conversation help.',
    x: 'Researchers call this stress spillover. Lisa Neff and Benjamin Karney’s studies found that when outside stress rises, partners tend to notice more negative behavior and interpret it less kindly, even when nothing at home has changed.\nThe program already has a rule for spotting it. REPORT-01’s “Load isn’t the driver” fires when stress rises for three or more weeks while workload balance stays flat, meaning something outside the chore split is behind it.\nThe social answer is dyadic coping (Guy Bodenmann’s term): partners handling one person’s outside stress together. Turning Toward’s stress-reducing conversation is a simple version, with the listener firmly on the speaker’s side.',
    u: '/program-overview-in-depth.html#signal', l: 'Program Overview: the Signal Layer', lt: 'Where outside pressure fits in the program, and the tools planned to separate it.' },

  designed: { t: 'Designed, not built yet', m: ['Designed, Not Built', 'designed-not-built', 'Static Dampening Log', 'Carrier Frequency Audit', 'Harmonic Resonance & Entrainment'],
    s: 'You read about WP-14 and go looking for it. It isn’t there yet, and the site says so plainly.',
    f: 'For you, it saves hunting. If it isn’t listed as live on the Suite Index, it isn’t live. Between people, it keeps the program honest about what it can offer right now.',
    w: 'Designed so far: the Carrier Frequency Audit (WP-12, pacing mismatch), the Static Dampening Log (WP-14, outside pressure versus friction between you) and Harmonic Resonance & Entrainment (WP-15, staying in step across years). Chapters VI to XII are also still being shaped.',
    x: 'Software teams keep a public roadmap for the same reason: saying clearly what exists, what’s planned and what’s only an idea builds more trust than implying everything is ready.\nThe names follow the program’s signal language. Entrainment is the physics of coupled rhythms falling into step, first noticed by Christiaan Huygens with two pendulum clocks in 1665. The planned WP-15 borrows it for staying in step over years rather than weeks.\nWhere the Program Overview and the Suite Index disagree, the Suite Index is correct. Release order and timing are estimates, not promises.',
    u: '/program-overview-in-depth.html#designed', l: 'Program Overview: designed, not built', lt: 'The honest list of what exists and what is still only sketched.' },

  suite: { t: 'The Workpaper Suite', m: ['Workpaper Suite'],
    s: 'Seven worksheets can feel like homework. The Workpaper Suite picks your road and lines them up in the order that helps most.',
    f: 'For you, choose just you, a kind of relationship, or the 6-week program. Between people, each fills in their own sheets, then you bring the files together into one report.',
    w: 'Four steps: choose your road; see your workpapers in order; bring in anything you’ve already filled in; make your PDF, either fillable or a report grouped the way your road goes. What you type stays on your device, and there’s a button to clear the page on a shared computer.',
    x: 'The ordering follows the Suite Index’s recommended sequence: WP-01, WP-02, WP-03, WP-09, WP-13, then CALC-01 once the first three have numbers, and WP-04 monthly. Sequencing matters because each sheet produces an input the next one uses.\nThe design is privacy-first: no account, no server copy. You keep a suite file or the PDF and bring it back to pick up later.\nIt’s built to lower friction, a behavioural-design idea: every extra step before starting reduces the chance people start at all, so the suite does the sorting for you.',
    u: '/workpapers/fill/suite.html', l: 'Build your Workpaper Suite', lt: 'Choose a road, see your sheets in order, and make one PDF.' },

  workpapers: { t: 'Workpapers', m: ['workpapers', 'workpaper'],
    s: 'Auditors don’t trust memory; they write evidence on working papers. The program’s workpapers are short sheets that each answer one question well.',
    f: 'For you, pick the one that fits where you are this week and leave the rest. Between people, each fills in their own side, then you read them together. New? One week of WP-01. Something on fire? WP-02, then WP-09.',
    w: 'The core set: WP-01 (who did what, and kind ways to say no), WP-02 (your battery), WP-03 (one owner per job), WP-04 (the monthly Deficit Audit), WP-09 (fact, feeling and ask), WP-11 (the Calm-Down Kit) and WP-13 (the daily check-in). The same argument keeps coming back? That’s a WP-03 problem, not a communication problem.',
    x: 'In professional auditing, working papers document the evidence gathered and the reasoning behind conclusions, so someone else could follow and check the work. The program keeps that spirit: plain records first, conclusions later, nothing hidden.\nFour workpapers produce numbers for CALC-01 (WP-01 hours, WP-02 batteries, WP-03 owners and WP-09 retunes), and the others deliberately don’t, because not everything worth doing is worth scoring. The numbering has gaps, and the site says those gaps are honest, not accidental.\nThe order is WP-01 → WP-02 → WP-03 → WP-09 → WP-13, then CALC-01 once there are numbers, then WP-04 monthly. REPORT-01 records the figures each week so the picture builds instead of resetting.',
    u: '/suite-index-in-depth.html#order-h', l: 'Suite Index: the order to use them in', lt: 'The recommended sequence, and where to start depending on what’s happening.' },

  selfdiscovery: { t: 'Self-discovery', m: ['self-discovery'],
    s: 'You snap at 4pm and can’t work out why. A quick check shows you’ve been running near empty since breakfast. That small “oh” is self-discovery.',
    f: 'For you, it’s understanding your own load, wiring and patterns first. Between people, once each person can describe their own side, the relationship tools have something real to work with.',
    w: 'It’s the first half of the program. It starts with Today’s Weather, WP-02 and WP-11, then Know Your Own Wiring, Wired Differently and the Wiring Card. It isn’t self-criticism: the aim is to understand yourself as kindly as you’d want to be understood.',
    x: 'Psychologists have studied how hard it is to see ourselves clearly. Research on “Solomon’s paradox” (Igor Grossmann and Ethan Kross, 2014) found people reason more wisely about other people’s problems than their own, and that taking a distanced, outside view of your own situation narrowed the gap.\nThe program’s tools build in that distance: write down what happened before deciding what it means; use the same questions every time; look at patterns across weeks rather than single days.\nKnow Your Own Wiring adds a three-layer model: wiring (lasting), shaped patterns (learned) and today’s conditions (passing). Sorting a reaction into the right layer tells you whether to work on it, work with it or work around it.',
    u: '/how-it-works-in-depth.html#discover', l: 'How it works: what you discover', lt: 'What tends to show up when you write things down for a few weeks.',
    ctx: { '/know-yourself.html': 'This page is the heart of it: sorting what’s lasting wiring, what’s a learned pattern, and what’s just today.', '/wired-differently.html': 'Here self-discovery means knowing how your own brain takes words in, so you can explain it.', '/relationships.html': 'Every relationship starts here: learn your own load and reactions before the shared tools.' } },

  outsidelens: { t: 'An outside lens', m: ['outside lens', 'honest auditor'],
    s: 'You believe you’re “just bad at mornings.” Written down for two weeks, the picture changes: mornings go wrong after late screens and no breakfast. That’s the outside lens.',
    f: 'For you, it’s looking at your own life calmly, as if from the outside. Between people, it replaces two sets of feelings about what happened with one shared record of what did.',
    w: 'It looks at three things that shape almost every reaction: your life situation, your wiring, and what you discover when you write things down for a few weeks. It should feel curious, not clinical, with nothing to defend. Every number describes a situation, never a person’s worth.',
    x: 'Ethan Kross’s research on self-distancing found that thinking about your own situation from a step back, even by using your own name or “you” instead of “I,” tends to reduce emotional reactivity and improve reasoning. The outside lens is a structured version of that step back.\nIt borrows an auditor’s stance: independence (no stake in the answer), evidence before conclusions, and consistent methods. The How It Works page is honest that self-report is never perfectly neutral, which is why repetition is what makes it trustworthy.\nThe program’s rule of thumb: one entry is a mood; twelve entries are a pattern.',
    u: '/how-it-works-in-depth.html#outside-lens', l: 'How it works: an outside lens', lt: 'The honest-auditor idea, turned gently inward, and the three things it looks at.' },

  /* ---------- Wiring and neurotypes ---------- */

  neurotypical: { t: 'Neurotypical', m: ['neurotypical'],
    s: 'A partner replies “No.” to “Want to go out tonight?” and simply means no. The neurotypical partner hears coldness. That’s a receiver built to read between the lines.',
    f: 'For you, if this is your wiring, you may trust the tone over the words when they disagree. Between people, adding the social layer a blunt message is missing helps: “No, not tonight. I’m wiped out, it’s not about you.” Same answer, softer landing.',
    w: 'Neurotypical listeners decode words, tone, face, timing and recent history all at once, and tend to expect some softening. The flip side is finding hidden meaning that was never there: a bare “No,” an “ok.” with a full stop, a long pause. Being neurotypical is one wiring among many, not the default that others deviate from.',
    x: 'The word “neurotypical” was coined within the autistic community in the 1990s to describe the majority wiring without treating it as simply “normal.” Wired Differently uses it for the kind of wiring most social customs were designed around.\nLinguists describe how this wiring handles meaning. Philosopher Paul Grice’s theory of implicature explains how listeners infer meaning beyond the literal words, and politeness theory (Penelope Brown and Stephen Levinson) describes how people soften requests to protect each other’s face. Neurotypical listeners tend to do both automatically, which is efficient between similar people and error-prone across different wirings.\nThe double empathy research cuts both ways: studies found non-autistic observers formed less favorable first impressions of autistic people from audio and video, but not from transcripts. The judgment was about delivery, which is exactly where a neurotypical receiver looks first.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#neurotypical', l: 'Wired Differently: neurotypical', lt: 'How this wiring receives words, common misreads, and how to speak to it.',
    ctx: { '/about.html': 'The founder’s aside on this page says it plainly: being neurotypical is awesome too. It’s one wiring among many.', '/how-it-works.html': 'Here it’s defined in passing as “wired like most people,” which is exactly why its habits get mistaken for the only way.' } },

  neurodivergent: { t: 'Neurodivergent', m: ['neurodivergent', 'neurodivergence'],
    s: 'Noisy family dinners always leave you drained and short-tempered, and nobody else seems to mind. For some people, that’s wiring that differs from what most customs assume.',
    f: 'For you, it isn’t a flaw to hide; it’s information to understand and then explain. Between people, two neurodivergent people can differ as much as any two people, so ask, don’t assume. A ten-minute quiet break halfway through dinner can help everyone.',
    w: 'Neurodivergent describes wiring that differs from the design most social customs assume, in a meaningful way. It covers neurodevelopmental wirings like autism, ADHD, dyslexia and dyspraxia, and many people carry more than one. Wired Differently can’t diagnose anyone; a qualified clinician is the right next step if you want to know more.',
    x: 'The word comes from the neurodiversity idea, usually credited to sociologist Judy Singer in the late 1990s: that variation in brains is a natural part of human diversity, like variation in any other trait. It’s a social and political idea as much as a scientific one, and people use it in slightly different ways.\nWired Differently gives an estimate of 15 to 20 percent of people worldwide, likely low because many people are never assessed. Co-occurrence is common: Autistica reports about 28 percent of autistic children meet ADHD criteria, and about 21 percent of children with ADHD are also autistic.\nOn language, a survey of 654 autistic adults (Keating and colleagues) found most preferred identity-first terms like “autistic person.” The program follows the person’s own preference when it’s known.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#words', l: 'Wired Differently: the words, used carefully', lt: 'What each word means, where estimates come from, and how the page chooses its language.',
    ctx: { '/about.html': 'The founder writes about his own neurodivergence here, and years spent masking it.', '/how-it-works.html': 'This page’s point: neurotypical or neurodivergent, your wiring is information, not a flaw.' } },

  neurodev: { t: 'Neurodevelopmental', m: ['neurodevelopmental'],
    s: 'Some wiring isn’t something that happened to you later; it’s how your brain has been built from the start. That’s what neurodevelopmental means.',
    f: 'For you, it separates lasting wiring from passing conditions like a bad week. Between people, it explains why “just try harder” doesn’t work: you can plan around wiring, but you can’t wish it away.',
    w: 'Wired Differently covers seven: autism, ADHD, dyslexia, dyspraxia, auditory processing differences, developmental language disorder and Tourette syndrome. It sets them beside traits found across every group (alexithymia, high sensitivity) and conditions that change how words land (anxiety, trauma, OCD). They often travel together.',
    x: 'In clinical classification (for example the DSM-5), neurodevelopmental conditions are a category defined by differences that show up early in development and affect functioning in areas like attention, learning, language, movement or social communication. The program uses the category only to organize the guide, never to diagnose.\nThe three-group layout is deliberate. Know Your Own Wiring’s model separates wiring (lasting), shaped patterns (learned) and conditions (passing). Neurodevelopmental wirings sit in the first layer; anxiety and trauma responses can shape the second; a hard week is the third.\nThat sorting tells you what to do: work with lasting wiring, work on learned patterns if you want to, and work around passing conditions with timing.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#words', l: 'Wired Differently: the words, used carefully', lt: 'How the page groups wirings, traits and conditions, and why.' },

  doubleempathy: { t: 'The double empathy problem', m: ['double empathy'],
    s: 'One of you thinks the other is being cold; the other thinks the first is being vague. Both are right about what they experienced. That’s the double empathy problem.',
    f: 'For you, it lifts the idea that the misunderstanding is all your fault, or all theirs. Between people, it swaps opening a case against either person for comparing notes: what did you mean, what did I hear?',
    w: 'Named by autistic researcher Damian Milton in 2012: when people with very different experiences of the world interact, the breakdown is mutual, not a deficit inside one person. Each uses a decoder built for their own kind of signal. The lesson applies to every pairing, not only autistic and neurotypical.',
    x: 'The evidence is still developing, and Wired Differently reports it carefully. Crompton and colleagues (2020) ran a “diffusion chain” study, passing a story along chains of people: all-autistic and all-non-autistic chains passed on similar detail, while mixed chains lost detail faster and reported lower rapport. A much larger repeat, published in Nature Human Behavior in 2025, didn’t find that extra detail loss, but did find lower rapport in mixed and autistic chains.\nThe honest reading: facts can get through fine, but the conversation tends to feel harder to both people. First-impression studies point the same way: a 2025 review found non-autistic observers judged autistic people less favourably from audio and video, but not from transcripts alone.\nThe program turns this into practice: both people describe their own wiring (the Wiring Card), both test wording (the Signal Translator), and after a mix-up each says what they meant and what they heard.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#double-empathy', l: 'Wired Differently: the problem runs in both directions', lt: 'The idea, the studies on both sides, and what it means for every pairing.',
    ctx: { '/know-yourself.html': 'Here it’s a reminder when explaining yourself: the other person’s decoder isn’t broken either, just built differently.', '/library/': 'This Library theme has the research; Wired Differently turns it into habits for every pairing.' } },

  autistic: { t: 'Autistic', m: ['autistic', 'autism'],
    s: '“Clean up a bit before my parents come” can create real anxiety, because “a bit” has no end point. “Clear the table and vacuum by 5?” is kind, clear and doable.',
    f: 'For you, if this is your wiring, words may arrive literally first, with tone and hints taking extra effort. Between people, directness is usually efficiency, not coldness, and silence often means “I’m processing.” An autistic person may show care through information, practical help or shared interests.',
    w: 'What helps: say the request as a request, define the finish line (what, where, how much, by when), flag sarcasm or leave it out, give processing time, offer writing for complex topics, and stop asking questions during a shutdown. The difference is often cost, not capacity.',
    x: 'Autism is a neurodevelopmental difference in social communication and sensory processing, with strong, focused interests and a preference for predictability. Wired Differently highlights a key research point: in one study autistic and non-autistic students read hints from context equally well, but decoding hints, tone and faces all at once takes deliberate effort, which runs out faster when tired or masking.\nThat effort is why the double empathy problem matters. Studies suggest the communication gap runs both ways, and that non-autistic people also find it harder to read autistic people’s emotions.\nThe program’s practical translation: literal, specific requests with a finish line and a time, flagged or no sarcasm, and written plans for anything complex. In Check-ins, a literal repeat back is respect, not coldness.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#autistic', l: 'Wired Differently: autistic', lt: 'How this wiring receives words, what helps, and what it means when they’re the one talking.',
    ctx: { '/about.html': 'The founder is autistic, and writes here about how his wiring shaped every tool in the program.', '/know-yourself.html': 'Here it’s one example of lasting wiring: something to understand and plan around, not fix.', '/turning-toward.html': 'For bids, this wiring may show care through information, practical help or shared interests rather than small talk.', '/library/': 'This Library theme gathers the research; Wired Differently has the practical how-to.' } },

  masking: { t: 'Masking', m: ['masking', 'camouflaging', 'drop the mask'],
    s: 'Home after a sociable day, you need an hour alone before you can talk. You’ve spent the day performing “normal.” That performance is masking.',
    f: 'For you, it means watching your eye contact, face and turn-taking so closely that you miss the conversation itself. Between people, knowing this lets others give you that hour without taking it personally. Count hidden effort like masking as real load.',
    w: 'It’s common among autistic adults, and exhaustion is its most consistent cost. The site’s founder writes about dropping the mask as the turning point that led to this program. A Wiring Card is one way to explain your needs openly instead of performing around them.',
    x: 'Researchers also call it camouflaging. Laura Hull and colleagues studied it in 2017 and later built a questionnaire to measure it (the CAT-Q). Their work describes three parts: masking (hiding autistic traits), compensation (using learned strategies to get through social situations) and assimilation (trying to fit in). Most autistic adults in these studies reported doing it, and exhaustion was the most consistent cost.\nMasking isn’t unique to autism. Many people adjust how they present at work or with in-laws. The difference is scale: when the adjustment is constant and effortful, it uses up capacity that would otherwise go to listening and thinking.\nIn the program, masking counts as hidden load (Pillar I) and explains why people load can empty some people faster.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#autistic', l: 'Wired Differently: autistic', lt: 'Where masking fits in how autistic people receive and send words.',
    ctx: { '/about.html': 'This is the founder’s story: years masking to make everyone else comfortable, and what changed when he stopped.', '/know-yourself.html': 'Here the point is to count it: masking is hidden effort, and hidden effort is real load.' } },

  adhd: { t: 'ADHD', m: ['ADHD'], cs: true,
    s: '“Grab milk, drop off the package, and call your mom back” becomes just “grab milk” by the time you reach the parking lot. That’s one everyday face of ADHD.',
    f: 'For you, if this is your wiring, attention comes in bursts and “later” can quietly evaporate. Between people, a forgotten task is rarely a message that someone doesn’t matter. “The bill slipped. Can we set a reminder together?” works far better than “You forgot again.”',
    w: 'What helps: one request at a time, or the list in writing; a real time instead of “later”; the ask first, then the reasons; criticize the system, not the person; an “echo” where the listener repeats back what they heard. Interrupting is often fear of losing the thought.',
    x: 'ADHD (attention deficit hyperactivity disorder) is a neurodevelopmental difference in attention regulation, activity level and impulse control. Researchers like Russell Barkley describe it largely as a difference in executive functions, especially working memory and time management. That’s why spoken lists vanish and vague times slip.\nWired Differently cites CHADD (drawing on Staley and colleagues, 2024) that about 6 percent of US adults have a current ADHD diagnosis. Emotion can run hot too: a review of 22 studies found adults with ADHD had lower emotion-regulation scores than controls, with estimates that 34 to 70 percent experience emotional dysregulation.\nThe program designs around this: WP-13’s ask line needs a real time, the Signal Translator flags “later,” and the treaty puts jobs in writing instead of in anyone’s head.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#adhd', l: 'Wired Differently: ADHD', lt: 'How this wiring receives words, what helps, and what it means when they’re the one talking.',
    ctx: { '/turning-toward.html': 'For bids: someone with ADHD may miss a bid during focus, then turn toward it warmly a minute later. It still counts.', '/about.html': 'The founder’s wiring includes ADHD alongside autism, OCD and anxiety.', '/library/motivation.html': 'On this page, “getting started” trouble with ADHD may reflect executive function rather than choice.', '/library/': 'The Library has the research on ADHD; Wired Differently turns it into habits, like a real time instead of “later.”' } },

  rsd: { t: 'Rejection sensitivity', m: ['rejection sensitivity', 'rejection sensitive dysphoria'],
    s: 'An unanswered text for three hours, and your mind has already decided they’re upset with you. That’s rejection sensitivity: reading unclear moments as rejection, and feeling it hard.',
    f: 'For you, naming it can help: “My brain is reading your silence as upset. Is it?” Between people, closing loops is kind: “Busy until 5, all good.” Five seconds of typing can save someone three hours of worry.',
    w: 'It isn’t a formal diagnosis, but many people with ADHD describe it. Ambiguity is where it bites: silence, a short reply or a delay leaves a gap, and the worst reading fills it. Describe the behavior, not the person, and remember criticism can land with far more force than you meant.',
    x: 'Two related ideas exist. Psychologists Geraldine Downey and Scott Feldman described rejection sensitivity in 1996 as a tendency to anxiously expect, readily perceive and strongly react to rejection, and linked it to relationship difficulties. Separately, “rejection sensitive dysphoria” is a term used by some ADHD clinicians for intense emotional pain after perceived rejection; it isn’t in diagnostic manuals and has less research behind it.\nBoth point to the same practical lever: reduce ambiguity. Research on uncertainty shows that not knowing is often harder to sit with than bad news.\nThe program’s tools help: a quick “all good, just busy” message, the anxiety guidance to lead with reassurance, and Check-ins’ bond signal before a hard topic.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#adhd', l: 'Wired Differently: ADHD and rejection sensitivity', lt: 'Where rejection sensitivity fits, and the habits that close the gap.' },

  audhd: { t: 'Autistic and ADHD together', m: ['AuDHD', 'Autistic and ADHD together'], cs: true,
    s: '“Let’s play it by ear this weekend” is vague, unstructured and has nothing to remember. For someone who’s autistic and has ADHD, it can hit two sensitivities at once.',
    f: 'For you, if this is your wiring, you might need blunt, literal clarity and still lose the second half of a long explanation. Between people, “Saturday: groceries at 10, free after 1” works better than “we’ll figure it out,” and leaves room for spontaneity.',
    w: 'There can be a real pull between ADHD’s need for novelty and autism’s need for routine. What helps: direct, short and written plans; changes given early with the reason and the new plan together; criticizing the process, not the person. An abrupt topic jump can be both a tangent and a deep dive: both are engagement.',
    x: 'Autism and ADHD commonly occur together. Since the DSM-5 in 2013, clinicians have been able to diagnose both in the same person, which wasn’t allowed before. Wired Differently cites Autistica’s figures: about 28 percent of autistic children meet ADHD criteria, and about 21 percent of children with ADHD are also autistic. “AuDHD” is a community term, not a diagnostic label.\nShared traits include trouble following conversations, sensory differences and rejection sensitivity. The pull between novelty and routine means a plan has to be both predictable and not boring, which is why a fixed structure with a free slot often works.\nThe program’s tools suit both at once: short written asks with a time (WP-09, WP-13), the Wiring Card’s “when I’m depleted” line, and one topic per talk.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#audhd', l: 'Wired Differently: autistic and ADHD together', lt: 'How the two wirings combine, what helps, and phrases that hit two sensitivities at once.' },

  dyslexic: { t: 'Dyslexic', m: ['dyslexic', 'dyslexia'],
    s: 'A careful five-paragraph message about a disagreement, meant as respect, arrives as a wall. For a dyslexic reader, reading effort and emotional weight stack up.',
    f: 'For you, if this is your wiring, spoken words may come easily while written ones cost effort. Between people, put the headline in the first line and offer a call for anything emotional. A short, blunt text may just mean typing costs effort.',
    w: 'It has nothing to do with intelligence. What helps: short lines, one idea per line, the important part first, a voice note or call for anything emotional or complex, and never commenting on spelling in a personal conversation. A slow reply may be a reread, not a cold shoulder.',
    x: 'Dyslexia is a specific learning difference that mainly affects reading and spelling. Research points to differences in phonological processing, the ability to break words into sounds and link them to letters. It runs in families and is not related to intelligence or effort.\nThe communication point is about channel cost. The same message costs very different effort depending on whether it arrives as speech, a voice note or a wall of text. Wired Differently treats choosing the channel on purpose as one of its ten rules for every wiring.\nThe program applies it: the Signal Translator’s reading load, WP-09’s one-sentence fact, and the Wiring Card’s channel preferences.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#dyslexic', l: 'Wired Differently: dyslexic', lt: 'How this wiring receives words, what helps, and a mixed-pair example.' },

  dyspraxic: { t: 'Dyspraxic', m: ['dyspraxic', 'dyspraxia'],
    s: '“It’s easy, just do it like this.” For a dyspraxic person, “just” and “easy” can shrink a task that really does cost more, and sting.',
    f: 'For you, if this is your wiring, fast-fire questions can lead to pressure and then a freeze. Between people, show each step in order, then wait. A long pause is someone building the answer, not a brush-off.',
    w: 'Dyspraxia mainly affects movement and coordination, and it often shows in conversation too: keeping up with fast talk and organizing a reply can take longer. Drop “just,” “easy” and “obviously.” Being late or disorganised isn’t a statement about how much someone cares.',
    x: 'Dyspraxia is also called developmental coordination disorder (DCD). Common estimates put it at around 5 to 6 percent of school-age children, and it often continues into adulthood. Beyond movement, many dyspraxic people find planning, organization, working memory and handwriting harder.\nThe communication advice follows from processing time: questions spaced out, one at a time, and instructions shown in order rather than described all at once. Typing is often easier than handwriting.\nIn the program, this is part of why the ten rules include “allow silence” and “one thing at a time,” and why the Signal Translator flags minimizing words like “just.”',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#dyspraxic', l: 'Wired Differently: dyspraxic', lt: 'How this wiring receives words, what helps, and what a pause means.' },

  apd: { t: 'Auditory processing differences', m: ['auditory processing'],
    s: '“I already told you that!” But you said it from the next room with the dishwasher running. It arrived as noise with a few words in it.',
    f: 'For you, if this is your wiring, the ears work but speech gets scrambled in noise or when it’s fast. Between people, say important things face to face, somewhere quiet, and send a one-line text confirming plans.',
    w: 'What helps: face to face, don’t cover your mouth, turn the TV off or move somewhere quieter, rephrase rather than repeat louder, and follow up important plans in text. “What?” is a real question, and a deadpan joke can easily be taken as serious.',
    x: 'Auditory processing disorder (APD) describes difficulty making sense of sound, especially speech in noise, when hearing tests are normal. It’s a debated diagnosis: audiologists disagree on how to define and test it, and it often overlaps with attention and language differences. Wired Differently uses “differences” to describe the experience without settling that debate.\nThe everyday mechanics are well understood. Speech in noise is harder for everyone, and some people need a much cleaner signal to follow it. Seeing the speaker’s mouth helps because lip-reading fills gaps.\nThe program’s room rules (quiet, phones down, face to face) help this wiring most, and the ten rules’ “check what arrived” catches misheard plans early.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#apd', l: 'Wired Differently: auditory processing differences', lt: 'How this wiring receives words, what helps, and what an off-topic answer might mean.' },

  dld: { t: 'Developmental language disorder', m: ['Developmental language disorder', 'developmental language disorder', 'DLD'], cs: true,
    s: '“We’ll cross that bridge when we come to it” gets a puzzled look. “Let’s decide that next week, when we know more” lands straight away.',
    f: 'For you, if this is your wiring, idioms and long sentences with “unless” clauses can get lost after the first part. Between people, short, concrete sentences help, and so does kindly checking: “Tell me what you’re taking from this, so I know I said it well.”',
    w: 'Developmental language disorder affects learning, understanding and using language, and can continue into adulthood. It’s common and often missed. A roundabout answer may be a word-finding problem, not evasion: give time to find the word before offering one.',
    x: 'DLD is diagnosed when language difficulties aren’t explained by another condition such as hearing loss. Population studies (including Norbury and colleagues, 2016) estimate it affects around 7 percent of children, roughly two in a class of thirty, yet it’s far less known than dyslexia or autism.\nWired Differently notes it also appears alongside autism: in one autism cohort, 46 percent of adults who could self-report screened positive for DLD.\nThe communication fixes are the same ones the program recommends for everyone under stress: one idea per sentence, concrete words, and checking what arrived. They matter most for this wiring.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#dld', l: 'Wired Differently: developmental language disorder', lt: 'How this wiring receives words, what helps, and why it’s so often missed.' },

  tourette: { t: 'Tourette syndrome', m: ['Tourette'],
    s: 'During a serious talk, a grunt and an eye roll. It feels like contempt, but it may be a tic.',
    f: 'For you, if this is your wiring, tics aren’t chosen, and stress can make them more frequent, exactly when serious talks happen. Between people, respond to the words, not the tic, and if someone tells you it was a tic, believe them.',
    w: 'In communication, the static usually sits with the listener, who mistakes a tic for a reaction. Don’t comment on tics mid-conversation unless asked. “Can’t you just stop?” adds stress and often more tics. Ask once, on a calm day, how they’d like you to respond.',
    x: 'Tourette syndrome is a neurodevelopmental condition involving motor and vocal tics that have lasted more than a year. Many people feel a “premonitory urge,” an uncomfortable sensation before a tic, and can hold tics back briefly at a cost, which is why others sometimes wrongly assume they’re deliberate.\nWired Differently notes that more than half of people with Tourette also have OCD, ADHD or both, so other wirings may be in the room too.\nThis is a clear case for the program’s rule that intent and impact are separate: the impact of an eye-roll tic can be real, and the intent is absent. Naming that on a calm day prevents a lot of hurt.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#tourette', l: 'Wired Differently: Tourette syndrome', lt: 'How tics interact with hard conversations, and what helps both people.' },

  alexithymia: { t: 'Alexithymia', m: ['alexithymia'],
    s: '“How does that make you feel?” can feel like an exam question with no answer. For some people, the feelings are there; the label isn’t.',
    f: 'For you, if this is you, distress may show up as “I’m tired” or a headache rather than a feeling-word. Between people, offer options (“more like tired, or more like annoyed?”) or ask about the body (“is your chest tight?”).',
    w: 'It’s a trait found in any wiring, especially common with autism. What helps: ask about the body or actions, offer two options instead of an open question, and turn feelings into actions: “Could you text me after my scan on Tuesday?” gives more to act on than “be more supportive.” “I’m fine” may be the honest answer they have right now.',
    x: 'The term was coined by psychiatrist Peter Sifneos in 1973, from Greek for “no words for emotions.” It’s usually measured with the Toronto Alexithymia Scale (TAS-20), and it’s a trait on a spectrum rather than a diagnosis. General population estimates are often given around 10 percent, and a 2019 review (Kinnaird and colleagues) estimated around half of autistic people show high levels.\nResearch links it to interoception: difficulty reading the body’s signals makes it harder to recognize and name the emotions they’re part of.\nThe program adapts for it: Check-ins warns against handing someone a feeling-word they didn’t use, WP-09’s feeling line can be left rough, and WP-13’s load line uses capacity (low, medium, high) rather than emotion.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#alexithymia', l: 'Wired Differently: alexithymia', lt: 'How this trait shapes conversations, and questions that work better than “how do you feel?”',
    ctx: { '/turning-toward.html': 'For bids: “how do you feel about us?” can be hard for this trait. A concrete question works better.', '/library/': 'This Library theme covers the research; Wired Differently has the everyday how-to.' } },

  hsp: { t: 'Highly sensitive', m: ['highly sensitive', 'sensory processing sensitivity'],
    s: 'A slightly clipped “Sure” gets noticed instantly and felt for hours. For a highly sensitive person, too little isn’t the problem; too much gets through.',
    f: 'For you, if this is you, a hard talk in a loud room can be overwhelming before a word is said. Between people, naming your tone helps: “I’m short because of work, not you.” Lower the volume of the house before you lower the volume of the talk.',
    w: 'What helps: a soft start, a quiet setting, one topic, time to process before a decision. When they’re the one talking, “something felt off” is often accurate about mood, even if the cause is misread.',
    x: 'Psychologist Elaine Aron introduced sensory processing sensitivity in 1997 as a temperament trait: deeper processing of information, stronger reactions to stimulation, and high awareness of subtleties. Wired Differently notes that roughly 20 percent of people fit this description.\nIt’s still being studied. Later work (for example Lionetti and colleagues, 2018) suggests people fall along a continuum, sometimes grouped as low, medium and high sensitivity, rather than into two clear types. Related research on “differential susceptibility” suggests sensitive people may be more affected by both harsh and supportive environments.\nThat last point is hopeful: the room and the tone matter more for this trait, so the program’s room rules and soft starts pay off most here.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#sensitive', l: 'Wired Differently: highly sensitive', lt: 'How this trait receives tone and mood, and what helps before and during a talk.',
    ctx: { '/check-ins.html': 'For check-ins: if one of you is highly sensitive, lower the volume of the house before the volume of the talk.', '/know-yourself.html': 'Here it’s an example of lasting wiring: something to plan around, not fix.', '/library/stress.html': 'This theme explains why the same room can empty a sensitive nervous system quickly.' } },

  overload: { t: 'Sensory overload', m: ['sensory overload', 'sensory differences'],
    s: 'A family argument keeps flaring at a busy dinner table with the TV on. Moved to a quiet room afterward, just two people, the tone changes completely. That’s sensory load at work.',
    f: 'For you, bright light, noise, crowds and strong smells can simply be too much to take in. Between people, needing to leave a loud place isn’t rejecting the company. Agree not to have hard conversations in your home’s loudest spot.',
    w: 'Highly sensitive people take in more than most, and sensory differences are common with autism, ADHD and auditory processing differences. Check-ins asks for light, noise and temperature low enough that a sensitive nervous system isn’t spending its budget on the room. The Calm-Down Kit’s environmental move is to lower the input.',
    x: 'Sensory processing research describes people as varying in how strongly they register sensation, with some more sensitive (noticing and being bothered by more) and some less. Winnie Dunn’s sensory processing model is one widely used framework. Sensory differences are part of the diagnostic description of autism in the DSM-5.\nThe cognitive-load idea helps explain why overload hurts conversations: attention is limited, and whatever the room takes, the talk doesn’t get.\nChapter IV treats light, noise and scent as real inputs, not background, and names its settling habit “gating.” The practical rule is simple: fix the room first, it’s often easier than fixing the words.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#sensitive', l: 'Wired Differently: highly sensitive', lt: 'Sensory load in context, and the room changes that help most.' },

  anxiety: { t: 'Anxiety and how words land', m: ['anxiety'],
    s: '“We need to talk.” For an anxious mind, that can mean hours of bracing for disaster, when you only meant the weekend plans.',
    f: 'For you, if anxiety is part of your wiring, gaps get filled with the worst reading. Between people, lead with the reassurance: “Nothing’s wrong. Can we talk about the weekend tonight?” “Are you mad at me?” is a real question; answer it plainly.',
    w: 'The fewer details a message gives, the more room the worst reading has. What helps: reassurance first, then the topic; the reason for a call up front; closing loops with a quick “Got it, thanks!” For anxious-and-slow-texter pairs: “No reply means busy, never mad. If I’m upset, I’ll say so in words.”',
    x: 'Psychologists call this interpretation bias: a tendency to resolve ambiguity toward threat. Wired Differently cites Beard and Amir (2009), where socially anxious participants chose the threatening meaning of ambiguous sentences like “People laugh after something you said” 59 percent of the time, versus 30 percent for non-anxious participants.\nResearch on intolerance of uncertainty adds that for many anxious people, not knowing is harder than bad news, which is why a quick “all good” message helps so much.\nThe program keeps a clear line: these tools aren’t for panic attacks or ongoing anxiety, and the Calm-Down Kit says a doctor or therapist is the better call for those. What it offers is communication habits that give anxious minds less to fill in.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#anxiety', l: 'Wired Differently: anxiety', lt: 'How anxiety changes the way words land, and habits that leave less room for the worst reading.',
    ctx: { '/wp-11.html': 'Here it’s a limit: the Kit is for ordinary bad moments, not panic or ongoing anxiety.', '/about.html': 'The founder’s wiring includes anxiety, alongside autism, OCD and ADHD.', '/library/relationships.html': 'On this page “anxiety” also means attachment anxiety: worry about being left, one of two dimensions researchers measure.', '/library/': 'This Library theme gathers the research; Wired Differently shows how it changes the way words land.' } },

  hypervigilance: { t: 'Hypervigilance', m: ['hypervigilance', 'trauma-wired'],
    s: 'A door bangs mid-conversation and someone flinches. Or an excited raised voice lands as danger. A nervous system that stays on watch hears the volume before the words.',
    f: 'For you, if this is your wiring, content only gets processed once your body decides it’s safe. Between people, a steady volume and the purpose first help: “Can you come look at this? It’s a good surprise.”',
    w: 'What helps: steady volume, the purpose first, no approaching from behind, no serious talks sprung late at night, and settling together before talking. A startle or a sharp reply may be the alarm, not their opinion of you. Needing to leave a heated conversation is often self-protection; agree a time to come back.',
    x: 'Hypervigilance means scanning for threat and reading neutral or unclear signals as dangerous. After frightening experiences, the brain’s threat-detection systems can stay more sensitive, which is protective in danger and exhausting in everyday life.\nResearch on threat processing shows attention is drawn faster to possible threats in people with trauma histories, which explains why volume, sudden movement and tone register before words.\nWired Differently can’t diagnose anyone and suggests a clinician for support. The program’s part is small and practical: co-regulating first, the signal line with a return time, and rooms that feel safe.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#trauma', l: 'Wired Differently: trauma and hypervigilance', lt: 'How a nervous system on watch receives words, and what helps first.' },

  ocd: { t: 'OCD and certainty', m: ['OCD', 'Reassurance seeking'], cs: true,
    s: '“I think I locked it” leaves the doubt open. “I locked it” closes it. For OCD, that one word can matter a lot.',
    f: 'For you, if this is your wiring, doubt can loop, and asking again feels like the only way to quiet it. Between people, a repeated question isn’t distrust of you; it’s the doubt talking. Answer the first time clearly, without hedging.',
    w: 'Agree in a calm moment, ideally with a clinician’s input, how you’ll both respond to repeat questions. Speak to the person, not the doubt, and keep your tone warm: “I answered that one. I’m here with you while the doubt passes.” It isn’t a treatment plan.',
    x: 'OCD (obsessive-compulsive disorder) involves intrusive, unwanted thoughts (obsessions) and repeated actions or mental acts done to reduce the distress (compulsions). Reassurance seeking is a common compulsion.\nClinicians use the term family accommodation for the ways relatives, out of love, take part in the cycle, like repeated reassurance. Research links high accommodation with the cycle continuing, which is why the page suggests agreeing a response with a clinician rather than improvising.\nThe program’s general rule of clear, unhedged language helps here most. “Probably fine” hands the doubt exactly the word it attaches to.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#ocd', l: 'Wired Differently: OCD and certainty', lt: 'How uncertainty and reassurance work with OCD, and answers that close the loop.',
    ctx: { '/about.html': 'The founder names OCD as part of his own wiring, alongside autism, ADHD and anxiety.', '/library/wiring.html': 'This theme notes the pull toward certainty runs on a spectrum in everyone, and is often stronger with OCD, anxiety and autism.' } },

  subtext: { t: 'Subtext and hints', m: ['subtext', 'indirectness'],
    s: '“The trash is full again,” with a sigh. Nothing happens. Next week: “Could you take the trash out before 9 tonight?” Done. The request was hiding in the subtext.',
    f: 'For you, hinting can feel politer. Between people, it works for some wirings and gets lost for others. If you can’t tell, it’s fair to ask: “Is that a request, or just an observation?”',
    w: 'People often soften a request to avoid friction and leave room to retreat. The first of Wired Differently’s ten rules: say the request as a request. The relationship changes the cost: a hint between friends risks awkwardness; between co-parents it can sound like a record being built.',
    x: 'Linguists have studied this closely. John Searle described indirect speech acts (“it’s cold in here” as a request to close the window), and Paul Grice explained how listeners infer the intended meaning. Steven Pinker and colleagues argued people use indirectness to manage relationships: it lets both sides save face if the answer is no.\nThat social benefit comes with a decoding cost, which falls unevenly: autistic listeners may take the literal meaning, ADHD listeners may catch the hint and then lose it without a time attached, and anxious listeners may over-read it.\nThe program’s fix keeps the kindness and drops the guesswork: a direct request, softened with warmth rather than vagueness, and a real time.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#same', l: 'Wired Differently: same words, many receivers', lt: 'Everyday sentences and how different wirings might receive each one.',
    ctx: { '/library/communication.html': 'This theme shows the other side: why indirectness can be a form of courtesy in some cultures and relationships.' } },

  tenrules: { t: 'Ten rules for every wiring', m: ['Ten rules'],
    s: '“Can you clean up a bit when you get a chance?” becomes “Could you clear the counter and run the dishwasher before 8 tonight?” Three rules in one sentence.',
    f: 'For you, pick the one rule you break most and practice it for a week. Between people, these help a sentence arrive well with almost any listener, at home or at work.',
    w: 'Say the request as a request; put a real time on it; one thing at a time; lead with the headline; say the feeling in words; leave sarcasm out of anything serious; choose the channel on purpose; allow silence; check what arrived; describe the behavior, never the person. They’re about how a sentence arrives, not permission to pile on topics or blame.',
    x: 'Accessibility designers call this universal design: build for the people with the greatest need, and it usually works better for everyone. Plain-language guidelines, ramps and captions are examples. The ten rules apply the same idea to conversation.\nEach rule maps to a specific wiring’s need (literal processing, working memory, reading load, processing time, threat interpretation) but also to stress in general, since almost everyone becomes more literal, forgetful and threat-sensitive when tired.\nThe program builds several rules into its tools: WP-09 forces a fact and an ask, WP-13 asks for a real time, the Signal Translator flags sarcasm and vague timing, and Check-ins’ mirror is “check what arrived.”',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#rules', l: 'Wired Differently: ten rules for every wiring', lt: 'All ten, with what each one protects against.' },

  dynamics: { t: 'The seven relationship dynamics', m: ['seven dynamics'],
    s: '“Whenever” is a loose plan between friends. Between partners it can sound like the shared life isn’t a priority. Between co-parents: “this will be late, and I’ll be blamed.”',
    f: 'For you, it explains why the same sentence works with one person and fails with another. Between people, naming the relationship helps you talk about the load, not each other.',
    w: 'The seven: partners (sharing a life), family (old roles still in the room), co-parents (raising a child, in one home or two), friends (chosen, no job description), roommates (a home, not a life), coworkers (shared work, sometimes uneven power) and caregivers (sharing care for someone). Wiring is how a brain takes words in; the relationship is the arrangement under the words.',
    x: 'Relationship scientists distinguish relationships by things like interdependence, choice, power and history. Harold Kelley and John Thibaut’s interdependence theory, for example, looks at how much each person’s outcomes depend on the other, which differs hugely between roommates and co-parents.\nClark and Mills’s communal and exchange distinction also varies by relationship: tit-for-tat accounting feels normal between coworkers and cold between partners.\nThe program lets you set both layers separately: wiring and relationship in the Signal Translator, relationship type in Today’s Weather’s forecast, and a starting point per relationship on the Relationships page.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#dynamics', l: 'Wired Differently: the relationship changes the landing', lt: 'Each of the seven dynamics, and how the same words land differently in each.' },

  conflictneeds: { t: 'Two conflict needs', m: ['two conflict needs'],
    s: 'In a hard moment, one of you needs a hug before anything else; the other needs to walk alone for twenty minutes. Neither need is the childish one.',
    f: 'For you, knowing which need you usually have helps you ask for it. Between people, the care is an order: the small thing first (a signal the bond is okay), then the pause, then the return. “I’m not leaving this. I need twenty minutes. We’re okay.”',
    w: 'Forcing the talk before the body settles makes a second spike; taking space with no signal makes the pause feel like an ending. Offer touch, never take it: “hug, hand, or words?” A no is complete. The other can say: “I can wait if I know you’re coming back.”',
    x: 'Couples researchers call the clash pursue–withdraw or demand–withdraw (Andrew Christensen and others): one person pushes for engagement, the other pulls back, and each move intensifies the other. It’s one of the most studied patterns in couple conflict.\nAttachment research explains the two needs. People higher in attachment anxiety tend to seek closeness under stress; people higher in avoidance tend to seek distance. Both are strategies for feeling safe.\nThe program’s bridge meets both: a bond signal for the closeness need, a signal line with a return time for the space need, and a re-entry check so the return is real.',
    u: '/wired-differently-in-depth.html#situation', l: 'Wired Differently: caring for both needs', lt: 'The situation, the room and a bridge sentence that meets both conflict needs.' },

  /* ---------- Turning toward ---------- */

  bids: { t: 'Bids for connection', m: ['bids', 'a bid', 'bid for', 'Turning away', 'turning against'],
    s: 'Someone on the sofa laughs and holds up their phone: “Look at this.” You could say “mm” without looking up, or lean over and laugh with them. That tiny reach is a bid.',
    f: 'For you, it’s noticing the small reaches: a sigh, a joke, a question about your day, a link sent with no comment. Between people, you can turn toward (look up, answer), turn away (miss it) or turn against (“can’t you see I’m busy?”).',
    w: 'Turning away is rarely cruel; it’s usually tiredness or focus. Nobody catches every bid; the overall pattern counts. Can’t right now? “I want to hear this. Give me ten minutes?” then come back. That still counts as turning toward. This is Pillar IV, Tune how you send and receive.',
    x: 'The idea comes from John Gottman’s research, including a study where newlywed couples spent time in an apartment-style lab. Following up six years later, the couples still together had turned toward each other’s bids most of the time, while those who split had done so only about a third of the time. The often-quoted figures are 86 percent versus 33 percent.\nIt’s one lab’s study of couples, so treat the exact numbers carefully, but the principle, that small everyday responsiveness adds up, is supported by wider research on perceived partner responsiveness.\nThe program links bids to repair and to drift: each missed bid is small, but a pattern of them is how people slowly fall out of step.',
    u: '/turning-toward-in-depth.html#bids', l: 'Turning Toward: notice bids', lt: 'Three ways to answer a bid, the research behind it, and what to do when you can’t right now.' },

  turning: { t: 'Turning toward', m: ['turning toward'],
    s: 'Closeness isn’t built mostly on big talks or special dates. It’s built in tiny moments, answered warmly, many times over. Turning toward is answering those moments.',
    f: 'For you, it’s looking up, answering, asking one question, a small “oh nice.” Between people, it looks different by relationship: bids and good news for partners, warmth about the children for co-parents, a weekly “what went well” for roommates and coworkers, thanks in both directions for caregivers.',
    w: 'Seven practices: notice bids; say thank you, specifically; celebrate good news; the stress-reducing conversation; small rituals; know their world; remember what you like about them. Warmth doesn’t make an unfair split acceptable, and a fair split doesn’t make up for coldness. This is Pillar IV with Pillar V.',
    x: 'The seven practices draw mainly on Gottman’s research (bids, love maps, fondness, the stress-reducing conversation), Shelly Gable’s work on responding to good news, Sara Algoe’s on gratitude, and William Doherty’s on rituals of connection. The page lists its sources and notes that most studies involve couples.\nUnderneath them all is responsiveness. Harry Reis and colleagues describe perceived responsiveness, feeling understood, valued and cared for, as a core ingredient of close relationships of many kinds.\nIn the program, turning toward is the everyday layer that makes hard talks easier. It shows up in WP-13’s appreciation line and in Check-ins’ closing appreciation.',
    u: '/turning-toward-in-depth.html#bids', l: 'Turning Toward: the seven practices', lt: 'All seven everyday practices, starting with bids, plus ideas for every kind of relationship.',
    ctx: { '/library/relationships.html': 'This theme covers the research; Turning Toward is the program’s seven everyday practices.' } },

  appreciation: { t: 'Appreciation', m: ['appreciation', 'specific thanks'],
    s: 'Not “thanks for everything,” but “Thank you for sorting the school forms. I know that took your whole lunch break.” Specific thanks proves you noticed.',
    f: 'For you, it’s how invisible work becomes seen. Between people, you can thank the thing, the effort, or the quality behind it: “You’re always the one who remembers birthdays.” It works for partners, kids, flatmates and coworkers.',
    w: 'It’s the second line of the daily check-in (WP-13), and a check-in closes with one specific appreciation. Thanking someone for their share doesn’t cancel a fairness conversation; it makes it easier. This is Pillar V, Notice the quiet incentives: fairness and thanks keep things steady.',
    x: 'Psychologist Sara Algoe’s “find, remind and bind” theory (2012) describes gratitude in close relationships as working three ways: it helps you find people who are good for you, reminds you of them, and binds you closer. Studies of couples found that feeling appreciated predicted more responsiveness and commitment.\nSpecificity matters because it’s evidence of attention. General praise can feel automatic; specific thanks shows the work was actually seen, which is exactly what unbilled debt lacks.\nIn the program it’s structural, not just nice: WP-13 starts with it because it sets the tone, and Check-ins ends with it so a hard talk closes on something that already works.',
    u: '/turning-toward-in-depth.html#thanks', l: 'Turning Toward: say thank you, specifically', lt: 'Why specific thanks works, three ways to phrase it, and the research on gratitude.',
    ctx: { '/workpapers/wp-13-pll-protocol.html': 'In the daily loop, start with the appreciation: it sets the tone for the rest.', '/five-pillars.html': 'In the Five Pillars this is Pillar V: name owners and say specific thanks.', '/library/fairness.html': 'This theme’s point: specific thanks shows effort was actually seen, better than general praise.' } },

  goodnews: { t: 'Celebrating good news', m: ['Active and warm'],
    s: '“I got the job!” “That’s amazing! What did they say? When do you start?” That response, active and warm, may matter as much as how you respond to bad news.',
    f: 'For you, the practical worries can wait until tomorrow. Between people, tonight ask one question that lets them tell the story again. A friend’s baby news deserves more than a thumbs-up.',
    w: 'Four common responses: active and warm (real interest and questions); quiet and warm (“oh, nice,” back to your screen); active and deflating (“isn’t the commute awful?”); quiet and deflating (“did you remember the milk?”). The others are common and usually meant kindly, but only the first was linked to closer relationships.',
    x: 'This comes from Shelly Gable and colleagues’ 2004 research on “capitalization,” sharing good news with others. They called the four styles active-constructive, passive-constructive, active-destructive and passive-destructive, and found active-constructive responses were linked with greater relationship satisfaction, trust and intimacy.\nThe surprise is that ordinary, well-meant responses like “oh nice” or practical concerns don’t build closeness the way enthusiastic ones do. Responding to good news turns out to be as important a skill as supporting bad news.\nThe Conversation Reader watches for good news met with a flat reply, one of the most common ways people drift apart without meaning to.',
    u: '/turning-toward-in-depth.html#good-news', l: 'Turning Toward: celebrate good news', lt: 'The four ways people respond, with examples, and the one linked to closeness.' },

  stresstalk: { t: 'The stress-reducing conversation', m: ['stress-reducing conversation'],
    s: 'After dinner, ten minutes on a difficult manager, while the other just listens: “No wonder you’re fed up.” Then swap. Nobody fixes anything, and both feel lighter.',
    f: 'For you, it gives outside stress somewhere to go other than irritability. Between people, the listener is on the speaker’s side, not defending the boss. For caregivers, it works in both directions.',
    w: 'Take turns, about ten to fifteen minutes each. Don’t problem-solve unless asked: “Do you want ideas, or do you want me to just listen?” Show you understand before anything else. It’s about stress from outside the relationship, not about each other.',
    x: 'The conversation comes from Gottman’s work. Researchers studying dyadic coping (Guy Bodenmann and others) have found that how partners handle each other’s stress together is linked with relationship quality, and that supportive coping helps buffer the stress spillover described by Neff and Karney.\nThe “ideas or just listen?” question addresses a well-known mismatch: unsolicited advice can feel like criticism, while understanding feels like support. Support-matching research points the same way.\nIn the program it pairs with the daily check-in and Today’s Weather, and it’s the social answer to REPORT-01’s “Load isn’t the driver” finding, when stress rises for reasons outside the chore split.',
    u: '/turning-toward-in-depth.html#stress-talk', l: 'Turning Toward: the stress-reducing conversation', lt: 'The rules for the talk, and why outside stress needs somewhere to go.' },

  rituals: { t: 'Rituals of connection', m: ['rituals of connection', 'small rituals'],
    s: 'Two friends in different cities keep a standing Sunday call, twenty minutes. Some weeks light, some heavy. It’s there either way. That’s a ritual of connection.',
    f: 'For you, it doesn’t depend on anyone’s mood, which is the point. Between people: a real hello and goodbye, a weekly walk, “best part of your day?” at dinner, a Friday “what went well” for roommates or coworkers.',
    w: 'Keep them small enough to survive a bad week: a ritual that only happens when things are good isn’t a ritual yet. A tiny one done every day beats a big one done when there’s time.',
    x: 'Family therapist William Doherty wrote about “rituals of connection” in The Intentional Family, arguing that families drift apart by default and stay close by design. Research on family routines and rituals (Barbara Fiese and others) links them with stronger family bonds. The Library adds research by Michael Norton and Francesca Gino suggesting rituals can reduce anxiety and increase enjoyment.\nHabit research explains why small works: a ritual tied to an existing cue (coming home, dinner, Friday) becomes automatic, so it keeps going when motivation dips.\nThe program’s own rhythms are rituals of a kind: the daily loop, the weekly closing, the monthly look-back.',
    u: '/turning-toward-in-depth.html#rituals', l: 'Turning Toward: small rituals of connection', lt: 'Ideas for rituals in every kind of relationship, sized to survive a bad week.' },

  lovemap: { t: 'A love map', m: ['love map'],
    s: '“What’s been stressing you that you haven’t mentioned?” Over tea, the answer is a worry about a parent you hadn’t heard. Now it can be carried together. That’s keeping a love map.',
    f: 'For you, it’s a detailed picture of someone’s inner world: what they’re worried about, looking forward to, and who matters to them right now. Between people, it needs updating, because people change. The listening matters more than the question.',
    w: 'Questions to try every so often: What’s on your mind this week that I don’t know about? Who at work or school do you enjoy right now? Who’s hard? What are you looking forward to? What’s a small thing I do that you like? What are you proud of lately?',
    x: 'Love maps are John Gottman’s term, the first of the “principles” in his book The Seven Principles for Making Marriage Work. His observation was that couples who knew each other’s worlds in detail coped better with stress and change.\nThe broader research idea is perceived partner knowledge: feeling known is part of feeling understood, one of the core parts of perceived responsiveness (Reis and colleagues).\nThe program applies it beyond couples. The questions work for friends, family and coworkers, and the Wiring Card is a kind of love map for how someone takes in words.',
    u: '/turning-toward-in-depth.html#their-world', l: 'Turning Toward: know their world', lt: 'Ten questions to ask every so often, and how to listen to the answers.' },

  fondness: { t: 'Fondness and admiration', m: ['fondness and admiration'],
    s: 'During a tense month, you tell a friend how patient your partner was with a tricky neighbor. The friend mentions it later. Something softens. That’s fondness and admiration at work.',
    f: 'For you, in a hard stretch attention narrows to what’s going wrong; remembering the good widens it again. Between people, once a week, name one quality you admire and one moment you saw it.',
    w: 'Three small practices: name one quality and one moment each week; tell someone else something good about them and let it get back to them; remember how you met, or a hard time you got through together. It guards against contempt.',
    x: 'Gottman calls fondness and admiration the antidote to contempt, the conflict pattern his research found most damaging. His lab found that couples who kept a positive view of each other, even while disagreeing, did better over time.\nPsychologists also describe a negativity bias: bad events tend to weigh more in memory and attention than good ones of the same size (Roy Baumeister and colleagues, “Bad is stronger than good”). Deliberately noticing the good is a counterweight.\nThe Stories page makes the same point from philosophy: Buber’s I and Thou warns against reducing a person to a role or a number, and fondness is how you keep seeing the whole person.',
    u: '/turning-toward-in-depth.html#fondness', l: 'Turning Toward: remember what you like about them', lt: 'Three small practices, and why they guard against contempt.' },

  /* ---------- Stories and calm places ---------- */

  stories: { t: 'Stories from Philosophy', m: ['Stories from Philosophy', 'Second Arrow', 'Ship of Theseus', 'Blind Men and the Elephant', 'Empty Boat', 'Ring of Gyges'],
    s: 'Something stings. You write two lines: “What happened” and “What I’m telling myself it means.” Only the first belongs in the ledger. That’s the Second Arrow, a 2,500-year-old idea.',
    f: 'For you, twelve old stories act as honest mirrors. Between people, they give you shared shorthand: “Is this an empty boat?” can stop a fight about intent before it starts.',
    w: 'Four parts: knowing yourself (the Second Arrow, the Ship of Theseus, What Is Up to Us); seeing the other person (the Cave, the Blind Men and the Elephant, the Joy of Fish, the Empty Boat); living together (the Porcupines, Three Kinds of Friendship, the Circles of Hierocles); and the ethics of keeping a ledger (the Ring of Gyges, Buber’s I and Thou). Ideas to think with, not proofs.',
    x: 'The sources span traditions: the Second Arrow from an early Buddhist text (the Sallatha Sutta); the Ship of Theseus from Plutarch; What Is Up to Us from the Stoic Epictetus; the Cave and the Ring of Gyges from Plato’s Republic; the Blind Men and the Elephant from South Asian tradition; the Joy of Fish and the Empty Boat from the Daoist Zhuangzi; the Porcupines from Schopenhauer; Three Kinds of Friendship from Aristotle; the Circles from the Stoic Hierocles; and I and Thou from Martin Buber (1923).\nEach links to a program tool. The Empty Boat (you wouldn’t be angry at an empty boat that bumped you) pairs with the filter step’s neutral reading; the Ship of Theseus with treaty amendments for a life that has changed.\nThe ethics part is a guardrail for the whole site: the moment the numbers become the person, as in “you’re a 0.4,” the ledger has failed.',
    u: '/learn/index-in-depth.html#intro', l: 'Stories from Philosophy', lt: 'Twelve old stories retold in plain words, each with where it lives in the program and one thing to try.' },

  garden: { t: 'The Night Garden', m: ['Night Garden'],
    s: 'Five minutes breathing with a glowing light before a planned talk, and the first sentence comes out softer. The Night Garden is a quiet place to breathe, play and settle.',
    f: 'For you, three gentle things to do: box breathing with a glowing light, connecting fireflies into pictures, and a slow lily-pond puzzle. Between people, it’s a good settling step before a check-in, or winding down after one.',
    w: 'No timers and no way to lose. The garden grows as you visit: flowers you bloom stay, the moon follows the real one, and seasons change with the calendar. Missing a day costs nothing, and everything is kept only on your device. It isn’t treatment. This is Pillar III, Read your state first.',
    x: 'The page explains its reasoning: slow, steady breathing settles the body; relaxed, casual play has been linked with better mood and lower stress; fitting shapes together uses the same mental room that worry loops use; and gently moving light, water and leaves lets tired attention rest.\nThat last idea echoes attention restoration theory (Rachel and Stephen Kaplan), which proposes that gently engaging surroundings, like nature, give focused attention a chance to recover.\nIts design choices are deliberate: no timers, no losing, no streaks to break. The aim is a pause you want to come back to, not a game that pulls at you.',
    u: '/night-garden.html', l: 'Step into the Night Garden', lt: 'Breathe with a glowing light, connect the fireflies, or float lily pads on the pond.' },

  petals: { t: 'Pause & Play levels', m: ['every few levels', 'Pause & Play'],
    s: 'One short Word Bloom level before starting dinner after a hard afternoon. A tiny thing, and it marks a real pause. That’s what the calm games are for.',
    f: 'For you, the games count the levels you finish, and every few levels something new and calm appears in the garden behind every page: a swing, koi, a little bridge. Between people, a few minutes of play is a lovely way to settle before a hard talk.',
    w: 'It’s a gentle nudge to take calm breaks, not a score to chase. There are no timers and no way to lose. The point isn’t to collect as much as possible; it’s to make a pause feel worth coming back to.',
    x: 'Research on casual games has linked short sessions with improved mood and lower stress in some studies, though results vary and the program makes no health claims. The key design elements are low stakes, no time pressure and a clear, small task that fits in a few minutes.\nThe games sit under Pillar III: they’re a way to change your state before you talk, the play version of regulate first, negotiate second.\nThe surprise items arrive at slightly uneven intervals, so there’s no fixed target to grind toward, and nothing is lost by stopping.',
    u: '/pause-and-play.html', l: 'Open Pause & Play', lt: 'All the calm games in one place, and what grows in the garden as you play.' },

  soundscapes: { t: 'The Soundscape Catalog', m: ['Soundscape Catalog'],
    s: 'Soft rain playing while you fill in the weekly log makes it feel a little more like a quiet ritual and a little less like paperwork. That’s the Soundscape Catalog’s job.',
    f: 'For you, it’s optional, always: no workpaper requires audio. Between people, some find a shared soundscape a gentle start to a calm evening together.',
    w: 'It holds finished ambient pieces, starting with The Breath Beneath, plus live soundscapes made in your browser as you listen, so they never repeat: Deep, Ocean, Soft rain and Singing bowls. Headphones help. It grew from the rule to regulate first and negotiate second.',
    x: 'Research on music and sound for relaxation is mixed but generally suggests that slow, low, predictable sound can help some people feel calmer, and that personal preference matters a lot. The catalog is honest about this: these are ambient pieces, not a validated therapeutic or clinical tool.\nThe live soundscapes are generated in real time, a technique called procedural or generative audio, which is why they never loop. Gentle left-to-right movement and deep tones are part of what many listeners find settling, and they come through best on headphones.\nThe program’s rule applies: a practice that becomes another obligation has already stopped working, so use it only if it helps you.',
    u: '/soundscapes.html', l: 'Open the Soundscape Catalog', lt: 'Finished ambient pieces and live soundscapes that never repeat.' }
};
